      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 553,233,942 and 645,157,245. Hmm, I need to add them digit by digit starting from the rightmost side. Let me write them down to visualize better.\n\nFirst, aligning the numbers:\n\n        553,233,942\n      + 645,157,245\n      --------------\n\nStarting with the ones place. The rightmost digits are 2 and 5. Adding those gives 7. So the ones place is 7.\n\nNext, the tens place: 4 and 4. That's 8. Tens digit is 8.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 9 and 2. 9+2=11. Oh, wait, that's 11. So I write down 1 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNow the thousands place: 3 (from first number) and 7 (second number) plus the carried 1. 3+7=10, plus 1 is 11. So again, write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 and 5. 3+5=8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. No carryover here.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 and 1. 2+1=3. So that's 3.\n\nMillions place: 5 (from first number) and 5 (second number). 5+5=10. So write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place: 3 and 4. 3+4=7 plus the carried 1 is 8.\n\nHundred-millions place: 5 and 6. 5+6=11. Wait, so here we have 11. That means we write 1 and carry over 1 again.\n\nNow the billions place: Since the first number has a 5 in the next position (billions) and the second has a 6? Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 553,233,942 is 9 digits: 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), etc. The second number is 645,157,245 which is also 9 digits: 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), etc. Wait, so when adding the hundred millions place, it's 5 + 6? Wait, no, let me recount the places properly.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write each digit position step by step carefully.\n\nLet me break down each number digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 553,233,942\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (since addition is done right to left):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 3\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 5\n\nSecond number: 645,157,245\n\nBreaking down similarly:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition 7 (millions):4\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):6\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):6\n\nWait, so when adding, let's go step by step:\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):\n\n2 +5 =7. Write 7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4+4=8. Write 8.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+2=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+7=10, plus carryover 1 is 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8 +1=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2+1=3. Write 3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5+4=9. Write 9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3+6=9. Write 9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11. Here, since it's the highest digit, we have to write both digits. So 1 and 1. Wait, but that would make it 11 in the hundred millions place? Wait, actually, when adding two 9-digit numbers, the sum can be up to 1,199,999,998 (if both are 999,999,999). So adding 553,233,942 and 645,157,245, their sum might be a 10-digit number. So when adding the hundred millions place (position9), which is 5+6=11, so we have to carryover 1 to a new tenth digit (billions place).\n\nWait, so let me correct that:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11. So write down 1 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nThen, since there's a carryover, we have an additional digit at the beginning: 1.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPositions 1-9:\n\nAfter each step:\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:8\n\n3:1 (with carryover 1)\n\n4:1 (with carryover 1)\n\n5:9\n\n6:3\n\n7:9\n\n8:9\n\n9:1 (with carryover 1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 becomes the next digit (position10: billions place:1).\n\nSo the total sum is 1 (billions) followed by the digits from position9 to 1:\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1):\n\nDigits collected so far (from right to left):\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition5:9\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:1 (with a carryover 1 to position10)\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 in position10.\n\nSo the digits from position10 to position1 are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition5:9\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition1:7\n\nWait, that might be confusing. Let me write them in order from left to right (billions to ones):\n\nThe highest digit is position10 (billions):1\n\nThen position9 (hundred millions):1\n\nposition8 (ten millions):9\n\nposition7 (millions):9\n\nposition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nposition5 (ten thousands):9\n\nposition4 (thousands):1\n\nposition3 (hundreds):1\n\nposition2 (tens):8\n\nposition1 (ones):7\n\nSo putting all together:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, position8 is ten millions, which was originally 3 (from first number) and 6 (second number) added to 9, so that was correct. Hmm, maybe I need to reconstruct the number properly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to write the sum step by step as we go through each digit, keeping track of the carryover.\n\nLet me try again:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 2 +5 =7. No carryover. So first digit (rightmost) is 7.\n\nDigit 2 (tens):4 +4=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):9 +2=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):3 +7=10 +1 (carryover)=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):3 +5=8 +1=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):2 +1=3. Write 3.\n\nDigit7 (millions):5 +4=9. Write 9.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3 +6=9. Write 9.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):5 +6=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we add a new digit to the left (billions place):1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nPutting that together: 1,199,391,877?\n\nWait, let me check the digits again step by step:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 5+6=11, so the 1 in the hundred millions and carryover 1 to billions)\n\nWait, actually, the hundred millions place was 5 +6 =11, so the digit in the hundred millions is 1, and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since there were no digits beyond hundred millions in the original numbers, the carryover becomes the billions digit. So the billions digit is 1, then hundred millions is 1, then ten millions is 9 (from digit8: 3+6=9?), wait let me check digit8 again.\n\nWait digit8 is ten millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3 (since 553,233,942: the digits are 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions?), wait wait, maybe I messed up the digit positions earlier.\n\nHold on, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me clarify the place values properly.\n\nLet me write the first number: 553,233,942.\n\nBreaking down each digit with place values:\n\nStarting from the right (position1):\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):5\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5\n\nWait, that's correct. So the ten millions digit is 3, and hundred millions is 5.\n\nSecond number:645,157,245.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nPosition1 (ones):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSo when adding the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3, second is 6. 3+6=9. So that's correct. So the ten millions digit is 9, no carryover there.\n\nThen the hundred millions place (position9):5+6=11. So write 1, carryover 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nBillions place:1 (from carryover)\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 5+6=11)\n\nTen millions:9 (from 3+6)\n\nMillions:5 (first number) +4 (second number) =9. Wait, position7 is millions place:\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 5, second is 4. 5+4=9. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands place (position6):2+1=3. Correct.\n\nTen thousands (position5):3+5=8? Wait, no, earlier I had 3 (first number) +5 (second) plus carryover? Wait, let me go step by step again.\n\nWait, let me retrace each digit with the carryover properly:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPosition1 (ones):2+5=7. No carryover. Digit1:7\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+4=8. Digit2:8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+2=11. Digit3:1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+7=10 +1 (carryover)=11. Digit4:1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8 +1 (carryover)=9. Digit5:9, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2+1=3. Digit6:3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5+4=9. Digit7:9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3+6=9. Digit8:9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11. Digit9:1, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from position9 becomes the next digit (billions place: position10). So digit10:1.\n\nSo the digits from position10 to position1 are:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),9 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),1 (hundreds),8 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nWait, but when we list them in order from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo combining these digits:\n\n1,1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write this as a number:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:1 \u2192 so first two digits are 11\n\nThen ten millions:9 \u2192 119\n\nMillions:9 \u2192 1,199\n\nHundred thousands:3 \u2192 1,199,3\n\nTen thousands:9 \u2192 1,199,39\n\nThousands:1 \u2192 1,199,391\n\nHundreds:1 \u2192 1,199,3911?\n\nWait, maybe I need to separate the commas properly.\n\nWait, let's construct the number step by step:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nDigit10 (billions):1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):1 \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,100,000,000\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):9 \u2192 90,000,000 \u2192 total 1,190,000,000\n\nDigit7 (millions):9 \u2192 9,000,000 \u2192 total 1,199,000,000\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):3 \u2192 300,000 \u2192 total 1,199,300,000\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):9 \u2192 90,000 \u2192 total 1,199,390,000\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1 \u2192 1,000 \u2192 total 1,199,391,000\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):1 \u2192 100 \u2192 total 1,199,391,100\n\nDigit2 (tens):8 \u2192 80 \u2192 total 1,199,391,180\n\nDigit1 (ones):7 \u2192 7 \u2192 total 1,199,391,187.\n\nWait, but that doesn't seem right. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the thousands place.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nAfter digit7 (millions place is 9), digit6 is hundred thousands:3 \u2192 so up to millions place: 1,199,3 (million). Then ten thousands is 9 \u2192 1,199,39 (ten thousands?), but maybe I need to think in terms of each digit's place value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to write the number as:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo writing with commas:\n\n1,199,391,187.\n\nWait, let's check:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nHundred millions:1 \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total 1,100,000,000\n\nTen millions:9 \u2192 90,000,000 \u2192 1,190,000,000\n\nMillions:9 \u2192 9,000,000 \u2192 1,199,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:3 \u2192 300,000 \u2192 1,199,300,000\n\nTen thousands:9 \u2192 90,000 \u2192 1,199,390,000\n\nThousands:1 \u2192 1,000 \u2192 1,199,391,000\n\nHundreds:1 \u2192 100 \u2192 1,199,391,100\n\nTens:8 \u2192 80 \u2192 1,199,391,180\n\nOnes:7 \u2192 7 \u2192 1,199,391,187.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the total sum is 1,199,391,187.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n2 +5 =7\n\n4+4=8\n\n9+2=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1\n\n3+7=10 +1=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\n3+5=8 +1=9\n\n2+1=3\n\n5+4=9\n\n3+6=9\n\n5+6=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 at the end gives the 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the result is 1,199,391,187. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers in parts:\n\n553,233,942 + 645,157,245\n\nBreak into millions:\n\n553,233,942 = 553,000,000 + 233,942\n\n645,157,245 = 645,000,000 + 157,245\n\nAdding the millions parts: 553,000,000 + 645,000,000 = 1,198,000,000\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 233,942 + 157,245\n\n233,942 + 157,245:\n\n233,942 + 100,000 = 333,942\n\n333,942 +57,245 = 391,187\n\nSo total sum: 1,198,000,000 + 391,187 = 1,198,391,187? Wait, that contradicts the previous result. Wait, no, wait:\n\nWait, 233,942 +157,245:\n\nLet me compute that again:\n\n233,942\n\n+157,245\n\nAdding:\n\nUnits:2+5=7\n\nTens:4+4=8\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 \u2192 1 carryover\n\nThousands:3+7=10 +1=11 \u2192 1 carryover\n\nTen thousands:3+5=8 +1=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+1=3\n\nMillions:0 (since 233,942 is up to hundred thousands place as 2) +1 (from 157,245's hundred thousands digit 1) \u2192 but wait, 157,245 is 1 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), etc.\n\nWait, 233,942 is 2 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), etc.\n\nAdding the hundred thousands: 2 +1 =3\n\nTen thousands:3 +5=8\n\nThousands:3 +7=10 \u2192 so that's where the carryover comes from.\n\nWait, let's do it step by step:\n\n233,942\n\n+157,245\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:2+5=7\n\nTens:4+4=8\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1\n\nThousands:3 (from 233,942's thousands digit is 3) +7 (from 157,245's thousands digit is 7) \u2192 3+7=10 +1=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3 (from 233,942's ten thousands digit is 3) +5 (from 157,245's ten thousands digit is5) \u2192 3+5=8 +1=9\n\nHundred thousands:2 (from 233,942) +1 (from 157,245) \u2192 3\n\nSo the total is 391,187.\n\nTherefore, adding to the millions parts:\n\n553M +645M = 1,198,000,000\n\nPlus 391,187 gives 1,198,391,187.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous total of 1,199,391,187. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me check the breakdown again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number:553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:2+5=7\n\nTens:4+4=8\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\nThousands:3 (from first) +7 (second) =10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3 (first) +5 (second)=8 +1=9\n\nHundred thousands:2 (first) +1 (second)=3\n\nMillions:5 (first) +4 (second)=9\n\nTen millions:3 (first) +6 (second)=9\n\nHundred millions:5 (first) +6 (second)=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1,199,391,187.\n\nWait, but when I broke into millions, I had 553M +645M =1,198M, then adding 233,942 +157,245=391,187 \u2192 total 1,198,391,187. But that's different.\n\nAh! Wait, the breakdown was wrong. Because 553,233,942 is 553 million plus 233,942. But 553 million is 553,000,000. Adding 233,942 gives 553,233,942. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, 645,157,245 is 645,000,000 +157,245. Correct.\n\nSo adding the millions parts:553,000,000 +645,000,000 =1,198,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:233,942 +157,245 = 391,187.\n\nTotal sum:1,198,000,000 +391,187 =1,198,391,187.\n\nBut this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,199,391,187. There's a mistake here.\n\nWait, so which is correct?\n\nWait, let me compute 553,233,942 +645,157,245 using another method.\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\nFirst number:553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with the first number:553,233,942\n\nAdd 600,000,000 \u2192 553,233,942 +600,000,000 =1,153,233,942\n\nThen add 45,000,000 \u2192 1,153,233,942 +45,000,000 =1,198,233,942\n\nThen add 157,245 \u2192 1,198,233,942 +157,245.\n\nCompute 1,198,233,942 +100,000 =1,198,333,942\n\nThen +57,245 \u2192 1,198,333,942 +57,245 =1,198,391,187.\n\nAh! So that gives 1,198,391,187. But the digit-by-digit method gave 1,199,391,187. There's a discrepancy of 1 million.\n\nHmm, so where is the error?\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nLet me re-examine the digit positions again carefully.\n\nFirst number:553,233,942\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (position1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:2 (ones)\n\nPosition2:4 (tens)\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:5 (millions)\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:4 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition7 (millions):5 (first) +4 (second) =9 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3 (first) +6 (second)=9 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1.\n\nThus, the hundred millions digit is 1, and carryover to billions.\n\nSo the billions digit is 1.\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo the number is 1,199,391,187.\n\nWait, but according to the other method, it's 1,198,391,187. So there's a difference in the hundred millions place.\n\nWait, in the breakdown method, the hundred millions place comes from 5 (first number) +6 (second number) =11, so the hundred millions digit is 1, and carryover 1 to billions. So the hundred millions digit is 1, but in the breakdown method, when adding 553M +645M, that's 553+645=1,198 million. Wait, but 553 million is 553,000,000, which has 5 in hundred millions (since 553,000,000 is 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (millions)), so the hundred millions digit is 5. The second number's hundred millions digit is 6. So 5+6=11, so the hundred millions digit is 1, and carryover 1 to billions. So the total hundred millions digit is 1, but in the breakdown method, when adding 553M +645M, that's 1,198,000,000, which is 1 (billion), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (millions). Wait, that's where the mistake is.\n\nWait, 553,000,000 +645,000,000 = (553 +645)*1,000,000 = 1,198,000,000. So that's correct. But 1,198,000,000 is written as 1,198,000,000. Breaking down:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1 (since 1,198,000,000 is 1 billion, 1 hundred million, 9 ten millions, 8 millions). Wait, no:\n\nWait, 1,198,000,000 is:\n\n1 (billion), 1 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 8 (million). So the hundred millions digit is 1, ten millions is 9, millions is 8.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, the millions digit was 9 (from 5+4). Wait, so there's a conflict here.\n\nAh! Here's the problem. In the breakdown method, when adding 553,000,000 (which has 5 in hundred millions, 5 in ten millions, 3 in millions) and 645,000,000 (6 in hundred millions, 4 in ten millions, 5 in millions):\n\nAdding the millions digits:3 (from first) +5 (from second) =8 \u2192 that's why the millions digit in the breakdown is 8.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, the millions digit was 5 (from first number) +4 (second number) =9. Wait, why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Because in the first number, the millions digit is 5, but in the breakdown, I considered 553,000,000 as having 3 in millions. Wait, that's the mistake.\n\nWait, the first number is 553,233,942. Breaking down:\n\n553,233,942:\n\nThe digits are:\n\nHundred millions:5\n\nTen millions:5\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:2.\n\nWait, so the millions digit is 3 (the third digit from the left in the millions place). Wait, no, let's parse it properly.\n\nWait, 553,233,942:\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n553,233,942 \u2192 553 (million), 233 (thousand), 942.\n\nSo the millions part is 553, which is 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (millions). So the millions digit is 3.\n\nSimilarly, the second number:645,157,245 \u2192 645 million, 157 thousand, 245.\n\nSo the millions part is 645 \u2192 6 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),5 (millions). So the millions digit is5.\n\nTherefore, when adding the millions digits (the millions place, which is the third digit from the right in the millions group):\n\nWait, in digit-by-digit addition, the millions place is position7 (since positions are from the right):\n\nFirst number's position7 (millions digit):5?\n\nWait, no, confusion arises from different ways of counting.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nThe first number is 553,233,942.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (position1):\n\nPosition1:2 (ones)\n\nPosition2:4 (tens)\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\