umber. The millions digit is position7 (third from the left), which is 3, not 5. So my initial digit-by-digit breakdown was incorrect.\n\nThis explains the discrepancy. Let me start over with the correct digit positions.\n\nCorrect digit breakdown for first number (553,233,942):\n\nFrom right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5\n\nSecond number (645,157,245):\n\nPosition1 (ones):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nNow, let's redo the addition with correct digit positions:\n\nStarting from position1 (ones):\n\n1. Ones:2 +5 =7. Write 7.\n\n2. Tens:4 +4=8. Write8.\n\n3. Hundreds:9 +2=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:3 (first) +7 (second)=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:3 (first) +5 (second)=8 +1=9. Write9.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:2 (first) +1 (second)=3. Write3.\n\n7. Millions:3 (first) +4 (second)=7. Write7.\n\n8. Ten millions:5 (first) +6 (second)=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:5 (first) +6 (second)=11 +1 (carryover)=12. Wait, no:\n\nWait, position9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is5, second is6. So 5+6=11. Plus any carryover from the previous step?\n\nWait, the previous step was ten millions place (position8):\n\nPosition8:5 (first) +6 (second)=11. So we write1 and carryover1 to the hundred millions place.\n\nSo for position9 (hundred millions):\n\n5+6=11 plus the carryover1 from position8 gives 12. So write2, carryover1.\n\nWait, no, wait:\n\nWait, position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is5, second is6. 5+6=11. So we write1 in the ten millions place and carryover1 to the hundred millions place.\n\nThen, position9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst digit5 + second digit6 =11, plus the carryover1 from ten millions place: total 12.\n\nSo we write2 in the hundred millions place and carryover1 to the billions place.\n\nThen, the billions place gets the carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:1 (from position8's result)\n\nMillions:7 (from position7)\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1 (from position4's result)\n\nHundreds:1 (from position3's result)\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nPutting it all together from left to right:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nBreaking this down with commas:\n\n1,217,391,187.\n\nWait, let's verify:\n\nBillions:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nHundred millions:2 \u2192 200,000,000 \u2192 total 1,200,000,000\n\nTen millions:1 \u2192 10,000,000 \u2192 1,210,000,000\n\nMillions:7 \u2192 7,000,000 \u2192 1,217,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:3 \u2192 300,000 \u2192 1,217,300,000\n\nTen thousands:9 \u2192 90,000 \u2192 1,217,390,000\n\nThousands:1 \u2192 1,000 \u2192 1,217,391,000\n\nHundreds:1 \u2192 100 \u2192 1,217,391,100\n\nTens:8 \u2192 80 \u2192 1,217,391,180\n\nOnes:7 \u2192 7 \u2192 1,217,391,187.\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown method earlier, adding 553,233,942 +645,157,245:\n\n553,233,942 +645,157,245.\n\nLet me compute this directly:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with the first number:553,233,942\n\nAdd 600,000,000 \u2192 1,153,233,942\n\nAdd 45,000,000 \u2192 1,198,233,942\n\nAdd 157,245 \u2192 1,198,233,942 +157,245 = ?\n\n1,198,233,942 +100,000 =1,198,333,942\n\n+57,245 \u2192 1,198,333,942 +57,245 =1,198,391,187.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the corrected digit-by-digit result of 1,217,391,187.\n\nThere's a big discrepancy here. Clearly, I'm making a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let me use another approach. Let me add the two numbers digit by digit correctly now that I have the correct digit positions.\n\nFirst number:553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding digit by digit from right to left with correct positions:\n\nPosition1 (ones):2+5=7\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+4=8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+2=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+7=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8 +1=9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2+1=3\n\nPosition7 (millions):3+4=7\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5+6=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11 +1 (carryover)=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nThus, the number is 1,217,391,187.\n\nBut when adding via the breakdown method, I get 1,198,391,187. This inconsistency must be resolved.\n\nWait, let me compute the two numbers numerically:\n\nFirst number:553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding them:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 553,233,942\n\nAdd 600,000,000 \u2192 1,153,233,942\n\nAdd 45,000,000 \u2192 1,153,233,942 +45,000,000 =1,198,233,942\n\nNow add the remaining 157,245:\n\n1,198,233,942 +157,245:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,198,233,942 +100,000 =1,198,333,942\n\n1,198,333,942 +50,000 =1,198,383,942\n\n1,198,383,942 +7,245 =1,198,391,187.\n\nSo total is 1,198,391,187.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition with correct positions, it's 1,217,391,187. There's a difference of 19 million.\n\nAh! The mistake must be in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let's