s), position2 was6 (tens), position1 was5 (ones).\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nThe result digits from each position (right to left):\n\nPositions:\n\n1:5\n\n2:6\n\n3:0 (with carryover)\n\n4:3 (with carryover)\n\n5:2 (with carryover)\n\n6:5 (with carryover)\n\n7:4 (with carryover)\n\n8:3 (with carryover)\n\n9:4 (with carryover)\n\n10:1 (the final carryover)\n\nSo when written in order from left to right (starting from the highest place, which is position10):\n\nDigit10:1 (billions place)\n\nDigit9:4 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit8:3 (ten millions)\n\nDigit7:4 (millions)\n\nDigit6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:2 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit4:3 (thousands)\n\nDigit3:0 (hundreds)\n\nDigit2:6 (tens)\n\nDigit1:5 (ones)\n\nSo putting them all together:\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,434,523,065?\n\nWait let me write it with commas:\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n1 (billions), then 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), so that's 1,434 (millions part). Then the next three digits: 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands) \u2192 523 (thousands). Then the last three: 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens),5 (ones) \u2192 065. So the full number is 1,434,523,065.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n637,836,865\n\n+796,686,200\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the millions:\n\n637,836,865 is 637,836,865\n\n796,686,200 is 796,686,200\n\nAdding the millions parts:\n\n637,000,000 + 796,000,000 = 1,433,000,000\n\nThen the thousands parts:\n\n836,000 + 686,000 = 1,522,000\n\nThen the last parts:\n\n865 + 200 = 1,065\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,433,000,000 + 1,522,000 = 1,434,522,000\n\nThen add 1,065: 1,434,522,000 + 1,065 = 1,434,523,065.\n\nYes, that matches. So