      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 620,684,370 and 973,329,535. Hmm, adding such large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Let me visualize it:\n\n      620684370\n    + 973329535\n    ------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 0 in the ones place, and the second has a 5. Adding those gives 0 + 5 = 5. So the ones digit of the sum is 5.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 7 and the second has a 3. 7 + 3 is 10. Wait, that's 10, so I write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 3 and the second has a 5. Adding those gives 3 + 5 = 8, plus the carried over 1 makes 9. So the hundreds digit is 9, no carryover here.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 4 and the second has a 9. 4 + 9 is 13. So I write 3 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: first number has an 8, second has a 2. 8 + 2 = 10, plus the carried 1 makes 11. So write 1 and carry over 1 again.\n\nHundred-thousands place: first number is 6, second is 3. 6 + 3 = 9, plus the carried 1 is 10. So another 0 here with a carryover of 1.\n\nMillions place: first number is 0 (since 620,684,370 has a 0 in the millions place?), wait let me check the digits again. Wait, maybe I miscounted the places. Let me recount the digits properly.\n\nWait, 620,684,370 is broken down as:\n\nDigits from right: 0 (ones), 7 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (hundred millions).\n\nAnd 973,329,535 is:\n\n5 (ones), 3 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 9 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe I should write them out digit by digit to avoid confusion. Let's list each digit position:\n\nStarting from the right (ones