ion4:0\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:8\n\nPosition8:5\n\nPosition9:5 (from 6+9=15, but with the carryover from position9? Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, Position9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 6, second is 9. 6+9=15. So we write down 5 and carryover 1. Since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit to the left. So the total digits would be 1 followed by the 5 from position9, then the rest.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position9 plus the carryover:\n\nThe result digits would be:\n\nCarryover 1, then the digits from position9 to 1:\n\nWait, actually, when writing the sum, the carryover from the highest position (position9) adds a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum would be:\n\n1 (from carryover) followed by the digits from each position:\n\nPosition9:5 (since 6+9=15, so 5 with carryover 1)\n\nPosition8:2+3=5, but wait, let me retrace:\n\nWait, let me list all the results step by step:\n\nLet me tabulate each position with their sum and carryover:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\nSum: 0 +5 =5. Carryover 0. Digit:5\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n7+3=10. Carryover 1. Digit:0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n3+9=12 +1=13. Carryover1. Digit:3\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n4+5=9 +1=10. Carryover1. Digit:0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n8+2=10 +1=11. Carryover1. Digit:1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n6+3=9 +1=10. Carryover1. Digit:0\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n0+7=7 +1=8. Carryover0. Digit:8\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n2+3=5. Carryover0. Digit:5\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6+9=15. Carryover1. Digit:5\n\nNow, after Position9, we have a carryover of 1, so that becomes the next digit (position10).\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position10 (starting from the right) are:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:3\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:8\n\nPosition8:5\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPosition10:1\n\nSo writing them from left to right (starting from the highest position):\n\nPosition10 (1), Position9 (5), Position8 (5), Position7 (8), Position6 (0), Position5 (1), Position4 (0), Position3 (3), Position2 (0), Position1 (5).\n\nWait, that would be:\n\n1 (position10), 5 (position9), 5 (position8), 8 (position7), 0 (position6), 1 (position5), 0 (position4), 3 (position3), 0 (position2), 5 (position1).\n\nPutting them all together: 1 5 5 8 0 1 0 3 0 5.\n\nWait, let me write that as a number:\n\n1,558,010,305?\n\nWait, let me check again:\n\nWait, the digits from left to right (starting with position10):\n\n1 (billions place?), then 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,558,010,305?\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370\n\nWait, no, that's not the right approach. Alternatively, maybe use calculator-like addition:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\nAdding the two:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n0+5=5\n\n7+3=10 (carry 1)\n\n3+9+1=13 (carry 1)\n\n4+5+1=10 (carry1)\n\n8+2+1=11 (carry1)\n\n6+3+1=10 (carry1)\n\n0+7+1=8 (no carry)\n\n2+3=5\n\n6+9=15 (carry1)\n\nSo the final digits with the carryover at the end give 1 followed by 5, then the rest as above.\n\nSo the total is 1,594,013,905? Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me do it step by step again.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write down each digit's sum properly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers in parts:\n\n620,684,370 + 973,329,535\n\nBreak it into millions, thousands, etc.\n\nAlternatively, let me add the two numbers:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\nAdding digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 0 +5 =5\n\nTens:7+3=10 \u2192 0, carryover1\n\nHundreds:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nThousands:4+5=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:6+3=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nMillions:0+7=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carryover\n\nTen millions:2+3=5 \u21925\n\nHundred millions:6+9=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nThen the final carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n5 (ones),0 (tens),3 (hundreds),0 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),8 (millions),5 (ten millions),5 (hundred millions), and the carryover 1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,558,010,305.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method. Let's add 620,684,370 + 973,329,535.\n\nLet me compute 620,684,370 + 973,329,535:\n\nFirst, 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370\n\nThen add the remaining 73,329,535:\n\n1,520,684,370 + 73,329,535 = ?\n\n1,520,684,370 + 70,000,000 = 1,590,684,370\n\nThen add 3,329,535:\n\n1,590,684,370 + 3,329,535 = 1,594,013,905.\n\nWait, that's conflicting with the previous result. Hmm, so there must be an error in my digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, so which is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the second method again:\n\n620,684,370 + 973,329,535.\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n0 +5 =5\n\n7 +3 =10 \u2192 0 carryover1\n\n3 +9 =12 +1=13 \u21923 carryover1\n\n4 +5 =9 +1=10 \u21920 carryover1\n\n8 +2 =10 +1=11 \u21921 carryover1\n\n6 +3 =9 +1=10 \u21920 carryover1\n\n0 +7 =7 +1=8 \u21928\n\n2 +3 =5\n\n6 +9 =15 \u21925 carryover1\n\nThen the final carryover1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n5,0,3,0,1,0,8,5,5 and then 1.\n\nSo writing left to right, starting with the 1, then 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo that's 1,558,010,305.\n\nBut when I did the other method, adding 620,684,370 + 973,329,535:\n\nLet me compute 620,684,370 + 973,329,535:\n\nFirst, 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370\n\nThen adding 73,329,535 to that:\n\n1,520,684,370 + 73,329,535:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,520,684,370 + 70,000,000 = 1,590,684,370\n\nThen add 3,329,535:\n\n1,590,684,370 + 3,000,000 = 1,593,684,370\n\nThen +329,535:\n\n1,593,684,370 + 329,535 = 1,594,013,905.\n\nHmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's do the digit-by-digit again step by step:\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst number: 620,684,370\n\nSecond number: 973,329,535\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n        6 2 0 6 8 4 3 7 0\n\n      + 9 7 3 3 2 9 5 3 5\n\n      --------------------\n\nStarting from the right (units place):\n\nPosition 1 (units):\n\n0 +5 =5. Write 5. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n7 +3 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n3 +9 =12 +1 (carry) =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n4 +5 =9 +1 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n8 +2 =10 +1 =11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n6 +3 =9 +1 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n0 +7 =7 +1 =8. Write8, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n2 +3 =5. Write5, carryover0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6 +9 =15. Write5, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions place becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n5 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 0 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),8 (millions),5 (ten millions),5 (hundred millions), and the 1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),8 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),3 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (units).\n\nThus the number is 1,558,010,305.\n\nBut when I did the other method, I got 1,594,013,905. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me compute using another approach. Let's add the two numbers digit by digit again but in a different way.\n\nFirst number: 620,684,370\n\nSecond number: 973,329,535\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +5 =5\n\n7 +3 =10 \u2192 0, carryover1\n\n3 +9 =12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\n4 +5 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\n8 +2 =10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\n6 +3 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\n0 +7 =7 +1=8 \u21928\n\n2 +3 =5 \u21925\n\n6 +9 =15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nThen the final carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),8 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),3 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (units).\n\nSo 1,558,010,305.\n\nBut when I did the other method, adding 620,684,370 + 973,329,535:\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in that method. Let me recalculate:\n\n620,684,370 + 973,329,535.\n\nLet me add 620,684,370 + 973,329,535:\n\nFirst, 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370.\n\nThen adding the remaining 73,329,535:\n\n1,520,684,370 + 73,329,535.\n\nLet me compute 1,520,684,370 + 70,000,000 = 1,590,684,370.\n\nThen add 3,329,535:\n\n1,590,684,370 + 3,329,535.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,590,684,370 + 3,000,000 = 1,593,684,370.\n\nThen add 329,535:\n\n1,593,684,370 + 329,535 = ?\n\n1,593,684,370 + 300,000 = 1,593,984,370\n\nThen +29,535:\n\n1,593,984,370 + 29,535 = 1,594,013,905.\n\nHmm, so this gives a different result. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check the positions again.\n\nWait, let me write both numbers with commas to see their place values:\n\nFirst number: 620,684,370\n\nThis is 6 hundred millions, 2 ten millions, 0 millions, 6 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 4 thousands, 3 hundreds, 7 tens,