 would be 802,116,001. But in the first method, the digit-by-digit gives 802,116,010. The difference is in the ones place: 1 vs 0? Wait no, the first method's ones place is 1, which matches the second method's ones place (the total is 802,116,001 has a 1 in the ones place). Wait, but according to the digit-by-digit, the digits are:\n\nFrom highest to lowest (hundred millions to ones):\n\n8 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So that's 802,116,010. But the second method gives 802,116,001. Wait, so which is correct?\n\nWait, let me do the addition directly:\n\n697,954,550\n\n+104,161,451\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 697,954,550\n\nAdd 100,000,000 \u2192 797,954,550\n\nAdd 4,161,451 \u2192 797,954,550 +4,161,451.\n\nWait, 797,954,550 +4,000,000 = 801,954,550\n\nThen add 161,451: 801,954,550 +161,451 = ?\n\n801,954,550 + 100,000 = 802,054,550\n\n+61,451 \u2192 802,054,550 +61,451 = 802,116,001.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 802,116,001. Therefore, my first digit-by-digit approach must have an error. Let me check again.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit:\n\nPosition1 (ones): 0+1=1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5+5=10 \u2192 0, carry1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5 (from first number's hundreds place?) Wait, let me re-express the numbers digit by digit properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I misaligned the digits. Let me write both numbers with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 6 9 7 9 5 4 5 5 0\n\nSecond number:1 0 4 1 6 1 4 5 1\n\nWait, but actually, the first number is 697,954,550 which is:\n\nDigits from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\n6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSecond number:104,161,451 is:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds)