      "text": "\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 130,037,904 and 963,899,905. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. First, I should write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by their place values. Let me visualize that:\n\n      130,037,904\n    + 963,899,905\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 4 in the ones place, and the second has a 5. Adding those gives 4 + 5 = 9. So the ones digit of the sum is 9. No carryover here.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 0, and the second has a 0. 0 + 0 = 0. So the tens digit is 0. Still no carryover.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 9. 9 + 9 = 18. Oh, that's more than 10, so I write down 8 and carry over 1 to the next column. So the hundreds digit is 8, and remember the carryover 1.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 7, and the second has a 9. Adding those gives 7 + 9 = 16, plus the carried over 1 makes 17. So we put 7 in the thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 9. 3 + 9 = 12, plus the carryover 1 is 13. So write 3 here and carry over 1.\n\nMoving to the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 0, and the second has an 8. 0 + 8 = 8, plus the carryover 1 makes 9. So that's 9 in the hundred thousands place, no carryover now.\n\nNext is the millions place. The first number has a 0, and the second has a 3. 0 + 3 = 3. So that's straightforward, 3 in the millions place.\n\nThen the ten millions place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 6. 3 + 6 = 9. So 9 there.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has a 1, and the second has a 9. 1 + 9 = 10. So we write 0 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place. Since there's no higher place, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the billions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together, starting from the billions down: 1 (billion), 0 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 9 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 7 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 0 (ten), 9 (one). Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount each digit step by step properly.\n\nWait, let me write it out step by step again carefully:\n\nLet me align the numbers properly:\n\n          1 3 0 0 3 7 9 0 4\n        + 9 6 3 8 9 9 9 0 5\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes: 4 + 5 = 9. Write 9.\n\nTens: 0 + 0 = 0. Write 0.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 7 + 9 = 16 + 1 (carry) = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 9 = 12 +1 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 0 +8 =8 +1=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nMillions: 0 +3 =3. Write 3.\n\nTen millions: 3 +6 =9. Write 9.\n\nHundred millions: 1 +9 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: The carryover 1. So that's 1.\n\nPutting all together from left to right: 1 (billion), 0 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 9 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 7 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 0 (ten), 9 (one). Wait, but the hundred millions place is 0? Let me check again.\n\nWait, the hundred millions place: the first number's digit is 1 (since 130,037,904 is 1 hundred million, 3 ten millions, etc.), and the second number's hundred millions digit is 9 (since 963,899,905 is 9 hundred millions, 6 ten millions, etc.). So 1 +9 =10. So yes, that gives 0 in the hundred millions place with a carryover to the billions. So the digits would be:\n\nBillions: 1 (from carryover)\n\nHundred millions: 0\n\nTen millions: 3 (from first number's ten millions is 3, second's is 6? Wait, maybe I messed up the place values.\n\nWait, let me write each number's digits with their place values to be precise.\n\nFirst number: 130,037,904\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Billions place: 1 (since it's 1 hundred million, so actually, wait, 130 million is 1.3 hundred million, so maybe I need to think in terms of each digit's place.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write each digit's position:\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit from right to left (units to higher places):\n\nFirst number: 130,037,904\n\nDigits from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (units): 4\n\n2: 0\n\n3: 9\n\n4: 7\n\n5: 3\n\n6: 0\n\n7: 0\n\n8: 3\n\n9: 1\n\nSecond number: 963,899,905\n\nDigits from right (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1:5\n\n2:0\n\n3:9\n\n4:9\n\n5:8\n\n6:3\n\n7:6\n\n8:9\n\n9:9\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\n963,899,905:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1: 5 (units)\n\nPosition 2: 0 (tens)\n\nPosition 3: 9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4: 9 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5: 8 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6: 9 (hundred thousands)? Wait, no, let's see:\n\nWait, 963,899,905:\n\nThe number is 9,638,99,905? Wait, maybe better to separate commas:\n\n963,899,905 is 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (units). Wait, perhaps I need to parse it properly.\n\nWait, 963,899,905:\n\nThe commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\nFirst comma separates thousands, then millions, etc.\n\nSo 963,899,905 is:\n\n9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions),\n\n8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSo the digits from left to right (billions place first):\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions):9\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions):6\n\nDigit 3 (millions):3\n\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands):8\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands):9\n\nDigit 6 (thousands):9\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds):9\n\nDigit 8 (tens):0\n\nDigit 9 (units):5\n\nWait, but actually, the first digit is the hundred millions place because it's a 9-digit number. Wait, 963,899,905 has 9 digits. So:\n\nPosition 1 (leftmost): 9 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 2:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition3:3 (millions)\n\nPosition4:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition7:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition8:0 (tens)\n\nPosition9:5 (units)\n\nSimilarly, the first number 130,037,904 is 8 digits? Wait, 130,037,904 is 1 (hundred millions?), let's see:\n\n130,037,904:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (units). So yes, 9 digits as well.\n\nSo when adding digit by digit from the right (units to hundred millions):\n\nLet me list each digit position from right (units) to left (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number (130,037,904):\n\nPosition 1 (units):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):0\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1\n\nSecond number (963,899,905):\n\nPosition1 (units):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nStarting from Position1 (units):\n\n4 +5 =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n0 +0 =0. Write 0, no carryover.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n9 +9 =18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n7 +9 =16, plus carryover 1 =17. Write 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n3 +9 =12, plus 1 =13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n0 +8 =8, plus1=9. Write9, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n0 +3 =3. Write3, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n3 +6 =9. Write9, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n1 +9 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions, we need to add a new digit to the left (billions place). So the total digits become 10.\n\nSo the result digits from right to left (positions 1 to 10):\n\nPosition1:9\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:8\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:9\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:0\n\nPosition10:1 (the carryover)\n\nSo writing them in order from left (billions) to right (units):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nWait, let me arrange them properly:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:0\n\nUnits:9\n\nWait, but the millions place is the 7th digit from the right. Let me see:\n\nWait, the positions after adding:\n\nThe digits from left (billions) to right (units):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n9 (units).\n\nSo putting it all together: 1,099,397,809.\n\nWait, let me check that number:\n\nBreaking down 1,099,397,809:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n0 (hundred million),\n\n9 (ten million),\n\n9 (million),\n\n3 (hundred thousand),\n\n9 (ten thousand),\n\n7 (thousand),\n\n8 (hundred),\n\n0 (ten),\n\n9 (unit).\n\nYes, that seems right. Let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n130,037,904 + 963,899,905.\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n130,037,904\n\n+963,899,905\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n130 million + 963 million = 1,093 million.\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n37,904 + 899,905 = ?\n\n37,904 + 899,905:\n\n37,904 + 800,000 = 837,904\n\nThen +99,905: 837,904 +99,905 = 937,809.\n\nSo total is 1,093,000,000 + 937,809 = 1,093,937,809?\n\nWait, that contradicts the previous result. Hmm, so I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let me recalculate that alternative approach.\n\nWait, 130,037,904 is 130,000,000 + 37,904.\n\n963,899,905 is 963,000,000 + 899,905.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 130,000,000 + 963,000,000 = 1,093,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 37,904 + 899,905.\n\n37,904 + 899,905:\n\nLet's compute 37,904 + 899,905:\n\n37,904 + 899,905 = (37,904 + 800,000) + 99,905 = 837,904 + 99,905.\n\n837,904 + 99,905:\n\nAdding 837,904 + 100,000 = 937,904, then subtract 75 (since 99,905 is 100,000 - 95?), wait no:\n\nWait 99,905 is 99,905. So 837,904 + 99,905:\n\n837,904 + 99,905:\n\nAdd 837,904 + 90,000 = 927,904\n\nThen add 9,905: 927,904 +9,905 = 937,809.\n\nSo total is 1,093,000,000 + 937,809 = 1,093,937,809.\n\nBut earlier step-by-digit addition gave me 1,099,397,809. There's a discrepancy here. So I must have messed up in one of the methods.\n\nHmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait let me do the digit-by-digit again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 130,037,904\n\nSecond number:963,899,905\n\nAdding digit by digit from right:\n\nPosition1 (units):4+5=9\n\nPosition2 (tens):0+0=0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+9=18 \u2192 8, carryover1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0+8=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover\n\nPosition7 (millions):0+3=3 \u21923\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3+6=9 \u21929\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1+9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions place:1\n\nSo the digits from left to right (billions to units):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n9 (units).\n\nSo that's 1,099,397,809.\n\nBut the other method gave 1,093,937,809. So there's a difference in the millions and hundred thousands places.\n\nWait, let me check the alternative approach again.\n\nWait, in the alternative approach, I split the numbers into millions and the rest:\n\nFirst number: 130,037,904 = 130,000,000 + 37,904\n\nSecond number:963,899,905 = 963,000,000 + 899,905\n\nAdding the millions parts: 130M + 963M = 1,093M\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 37,904 + 899,905 = 937,809\n\nTotal: 1,093,000,000 + 937,809 = 1,093,937,809.\n\nHmm, so why the discrepancy?\n\nAh, I think I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit approach. Let me check the positions again.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit approach, when adding the millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number's millions digit:0 (since 130,037,904 is 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), etc.)\n\nSecond number's millions digit:3 (since 963,899,905 has 3 in the millions place? Wait, let's re-examine the digit breakdown.\n\nWait, the second number is 963,899,905.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- The millions place is the 4th digit from the right? Wait, no, let's clarify:\n\nWait, the number is 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSo the millions digit is the third digit from the left, which is 3. So in the digit-by-digit addition, position7 (millions place) is indeed 0 (from first number) +3 (from second number) =3. So that's correct.\n\nThen position8 (ten millions place):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3 (since 130,037,904 is 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions)...).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 6 (from 963,899,905: 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions)...).\n\nSo 3 +6 =9. Correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n1 +9 =10 \u21920 with carryover. Correct.\n\nSo the digit-by-digit gives 1,099,397,809.\n\nBut the other method gives 1,093,937,809. The difference is in the millions and hundred thousands places.\n\nWait, let me check the alternative approach again. Maybe I split the numbers incorrectly.\n\nWait, the first number is 130,037,904. Let's parse it:\n\n130,037,904:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n4 (units).\n\nSo the millions part is 130,000,000 (1 hundred million +3 ten millions +0 millions). So that's correct.\n\nThe remaining part is 037,904, which is 37,904.\n\nSecond number:963,899,905:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n3 (millions),\n\n8 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n9 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n5 (units).\n\nSo the millions part is 963,000,000 (9 hundred millions +6 ten millions +3 millions). The remaining part is 899,905 (8 hundred thousands +9 ten thousands +9 thousands +9 hundreds +0 tens +5 units).\n\nAdding the millions parts:130,000,000 +963,000,000 =1,093,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:37,904 +899,905.\n\nWait, 37,904 +899,905:\n\nLet me compute this again step by step:\n\n37,904\n\n+899,905\n\n------------\n\nAdding units:4+5=9\n\nTens:0+0=0\n\nHundreds:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nThousands:7+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:0+8=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nMillions place: there's no millions in the smaller parts, so 0+0=0.\n\nWait, but the smaller parts are 37,904 and 899,905. The 899,905 has an 8 in the hundred thousands place (since it's 899,905: 8 hundred thousands, 9 ten thousands, etc.), so when adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number's smaller part has 0 (since 37,904 is less than a million), so 0 +8 =8, plus any carryover?\n\nWait, let's do it digit by digit for the smaller parts:\n\n37,904 is:\n\nPosition1 (units):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):0\n\n899,905 is:\n\nPosition1 (units):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):0\n\nAdding them:\n\nPosition1:4+5=9\n\nPosition2:0+0=0\n\nPosition3:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nPosition4:7+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nPosition5:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition6:0+8=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover\n\nPosition7:0+0=0\n\nSo the sum of the smaller parts is 0 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 9 (units) \u2192 937,809.\n\nSo total sum is 1,093,000,000 + 937,809 =1,093,937,809.\n\nBut this contradicts the digit-by-digit approach which gave 1,099,397,809.\n\nHmm, so there must be an error in one of the methods. Let me check with a calculator approach.\n\nAlternatively, let me add the two numbers step by step:\n\n130,037,904\n\n+963,899,905\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits:4+5=9\n\nTens:0+0=0\n\nHundreds:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nThousands:7+9=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:0+8=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover\n\nMillions:0+3=3 \u21923\n\nTen millions:3+6=9 \u21929\n\nHundred millions:1+9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the digits are 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 9 (units). So that's 1,099,397,809.\n\nWait, but in the alternative method, the millions place in the first number is 0, and in the second number's millions place is 3, so adding gives 3. So the millions digit in the total should be 3. But in the alternative approach, the total was 1,093,937,809, which has 3 in the millions place (the 3 is the millions digit?), let's see:\n\nWait, 1,093,937,809:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,093,937,809:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n0 (hundred million),\n\n9 (ten million),\n\n3 (million),\n\n9 (hundred thousand),\n\n3 (ten thousand),\n\n7 (thousand),\n\n8 (hundred),\n\n0 (ten),\n\n9 (unit).\n\nWait, so the millions digit is 3 (the fourth digit from the right?), no, let's parse it properly:\n\nThe number is 1,093,937,809.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:0\n\nUnits:9\n\nAh, so the millions digit is indeed 3, which matches the digit-by-digit result. Wait, but the total in the alternative approach was 1,093,937,809, which is the same as the digit-by-digit result. Wait, but earlier I thought there was a discrepancy. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the alternative approach's final total.\n\nWait, in the alternative approach, I had:\n\nTotal millions parts:1,093,000,000\n\nPlus smaller parts:937,809 \u2192 total 1,093,000,000 + 937,809 = 1,093,937,809.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, the total is 1,099,397,809. Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, 1,093,937,809 vs 1,099,397,809. The difference is in the ten millions place: 0 vs 9?\n\nWait, let me write both numbers:\n\nDigit-by-digit result:1,099,397,809\n\nAlternative approach:1,093,937,809\n\nWait, the difference is in the hundred millions and ten millions places.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 0, ten millions is 9.\n\nIn the alternative approach, the hundred millions digit is 0, ten millions is 9 as well. Wait, let me parse the alternative total again:\n\n1,093,937,809:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n0 (hundred million),\n\n9 (ten million),\n\n3 (million),\n\n9 (hundred thousand),\n\n3 (ten thousand),\n\n7 (thousand),\n\n8 (hundred),\n\n0 (ten),\n\n9 (unit).\n\nWait, so the ten millions digit is 9, which matches the digit-by-digit. The hundred millions digit is 0, which matches. The millions digit is 3, which matches. The hundred thousands digit is 9 (from digit-by-digit's 9), but in the alternative approach, the hundred thousands digit is 9 as well (since the smaller parts added to 937,809, which has 9 in the hundred thousands place? Wait, 937,809 is 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), etc. So yes, the hundred thousands digit is 9