569. These are different. There's a mistake here.\n\nAh! I see the problem. In the first method, when adding the hundred millions place, I might have miscalculated the digits.\n\nWait, let's re-express the numbers properly:\n\nFirst number: 464,101,179\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSecond number:560,893,390\n\nBreaking down:\n\n5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the hundred millions place (the 9th digit from the right):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 4, second is 5. So 4+5 =9, but with the carryover from the ten millions place?\n\nWait, let's go step by step again with the digit positions:\n\nStarting from the right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (Ones):9+0=9\n\nPosition2 (Tens):7+9=16 \u21926, carryover1\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):1+3 +1=5\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):1+3=4\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):0+9=9\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):4+8=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition7 (Millions):6 (from first number's millions place) +0 (second's millions place) +1 (carryover) =7\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):4 (first's ten millions) +6 (second's ten millions) =10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):4 (first's hundred millions) +5 (second's hundred millions) +1 (carryover) =10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:9\n\nSo the number is 1,007,294,569.\n\nBut when I added the millions parts:\n\nFirst number is 464,101,179 \u2192 464,000,000 + 101,179\n\nSecond number is 560,893,390 \u2192560,000,000 + 893,390\n\nAdding the millions parts: 464M +560M = 1,024M\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 101,179 +893,390 = 994,569\n\nTotal sum: 1,024,000,000 + 994,569 = 1,024,994,569.\n\nWait, this is conflicting. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me compute 464,101,179 +560,893,390 using another approach.\n\nLet me write them out:\n\n464,101,179\n\n+560,893,390\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:9+0=9\n\nTens:7+9=16 \u21926, carryover1\n\nHundreds:1+3 +1=5\n\nThousands:1+3=4\n\nTen thousands:0+9=9\n\nHundred thousands:4+8=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+0 +1=7\n\nTen millions:4+6=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:4+5 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nBillions:1\n\nSo the result is 1,007,294,569.\n\nBut when I broke it into millions, I got 1,024,994,569. That's a big difference. Where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The first number is 464,101,179. Breaking down:\n\n464,101,179 = 400,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 4,000,000 + 100,000 + 1,000 + 100 +70 +9.\n\nWait, so the millions part is 464,000,000 (400M +60M +4M). The remaining is 101,179.\n\nThe second number is 560,893,390 = 500,000,000 +60,000,000 +0 +800,000 +90,000 +3,000 +300 +90 +0.\n\nSo the millions part is 560,000,000 (500M +60M). The remaining is 893,390.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 464M +560M = 1,024M. That's correct.\n\nAdding the remainders: 101,179 +893,390.\n\nLet me compute that again:\n\n101,179 + 893,390:\n\n101,179 + 800,000 = 901,179\n\n901,179 +93,390:\n\n901,179 +90,000 = 991,179\n\n991,179 +3,390 = 994,569. So total is 994,569.\n\nThus total sum is 1,024,000,000 + 994,569 = 1,024,994,569.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit addition, it's 1,007,294,569. These are two different results. There must be a miscalculation in one of the methods.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check the positions again carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 464,101,179\n\nSecond number:560,893,390\n\nAdding:\n\n          464,101,179\n      