      "text": "\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 197,161,220 and 800,090,976. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. First, I should write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by their place values. Let me visualize that:\n\n      197,161,220\n    + 800,090,976\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's break it down digit by digit.\n\n1. **Ones place:** The first number has a 0, and the second also has a 6. Adding those gives 0 + 6 = 6. So the ones digit of the sum is 6.\n\n2. **Tens place:** Next, moving left to the tens place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 7. 2 + 7 = 9. So the tens digit is 9.\n\n3. **Hundreds place:** The first number has a 2 here, and the second has a 9. 2 + 9 = 11. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over 1 to the next higher place. I'll write down 1 in the hundreds place and remember the carryover.\n\n4. **Thousands place:** Now, the first number has a 1 here, and the second has a 7. Adding those gives 1 + 7 = 8, plus the carried over 1 makes 9. So the thousands digit is 9, and no carryover here.\n\n5. **Ten-thousands place:** Moving on, the first number has a 6, and the second has a 0. 6 + 0 = 6. So that's straightforward, 6 in the ten-thousands place.\n\n6. **Hundred-thousands place:** The first number has a 1 here, and the second has a 9. 1 + 9 = 10. Again, that's 10, so we write 0 and carry over 1.\n\n7. **Millions place:** The first number has a 7, and the second has a 0. 7 + 0 = 7, plus the carried over 1 makes 8. So 8 in the millions place.\n\n8. **Ten-millions place:** The first number has a 9, and the second has a 0. 9 + 0 = 9. So that's 9 here.\n\n9. **Hundred-millions place:** The first number has a 1, and the second has an 8. 1 + 8 = 9. So 9 in the hundred-millions place.\n\nWait a second, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me recount each place step by step carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 197,161,220\nBreaking it down digit by digit from right to left (since we start adding from the right):\nPositions (from right):\n1 (ones): 0\n2 (tens): 2\n3 (hundreds): 2\n4 (thousands): 1\n5 (ten thousands): 6\n6 (hundred thousands): 1\n7 (millions): 7\n8 (ten millions): 9\n9 (hundred millions): 1\n\nSecond number: 800,090,976\nBreaking down similarly:\n1 (ones): 6\n2 (tens): 7\n3 (hundreds): 9\n4 (thousands): 0\n5 (ten thousands): 0\n6 (hundred thousands): 9\n7 (millions): 0\n8 (ten millions): 0\n9 (hundred millions): 8\n\nNow adding each corresponding digit:\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 6 = 6\n2. Tens: 2 + 7 = 9\n3. Hundreds: 2 + 9 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n4. Thousands: 1 (from first) + 0 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 2? Wait, wait, hold on. Wait, the thousands place in the first number is 1, and the second number's thousands place is 0. So 1 + 0 = 1, plus the carryover 1 makes 2. Wait, earlier I thought it was 1 +7? Wait, maybe I messed up the positions here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me write both numbers with their digits properly aligned:\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 197,161,220\nBreaking down each digit from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0\nPosition 2 (tens): 2\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 6\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 1\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 9\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 1\n\nSecond number: 800,090,976\nBreaking down similarly:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6\nPosition 2 (tens): 7\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 9\nPosition 4 (thousands): 0\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 0\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9\nPosition 7 (millions): 0\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8\n\nNow adding each position:\n\n1. Position 1 (ones): 0 + 6 = 6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Position 2 (tens): 2 + 7 = 9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\n3. Position 3 (hundreds): 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n4. Position 4 (thousands): 1 (from first) + 0 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 2. Write 2. Carryover 0.\n\n5. Position 5 (ten thousands): 6 + 0 = 6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\n6. Position 6 (hundred thousands): 1 + 9 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n7. Position 7 (millions): 7 + 0 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\n8. Position 8 (ten millions): 9 + 0 = 9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\n9. Position 9 (hundred millions): 1 + 8 = 9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left (since we started from position 1):\n\nThe digits we wrote are:\n\nPosition 1: 6\n\nPosition 2:9\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:8\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:9\n\nSo combining them from highest to lowest (hundred millions down to ones):\n\n9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 998,062,196?\n\nWait, let me arrange them properly. Since the digits are from position 9 to 1:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):8\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1\n\nPosition2 (tens):9\n\nPosition1 (ones):6\n\nSo the number is 998,062,196.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let's write the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the highest place (hundred millions):\n\n9 (hundred millions)\n\n9 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n0 (hundred thousands)\n\n6 (ten thousands)\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n6 (ones)\n\nSo putting commas in the right places:\n\n998,062,196.\n\nHmm, but let me verify this with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:\n\n197,161,220 + 800,090,976.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 197,161,220 + 800,000,000 = 997,161,220.\n\nThen add the remaining 90,976:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,976 = ?\n\n997,161,220 + 90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen add 976: 997,251,220 + 976 = 997,252,196.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which means I made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me recalculate the first method again carefully.\n\nFirst method step-by-step digit addition:\n\nPositions:\n\n1. Ones: 0 +6=6\n\n2. Tens:2+7=9\n\n3. Hundreds:2+9=11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\n4. Thousands:1 (from first number) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) = 2. So thousands digit is 2, carryover 0.\n\n5. Ten thousands:6 +0=6\n\n6. Hundred thousands:1 +9=10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\n7. Millions:7 +0 +1 (carryover) =8\n\n8. Ten millions:9 +0=9\n\n9. Hundred millions:1 +8=9\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:9\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo the number is 998,062,196.\n\nBut when I did the second method, adding 197,161,220 + 800,090,976:\n\nFirst, 197,161,220 + 800,000,000 = 997,161,220.\n\nThen adding the remaining 90,976:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,976.\n\nLet me compute that again:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen add 976: 997,251,220 + 976 = 997,252,196.\n\nWait, so now there's a conflict between the two methods. Which one is correct?\n\nHmm, perhaps I made an error in the second method's breakdown.\n\nWait, the second number is 800,090,976. Breaking that down:\n\n800,090,976 is 800,000,000 + 90,000 + 976. Wait, 800,000,000 + 90,000 is 800,090,000, plus 976 gives 800,090,976. So that's correct.\n\nSo adding to 197,161,220:\n\n197,161,220 + 800,090,976 = ?\n\nLet me do this addition step by step:\n\nStart with 197,161,220\n\nAdd 800,000,000: 197,161,220 + 800,000,000 = 997,161,220\n\nNow add the remaining 90,976:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,976.\n\nLet me add 90,000 first:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen add 976:\n\n997,251,220 + 976 = 997,252,196.\n\nHmm, so according to this method, the total is 997,252,196.\n\nBut the first method gave 998,062,196. There's a difference here. So I must have messed up one of the steps.\n\nWait, let me check the first method again. Let me write both numbers aligned properly:\n\nFirst number: 197,161,220\n\nSecond number:800,090,976\n\nLet me write them without commas for clarity:\n\n197161220\n\n+800090976\n\n------------\n\nAdding digit by digit from right:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1):\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n0 +6 =6\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n2 +7=9\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2 +9=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n1 (from first number) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =2 \u2192 write 2, carryover 0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6 (first) +0 (second)=6 \u2192 write 6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n1 (first) +9 (second)=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n7 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=8 \u2192 write8, carryover0\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n9 (first) +0 (second)=9 \u2192 write9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n1 (first) +8 (second)=9 \u2192 write9\n\nSo the digits from right to left (positions 1-9) are:\n\n6 (ones),9 (tens),1 (hundreds),2 (thousands),6 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),8 (millions),9 (ten millions),9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo arranging from highest to lowest place:\n\n9 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),8 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),1 (hundreds),9 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nWhich is 998,062,196.\n\nBut the other method gave 997,252,196. So there's a discrepancy in the millions place. Let me check the millions place in the first method.\n\nWait, in the first method, position7 (millions) is 7 (from first number) +0 (second) plus carryover1 from the previous step (position6). So 7+0+1=8. So millions digit is 8. But in the second method, when adding 197,161,220 + 800,090,976, the millions digit in the first number is 7 (since 197,161,220 is 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),7 (millions)...). The second number's millions digit is 0 (since 800,090,976 is 8 (hundred millions),0 (ten millions),0 (millions)...). So adding 7 +0 =7, but in the first method, we had a carryover from the hundred thousands place. Wait, the carryover from position6 (hundred thousands) was 1, so that's why it's 7+0+1=8. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the second method, when adding 197,161,220 + 800,090,976, the millions place would be 7 (from first) +0 (second) =7, but in the total of 997,252,196, the millions digit is 2? Wait, no, let me look at the number 997,252,196.\n\nBreaking down 997,252,196:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n- 9 (ten millions)\n\n-7 (millions)\n\n-2 (hundred thousands)\n\n-5 (ten thousands)\n\n-2 (thousands)\n\n-1 (hundreds)\n\n-9 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nWait, so the millions digit here is 7, but according to the first method it should be 8. So there's a conflict here. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, let me recalculate the second method again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 197,161,220\n\nSecond number:800,090,976\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n       197,161,220\n\n     + 800,090,976\n\n     ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:0+6=6\n\nTens:2+7=9\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\nThousands:1+0 +1=2\n\nTen thousands:6+0=6\n\nHundred thousands:1+9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nMillions:7+0 +1=8\n\nTen millions:9+0=9\n\nHundred millions:1+8=9\n\nSo the total is 998,062,196. So the second method must have had an error in the addition steps.\n\nWait, when I did the second method earlier, I added 197,161,220 + 800,000,000 = 997,161,220. That's correct. Then adding 90,976 to that:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,976.\n\nLet me compute this again step by step:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen add 976:\n\n997,251,220 + 976 = ?\n\nAdding 900: 997,251,220 + 900 = 997,252,120\n\nThen add 76: 997,252,120 +76 = 997,252,196.\n\nWait, so that gives 997,252,196. But according to the digit-by-digit addition, it should be 998,062,196. There's a difference in the hundred thousands and millions places.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method's breakdown of the numbers. Let me check the second number again: 800,090,976.\n\nBreaking down 800,090,976:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n\n- 0 (ten millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 0 (ten thousands)\n\n- 0 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 6 (ones)\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. Let me parse 800,090,976 properly.\n\nThe number is 800,090,976.\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1 (ones):6\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):0\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nSo the millions digit (position7) is 0, which is correct. So when adding to the first number's millions digit (7), it should be 7+0=7 plus any carryover.\n\nWait, but in the first method, the millions digit was 8 because of a carryover from the hundred thousands place. Let me see:\n\nIn the first method, the hundred thousands place (position6) was 1 (from first number) +9 (second) =10, so we write 0 and carryover 1 to the millions place. So the millions place (position7) is 7 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =8. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the second method must have an error. Let me see where.\n\nWait, when I added 197,161,220 + 800,090,976 as:\n\nFirst adding 800,000,000 to get 997,161,220, then adding the remaining 90,976. But the remaining part is not 90,976. Wait, the second number is 800,090,976. So subtracting 800,000,000 gives 90,976. That's correct. So adding 90,976 to 997,161,220.\n\nWait, let me do this addition in another way:\n\n997,161,220\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a090,976\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:0 +6=6\n\nTens:2 +7=9\n\nHundreds:2 +9=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1\n\nThousands:1 (from first number) +0 (since 90,976 has 0 in thousands place?) Wait, 90,976 is 090,976 when considering up to millions?\n\nWait, 90,976 is 000,090,976 when aligned with 997,161,220.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write both numbers with the same number of digits:\n\n997,161,220\n\n+\u00a0\u00a000,090,976\n\n------------------\n\nAdding these:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:0+6=6\n\nTens:2+7=9\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nThousands:1 (from first) +0 (second) +1=2\n\nTen thousands:6 +0=6\n\nHundred thousands:1 +9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nMillions:7 (from first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=8\n\nTen millions:9 +0=9\n\nHundred millions:9 +0=9\n\nSo the total is 998,062,196. Which matches the first method. So my earlier mistake in the second method was probably an arithmetic error when adding 997,161,220 + 90,976. Let me recalculate that:\n\n997,161,220\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a090,976\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add 90,000 first:\n\n997,161,220 + 90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen add 976:\n\n997,251,220 + 976 = ?\n\nAdding 900: 997,251,220 + 900 = 997,252,120\n\nThen add 76: 997,252,120 +76 = 997,252,196.\n\nWait, but according to the correct digit-by-digit addition, it should be 998,062,196. There's a discrepancy here. Wait, why?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The second number is 800,090,976. When I broke it down as 800,000,000 + 90,976, that's correct. But when adding to 197,161,220, the total should be:\n\n197,161,220 + 800,090,976 = (197,161,220 + 800,000,000) + 90,976 = 997,161,220 + 90,976.\n\nBut when I add 997,161,220 + 90,976, I get 997,252,196, but according to the digit-by-digit method, it should be 998,062,196. So there's a mistake here. Let me do this addition again carefully:\n\n997,161,220\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a090,976\n\n-------------\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n997,161,220\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a090,976\n\n-------------\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 0 +6 =6\n\nTens:2 +7=9\n\nHundreds:2 +9=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nThousands:1 (from first number) +0 (since 90,976 has 0 in thousands place?) Wait, 90,976 is 090,976 when considering up to millions. Wait, the thousands place in 90,976 is the fourth digit from the right, which is 0 (since it's 90,976 \u2192 9 (ten thousands?), wait no:\n\nWait, 90,976 is:\n\nPosition1 (ones):6\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9\n\nWait, no. Wait, 90,976 is 90 thousand 976. So:\n\nBreaking down 90,976:\n\n- 9 (ten thousands place)\n\n- 0 (thousands place)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n-7 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 90,976 is:\n\nDigit positions from right:\n\nPosition1:6 (ones)\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:0 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands)\n\nWait, but 90,976 is 9*10,000 + 0*1,000 + 9*100 +7*10 +6*1. So yes, the thousands place is 0, ten thousands is 9.\n\nTherefore, when adding to 997,161,220:\n\nThe thousands place in 997,161,220 is the fourth digit from the right: let's see:\n\n997,161,220:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nPosition1 (ones):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):2\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition7 (millions):7\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nWait, so the thousands place (position4) is 1.\n\nAdding to the thousands place of 90,976 which is 0 (position4):\n\nSo 1 +0 =1, plus any carryover?\n\nWait, but in the previous step (hundreds place):\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2 (from first number) +9 (from second) =11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1.\n\nSo the thousands place becomes 1 (original) +0 (from second number) +1 (carryover) =2. So thousands digit is 2.\n\nContinuing:\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 (first) +9 (second) =15 \u2192 write5, carryover1.\n\nWait, but in the previous method, when adding 997,161,220 +90,976, the ten thousands place would be 6 (from first number's ten thousands) plus 9 (from the second number's ten thousands) =15, so that's 5 with a carryover.\n\nWait, but in the digit-by-digit addition earlier, when adding the original two numbers, the ten thousands place was 6 (from first) +0 (from second) =6. Wait, that's conflicting.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. In the second method, when I broke down the second number as 800,000,000 +90,976, the 90,976 is part of the second number's digits beyond the hundred thousands place. Wait, the second number is 800,090,976, so the ten thousands place is 0, not 9. Wait, no:\n\nWait, the second number is 800,090,976. Let's parse it correctly:\n\nBreaking down 800,090,976:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n\n- 0 (ten millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 0 (ten thousands)\n\n- 0 (thousands)\n\n- 9 (hundreds)\n\n-7 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nAh! So the ten thousands place is 0, not 9. The 9 is in the hundred thousands place. Therefore, when adding 90,976 to 997,161,220, that 90,976 is actually part of the second number's digits beyond the hundred thousands. Wait, but when I broke down the second number as 800,000,000 +90,976, that's incorrect because the 90,976 is not in the lower digits but in the hundred thousands and below.\n\nWait, 800,090,976 is 800,000,000 + 90,000 + 976. So the 90,000 is in the hundred thousands place (since 90,000 is 0.09 million). Wait, no:\n\nWait, 800,090,976:\n\n- 800,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n- 090,000 (hundred thousands place: 90,000)\n\n- 976 (the last three digits)\n\nSo the 90,000 is in the hundred thousands place (the sixth digit from the right). Therefore, when adding 800,090,976 to 197,161,220, the breakdown should be:\n\n197,161,220 + 800,000,000 = 997,161,220\n\nThen adding the remaining 90,000 +976 =90,976:\n\n997,161,220 +90,000 = 997,251,220\n\nThen +976 = 997,252,196.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the correct total is 998,062,196. So there's a conflict here. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me re-express both numbers numerically:\n\nFirst number: 197,161,220\n\nSecond number:800,090,976\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them without commas:\n\n197161220\n\n+800090976\n\n-------------\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1:0+6=6\n\nPosition2:2+7=9\n\nPosition3:2+9=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition4:1+0 +1=2\n\nPosition5:6+0=6\n\nPosition6:1+9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nPosition7:7+0 +1=8\n\nPosition8:9+0=9\n\nPosition9:1+8=9\n\nSo the result is 998,062,196.\n\nBut when adding 197,161,220 +800,090,976 as:\n\n197,161,220 +800,000,000 =997,161,220\n\nThen adding 90,976 (the remaining part of the second number):\n\n997,161,220 +90,976 = ?\n\nWait, but 90,976 is 90,000 +976. Adding 90,000 to 997,161,220 gives 997,251,220. Then adding 976 gives 997,252,196.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit, it should be 998,062,196. The difference is in the hundred thousands place. Let me see:\n\nThe digit-by-digit result is 998,062,196.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n-9 (ten millions)\n\n-8 (millions)\n\n-0 (hundred thousands)\n\n-6 (ten thousands)\n\n-2 (thousands)\n\n-1 (hundreds)\n\n-9 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nWhereas the other method gives 997,252,196:\n\n-9 (hundred millions)\n\n-9 (ten millions)\n\n-7 (millions)\n\n-2 (hundred thousands)\n\n-5 (ten thousands)\n\n-2 (thousands)\n\n-1 (hundreds)\n\n-9 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nThe discrepancy is in the millions and hundred thousands places.\n\nAh! I see now. The mistake in the second method is that when adding 800,090,976, the 90,000 is in the hundred thousands place, not the ten thousands. Therefore, when adding to 197,161,220, the hundred thousands place of the first number is 1, and adding 9 (from the second number's hundred thousands place) gives 10, which carries over to the millions place. But in the second method, when I added 90,000 (which is ten thousands place?), I was incorrectly adding it to the ten thousands place instead of the hundred thousands.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nThe se