      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 280,366,684 and 685,594,101. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down one under the other to align the digits properly. Let me see:\n\n      280,366,684\n    + 685,594,101\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 4 in the ones place, and the second has a 1. Adding those gives 4 + 1 = 5. So the ones digit of the sum is 5. No carryover here.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 0. 8 + 0 = 8. So the tens digit is 8. Still no carryover.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 1. 6 + 1 = 7. Hundreds digit is 7. No problem there.\n\nThousands place: 6 (from first number) + 4 (from second) = 10. Oh, here we get a two-digit number. So I write down 0 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the next higher digit.\n\nTen-thousands place: The first number has a 3, the second has a 9. Adding those gives 12, plus the carried over 1 makes 13. So we put 3 here and carry over 1 again.\n\nHundred-thousands place: First number has a 6, second has a 5. 6 + 5 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. So 2 in this place, carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: First number has an 0, second has an 8. 0 + 8 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. So 9 here, no carryover.\n\nTen-millions place: First number has an 8, second has a 5. 8 +5 =13. So write 3 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: First number has a 2, second has a 6. 2 +6 =8, plus the carried 1 makes 9.\n\nBillions place: The first number has nothing here (since it's 280 million), so it's 0, and the second number has a 6 in the billions place. Wait, actually, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 280,366,684 is 280 million, so the digits are:\n\nBreaking down 280,366,684:\n- Billions: 2 (since 200,000,000)\n- Hundred millions: 8 (80,000,000)\n- Ten millions: 0 (0,000,000)\nWait, actually, maybe I miscounted the places. Let me write both numbers digit by digit to be precise.\n\nLet me list each digit position for both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 280,366,684\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (starting at ones place):\n\nPosition: 1 (ones): 4\n2 (tens): 8\n3 (hundreds):6\n4 (thousands):6\n5 (ten thousands):3\n6 (hundred thousands):6\n7 (millions):0\n8 (ten millions):8\n9 (hundred millions):2\n\nSecond number: 685,594,101\nBreaking down similarly:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1\n2 (tens):0\n3 (hundreds):1\n4 (thousands):4\n5 (ten thousands):9\n6 (hundred thousands):5\n7 (millions):8\n8 (ten millions):5\n9 (hundred millions):6\n10 (billions):6 (Wait, 685 million is 685,594,101. So the billions digit is 6, then hundred millions 8, ten millions 5, etc. Let me confirm:\n\nWait, 685,594,101 is 6 hundred millions (600,000,000), 8 ten millions (80,000,000), 5 millions (5,000,000), so:\n\nPosition breakdown:\n\nPosition 1:1 (ones)\n2:0 (tens)\n3:1 (hundreds)\n4:4 (thousands)\n5:9 (ten thousands)\n6:5 (hundred thousands)\n7:8 (millions)\n8:5 (ten millions)\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n10:6 (billions)\n\nWait, so the first number is 9 digits (up to hundred millions), the second is 9 digits as well, but the second has a billions digit (position 10). Wait, 685,594,101 is 685 million, so the billions digit is 6 (since 600 million is part of it). So the second number has a digit in the billions place (position 10), which is 6, while the first number has 0 there. So when adding, the billions place will be 6 (from second number) plus 0 (from first), plus any carryover.\n\nHmm, maybe I need to realign the digits properly. Let me write both numbers with all digits aligned:\n\nFirst number: 2 8 0 3 6 6 6 8 4 (positions 9 to 1)\nWait, actually, let's write each digit from left to right, starting with the highest place.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write each number digit by digit in order:\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, that can't be right. Wait, 280,366,684:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n2 (hundred millions place)\n8 (ten millions)\n0 (millions)\n3 (hundred thousands)\n6 (ten thousands)\n6 (thousands)\n6 (hundreds)\n8 (tens)\n4 (ones)\n\nWait, but that's 9 digits. The second number is 685,594,101:\n\n6 (hundred millions? Wait, 685 million is 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions), then 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, but 685,594,101 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n8 (ten millions)\n5 (millions)\n5 (hundred thousands)\n9 (ten thousands)\n4 (thousands)\n1 (hundreds)\n0 (tens)\n1 (ones)\n\nWait, but that's 9 digits as well. Wait, but 685,594,101 is actually 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions), so the first three digits are 6,8,5 (hundred millions, ten millions, millions). Then the next three digits: 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands). Then 1 (hundred), 0 (ten), 1 (one). So total 9 digits. So both numbers are 9 digits. But when adding, the second number has a 6 in the hundred millions place, and the first has 2 there. Wait, but when adding, the highest place would be hundred millions, so no billions place needed unless there's a carryover. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me write both numbers digit by digit from left to right (highest to lowest place):\n\nFirst number: 2 8 0 3 6 6 6 8 4\n\nSecond number: 6 8 5 5 9 4 1 0 1\n\nWait, let's confirm:\n\nFirst number: 280,366,684\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions): 2\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions):8\n\nDigit 3 (millions):0\n\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands):3\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit 6 (thousands):6\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds):6\n\nDigit 8 (tens):8\n\nDigit 9 (ones):4\n\nSecond number: 685,594,101\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions):6\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions):8\n\nDigit 3 (millions):5\n\nDigit 4 (hundred thousands):5\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands):9\n\nDigit 6 (thousands):4\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds):1\n\nDigit 8 (tens):0\n\nDigit 9 (ones):1\n\nSo when adding, we can align them as:\n\n       2 8 0 3 6 6 6 8 4\n     + 6 8 5 5 9 4 1 0 1\n     --------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nDigit 9 (ones): 4 +1 =5\n\nDigit 8 (tens):8 +0=8\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):6 +1=7\n\nDigit6 (thousands):6 +4=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover 1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6 +9=15, plus carryover 1 \u219216 \u2192 write 6, carryover1\n\nWait, wait, hold on. Wait, the digits for each place:\n\nWait, let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 9 (ones):\n\nFirst number:4, second:1 \u2192 4+1=5. Write 5. No carryover.\n\nPosition8 (tens):\n\nFirst:8, second:0 \u21928+0=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):\n\nFirst:6, second:1 \u21926+1=7. Write7. No carryover.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):\n\nFirst:6, second:4 \u21926+4=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst:6 (from first number's ten thousands place?), wait let me check:\n\nWait, the first number's digit breakdown:\n\nDigit positions (from left to right):\n\n1: hundred millions (2)\n\n2: ten millions (8)\n\n3: millions (0)\n\n4: hundred thousands (3)\n\n5: ten thousands (6)\n\n6: thousands (6)\n\n7: hundreds (6)\n\n8: tens (8)\n\n9: ones (4)\n\nSecond number's digit breakdown:\n\n1: hundred millions (6)\n\n2: ten millions (8)\n\n3: millions (5)\n\n4: hundred thousands (5)\n\n5: ten thousands (9)\n\n6: thousands (4)\n\n7: hundreds (1)\n\n8: tens (0)\n\n9: ones (1)\n\nSo Position5 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number has 6 (digit5), second has 9 (digit5). So 6+9=15. Plus the carryover from previous (position6) which was 1. Wait, no, the carryover from position6 (thousands place) is added to the ten thousands place?\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nWhen adding, after the thousands place (position6) gives a carryover, that carryover is added to the next higher place (ten thousands, position5). Wait no, actually, the carryover from position6 (thousands) would go to the ten thousands place (position5). Wait, the positions are numbered from right, but when adding left to right, perhaps I got confused.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to think in terms of starting from the right (ones) and moving left, adding each column and carrying over as needed.\n\nLet me reorganize:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position9):\n\nPosition9 (ones): 4 +1 =5. Write 5. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition8 (tens):8 +0 =8. Write8. Carryover0.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):6 +1 =7. Write7. Carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):6 (from first) +4 (from second) =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 (first) +9 (second) =15, plus carryover1 \u219216. So write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):3 (first) +5 (second) =8, plus carryover1 \u21929. Write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition3 (millions):0 (first) +5 (second) =5. Write5, carryover0.\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):8 (first) +8 (second) =16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):2 (first) +6 (second) =8, plus carryover1 \u21929. Write9, no carryover.\n\nWait, let me check each step again carefully:\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's digit1:2, second's digit1:6 \u21922+6=8, plus any carryover from the previous (ten millions place). Wait, the carryover from position2 (ten millions) would be added here. Wait, let's track step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition9: 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition8:8, no carryover.\n\nPosition7:7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6:6+4=10 \u21920 with carryover1.\n\nPosition5:6+9=15 +1 (carryover) =16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:3+5=8 +1 (carryover) =9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nPosition3:0+5=5 \u21925, carryover0.\n\nPosition2:8 (first) +8 (second) =16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition1:2+6=8 +1 (carryover) =9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from position1 to