econd number) =11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nWait, but the first number's ten thousands digit is 7, and the second's is 4? Let me check the second number again. The second number is 280,544,260. Breaking down:\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 0 (millions)\n- 5 (hundred thousands)\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n- 4 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 6 (tens)\n- 0 (ones)\n\nYes, so the ten thousands place is indeed 4. So 7+4=11. So we write 1 and carryover 1.\n\n6. **Hundred thousands place:** Next, the hundred thousands place. The first number has 0 (since the digits are ...2 (hundred thousands?), wait let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to list all digits with their place values properly.\n\nFirst number: 666,270,385\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions place)\n- 6 (ten millions)\n- 6 (millions)\n- 2 (hundred thousands)\n- 7 (ten thousands)\n- 0 (thousands)\n- 3 (hundreds)\n- 8 (tens)\n- 5 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 280,544,260\n\n- 2 (hundred millions)\n- 8 (ten millions)\n- 0 (millions)\n- 5 (hundred thousands)\n- 4 (ten thousands)\n- 4 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 6 (tens)\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSo moving to the next digit after ten thousands (position 5):\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands place):\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred thousands)\n\nSecond number:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nSo 2 +5 =7, plus the carryover 1 from the previous step (from ten thousands place) makes 8. So write 8, no carryover.\n\n7. **Millions place:** Next is the millions place. \n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6 (since after hundred thousands comes millions: the digits are 6 (millions), then 2 (hundred thousands). Wait, let me confirm:\n\nWait, the first number's digits from left:\n\n1. 6 (hundred millions)\n2. 6 (ten millions)\n3. 6 (millions)\n4. 2 (hundred thousands)\n5. 7 (ten thousands)\n6. 0 (thousands)\n7. 3 (hundreds)\n8. 8 (tens)\n9. 5 (ones)\n\nSo the millions place is the third digit from the left, which is 6.\n\nSecond number's millions 