      "index": 0,
      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 967,099,234 and 125,931,556. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down one under the other to align the digits properly. Let me see:\n\n      967,099,234\n    + 125,931,556\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 4 in the ones place, and the second has a 6. Adding those gives 4 + 6 = 10. So I write down 0 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place: the first number has a 3, the second has a 5. Adding those plus the carried 1: 3 + 5 + 1 = 9. No carryover here.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 (from first number) + 5 (second number) = 7. Still no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 9 (first) + 1 (second) = 10. Wait, so that's 0 with a carryover of 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: The first number has a 9, the second has a 3. Adding them plus the carried 1: 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. So write 3 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 (from first number) + 9 (second) + 1 (carry) = 10. So 0 here, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 7 (first) + 5 (second) + 1 (carry) = 13. Again, 3 and carryover 1.\n\nTen-millions place: 6 (first) + 2 (second) + 1 (carry) = 9. No carryover here.\n\nHundred-millions place: 9 (first) + 1 (second) = 10. So 0 with a carryover of 1.\n\nBillions place: The first number has a 9, the second has nothing except the carried 1. So 9 + 1 = 10. That gives us a 0 in the billions place and a carryover of 1 to the next (ten-billions) place, which is just the carried 1.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits would be 0 (ones), 9 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 0 (hundred-thousands), 3 (millions), 9 (ten-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), 0 (billions), and the 1 in the ten-billions place. Wait, let me check that again because I might have messed up the places.\n\nWait, maybe I should write each step more carefully. Let me recount each digit position step by step:\n\nLet me align the numbers properly:\n\n          9 6 7 0 9 9 2 3 4\n        + 1 2 5 9 3 1 5 5 6\n        ---------------------\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n1. Ones: 4 + 6 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n2. Tens: 3 + 5 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n3. Hundreds: 2 + 5 = 7 \u2192 7, no carry\n4. Thousands: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n5. Ten-thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n6. Hundred-thousands: 0 + 9 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n7. Millions: 7 + 5 = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n8. Ten-millions: 6 + 2 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n10. Billions place: The first number has 9, the second has 1 (since it's the next digit?), wait no. Wait, the second number is 125,931,556 which is 9 digits, so when aligned, the first number is 9 digits as well? Wait, 967,099,234 is 9 digits, and 125,931,556 is also 9 digits. So when adding, the billions place for the second number is 1 (since it's 125 million...). Wait, let me check the digits again:\n\nWait, 967,099,234 is broken down as:\n\nBillions: 9\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions:7\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:4? Wait, no, wait. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. Let me parse each number properly.\n\nFirst number: 967,099,234\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place?), wait actually, in the standard format:\n\nThe number 967,099,234 is:\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions?), let's see:\n\nWait, commas are placed as follows: for numbers, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So 967,099,234 is:\n\n9 (hundred millions place?), let's see:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- The first comma from the right separates thousands, the next separates millions, etc.\n\nWait, actually, in the number 967,099,234:\n\n- The digits are: 9 6 7 , 0 9 9 , 2 3 4\n\nSo:\n\n- The first part (leftmost) is 967 (millions), then 099 (thousands), then 234 (units).\n\nSo the places are:\n\nBillions: 9 (since 900,000,000)\n\nHundred millions: 6 (60,000,000)\n\nTen millions:7 (7,000,000)\n\nMillions:0 (0)\n\nHundred thousands:9 (90,000)\n\nTen thousands:9 (9,000)\n\nThousands:2 (2,000)\n\nHundreds:3 (300)\n\nTens:4 (40)\n\nOnes:4? Wait, no, the last part is 234, so the ones digit is 4, tens is 3, hundreds is 2.\n\nWait, perhaps I should list each digit's place:\n\nLet me write each digit with its place value for both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 967,099,234\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\n1. 9 - Hundred millions place (since it's the 9th digit from the right? Wait, actually, let's count the digits:\n\nTotal digits: 9. So positions are:\n\nDigit 1 (leftmost): 9 \u2192 Hundred millions (10^8)\n\nDigit 2:6 \u2192 Ten millions (10^7)\n\nDigit3:7 \u2192 Millions (10^6)\n\nDigit4:0 \u2192 Hundred thousands (10^5)\n\nDigit5:9 \u2192 Ten thousands (10^4)\n\nDigit6:9 \u2192 Thousands (10^3)\n\nDigit7:2 \u2192 Hundreds (10^2)\n\nDigit8:3 \u2192 Tens (10^1)\n\nDigit9:4 \u2192 Ones (10^0)\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, actually, the first digit is the highest place. Let me think again.\n\nWait, 967,099,234:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place) because 9 * 100,000,000 = 900,000,000\n\nThen 6 (ten millions: 60,000,000)\n\n7 (millions: 7,000,000)\n\n0 (hundred thousands: 0)\n\n9 (ten thousands: 90,000)\n\n9 (thousands: 9,000)\n\n2 (hundreds: 200)\n\n3 (tens: 30)\n\n4 (ones:4)\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition (from right, starting at 1):\n\nOnes (1):4\n\nTens (2):3\n\nHundreds (3):2\n\nThousands (4):9\n\nTen thousands (5):9\n\nHundred thousands (6):0\n\nMillions (7):7\n\nTen millions (8):6\n\nHundred millions (9):9\n\nWait, that's confusing. Maybe better to list each digit's place:\n\nLet me write the first number as:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1:4 (ones)\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:9 (thousands)\n\n5:9 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:7 (millions)\n\n8:6 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 125,931,556\n\nBreaking down similarly:\n\n125,931,556:\n\nDigit positions from right:\n\n1:6 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:1 (thousands)\n\n5:3 (ten thousands)\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:5 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, let's confirm:\n\nThe number is 125,931,556:\n\n- 1 (hundred millions place: 100,000,000)\n\n2 (ten millions: 20,000,000)\n\n5 (millions:5,000,000)\n\n9 (hundred thousands:900,000)\n\n3 (ten thousands:30,000)\n\n1 (thousands:1,000)\n\n5 (hundreds:500)\n\n5 (tens:50)\n\n6 (ones:6)\n\nSo digit positions from right (ones to hundred millions):\n\nPosition 1:6 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:1 (thousands)\n\n5:3 (ten thousands)\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:5 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding digit by digit from right to left (starting at position 1):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\nFirst number:4, second:6 \u2192 4+6=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n3 +5 =8 +1 (carry) =9. Write 9, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2 +5 =7. Write7, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 (first) +1 (second) =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n9 (first) +3 (second) =12 +1 (carry)=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n0 (first) +9 (second)=9 +1 (carry)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n7 (first) +5 (second)=12 +1 (carry)=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n6 (first) +2 (second)=8 +1 (carry)=9. Write9, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n9 (first) +1 (second)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (hundred millions), but there's a carryover of 1. So we need to add a new digit to the left (billions place). So the total becomes 1 followed by the digits we've written.\n\nPutting all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have are:\n\nPosition1:0\n\n2:9\n\n3:7\n\n4:0\n\n5:3\n\n6:0\n\n7:3\n\n8:9\n\n9:0\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 in the billions place (position10).\n\nSo the result is:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, maybe I need to list them properly.\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the number step by step:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1):\n\nDigits collected from each position (after adding):\n\nPosition1:0\n\nPosition2:9\n\nPosition3:7\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:0\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 at position10 (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (positions1 to 10):\n\n0 (position1),9 (2),7 (3),0 (4),3 (5),0 (6),3 (7),9 (8),0 (9),1 (10).\n\nTo write the number normally, we reverse this:\n\nStarting from the highest place (position10) to the lowest (position1):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions)\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:7 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:9 (tens)\n\nPosition1:0 (ones)\n\nWait, no, perhaps I'm mixing up the positions. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the digits collected are:\n\nAfter each position:\n\nPositions 1-9 plus the carryover:\n\nThe digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9) plus the carryover:\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1 (ones):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):9\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):0\n\nPlus the carryover 1 in the billions place (position10).\n\nSo the full number is:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:0\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n1,099,303,790?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down the number:\n\nBillions place:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nHundred millions:0 \u2192 0\n\nTen millions:9 \u2192 90,000,000\n\nMillions:9 \u21929,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:3 \u2192300,000\n\nTen thousands:0 \u21920\n\nThousands:3 \u21923,000\n\nHundreds:7 \u2192700\n\nTens:9 \u219290\n\nOnes:0 \u21920\n\nAdding those up:\n\n1,000,000,000 + 90,000,000 = 1,090,000,000\n\n+9,000,000 \u21921,099,000,000\n\n+300,000 \u21921,099,300,000\n\n+3,000 \u21921,099,303,000\n\n+700 \u21921,099,303,700\n\n+90 \u21921,099,303,790\n\n+0 \u21921,099,303,790.\n\nWait, but let me check if that's correct. Alternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the digit positions.\n\nAlternatively, let's write the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:0\n\nSo the number is 1,099,303,790.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method. Let's add the two numbers using another approach.\n\nFirst number: 967,099,234\n\nSecond number:125,931,556\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n967,099,234 + 125,931,556\n\nBreakdown:\n\n967,099,234\n\n+125,931,556\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the millions parts:\n\n967,000,000 + 125,000,000 = 1,092,000,000\n\nThen the thousands parts:\n\n99,000 + 931,000 = 1,030,000\n\nWait, no, perhaps better to do:\n\n967,099,234 is 967 million, 99 thousand, 234.\n\n125,931,556 is 125 million, 931 thousand, 556.\n\nAdding the millions: 967 + 125 = 1,092 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 99,000 + 931,000 = 1,030,000 (which is 1 million 30 thousand).\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 234 + 556 = 790.\n\nSo total:\n\n1,092,000,000 + 1,030,000 = 1,093,030,000\n\nThen +790 \u2192 1,093,030,790.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous result of 1,099,303,790. Hmm, so there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me recalculate the breakdown:\n\nWait, 967,099,234 is:\n\n967,000,000 (millions part) + 99,234 (the rest).\n\nWait, actually, 967,099,234 is 967 million plus 99 thousand 234.\n\nWait, 967,099,234 = 967,000,000 + 99,234?\n\nNo, wait, 967,099,234 is 967 million, 99 thousand, 234. Wait, the breakdown is:\n\nThe first three digits after the first comma are millions. Let me parse it again:\n\n967,099,234:\n\n- 967 (million) \u2192 967,000,000\n\n- 099 (thousand) \u2192 99,000\n\n- 234 \u2192 234\n\nSo total: 967,000,000 + 99,000 + 234 = 967,099,234.\n\nSimilarly, 125,931,556 is:\n\n125 (million) \u2192125,000,000\n\n931 (thousand) \u2192931,000\n\n556 \u2192556\n\nTotal: 125,000,000 + 931,000 +556 = 125,931,556.\n\nAdding them:\n\nMillions: 967 +125 = 1,092 \u2192 1,092,000,000\n\nThousands: 99,000 +931,000 = 1,030,000 \u2192 which is 1,030,000\n\nThe remaining parts: 234 +556 = 790.\n\nSo total sum is:\n\n1,092,000,000 + 1,030,000 = 1,093,030,000\n\nThen add 790: 1,093,030,000 + 790 = 1,093,030,790.\n\nHmm, so this contradicts the previous digit-by-digit result of 1,099,303,790. So there must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit approach?\n\nLet me re-examine the digit positions.\n\nFirst number: 967,099,234\n\nDigits from right (position1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\nPosition2:3 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:125,931,556\n\nDigits from right (position1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:6 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:5 (millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding each position with carryover:\n\nStarting with position1 (ones):\n\n4 +6 =10 \u2192 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n3 +5 =8 +1=9 \u21929, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2 +5 =7 \u21927, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 +1 =10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n9 +3 =12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n0 +9 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n7 +5 =12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n6 +2 =8 +1=9 \u21929, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n9 +1 =10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from position9 goes to the next (billions) place, which is position10.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to 9 are:\n\n0 (position1),9 (2),7 (3),0 (4),3 (5),0 (6),3 (7),9 (8),0 (9)\n\nPlus the carryover1 at position10.\n\nSo the number is:\n\nPosition10:1, Position9:0, Position8:9, Position7:9, Position6:0, Position5:3, Position4:0, Position3:7, Position2:9, Position1:0.\n\nWait, let me list them in order from highest to lowest (position10 to position1):\n\nPosition10:1 (billions)\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:0 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:7 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:9 (tens)\n\nPosition1:0 (ones)\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nPutting this together:\n\n1,099,030,790.\n\nWait, that's different from both previous results. Wait, let's parse this:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Billions:1 \u21921,000,000,000\n\n- Hundred millions:0 \u21920\n\n- Ten millions:9 \u219290,000,000\n\n- Millions:9 \u21929,000,000\n\n- Hundred thousands:0 \u21920\n\n- Ten thousands:3 \u219230,000\n\n- Thousands:0 \u21920\n\n- Hundreds:7 \u2192700\n\n- Tens:9 \u219290\n\n- Ones:0 \u21920\n\nAdding these:\n\n1,000,000,000 +90,000,000 =1,090,000,000\n\n+9,000,000 \u21921,099,000,000\n\n+30,000 \u21921,099,030,000\n\n+700 \u21921,099,030,700\n\n+90 \u21921,099,030,790.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 1,099,030,790.\n\nWait, but in the previous breakdown method, I had 1,093,030,790. So there's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the addition directly:\n\n967,099,234\n\n+125,931,556\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with the first number: 967,099,234\n\nAdd 125,931,556:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n967,099,234\n\n+125,931,556\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+6=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTens:3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carry\n\nHundreds:2+5=7 \u21927\n\nThousands:9+1=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTen thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nHundred thousands:0+9=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nMillions:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nTen millions:6+2=8 +1=9 \u21929\n\nHundred millions:9+1=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nBillions: carry1 \u21921\n\nSo the result is 1,093,030,790? Wait, no, according to the digit-by-digit breakdown above, the digits are:\n\nPositions:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:0\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:0\n\nWait, but when I did the digit-by-digit breakdown earlier, I had:\n\nAfter adding, the digits from right to left (positions1-10):\n\n0 (position1),9 (2),7 (3),0 (4),3 (5),0 (6),3 (7),9 (8),0 (9),1 (10).\n\nSo when written from left to right (highest to lowest):\n\n1 (billions),0 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),9 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),7 (hundreds),9 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,099,030,790.\n\nBut when I did the breakdown by millions, I got 1,093,030,790. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the addition numerically:\n\n967,099,234\n\n+125,931,556\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the two numbers:\n\n967,099,234\n\n+125,931,556\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits place:4+6=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTens:3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929\n\nHundreds:2+5=7 \u21927\n\nThousands:9+1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:0+9=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nMillions:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nTen millions:6+2=8 +1=9 \u21929\n\nHundred millions:9+1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the result is 1,093,030,790? Wait, no, according to the digit positions:\n\nWait, the millions place in the first number is 7 (from 967,099,234: the millions digit is the 7 in the 7th position from the right, which is the millions place). The second number's millions digit is 5 (from 125,931,556: the millions digit is the 5 in the 7th position from the right). So adding those gives 7+5=12 plus carryover1 makes 13, so 3 with carryover1. That's correct.\n\nThen the ten millions place: first number has 6 (ten millions place), second has 2 (ten millions place). 6+2=8 plus carryover1 is 9. So ten millions digit is 9.\n\nHundred millions place: first has 9, second has1 \u21929+1=10 \u21920 with carryover1.\n\nThen the billions place is the carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:3 (from the millions place addition)\n\nWait, no, let me list all digits again properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:0\n\nTens:9\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:0\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nMillions:3 (from the millions place addition)\n\nTen millions:9\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nBillions:1\n\nWait, so the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,093,030,790.\n\nAh! Here's the confusion. In the digit-by-digit breakdown earlier, I might have 