when I got to the ten millions place, I said the first number has a 6 and the second a 9. Wait no, the ten millions digit for the first number is 3, not 6. Wait, the first number is 632,374,341. Breaking down:\n\nBillions: 6\n\nHundred millions: 3\n\nTen millions: 2? Wait no, commas are placed every three digits. Let me parse the numbers correctly.\n\nWait, 632,374,341 is:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), then 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 1 (units). Wait, no, actually, commas are placed as follows:\n\nIn the number 632,374,341:\n\n- The first comma separates the thousands, so it's 632,374,341 \u2192 632 (million), 374 (thousand), 341.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right:\n\nPosition 1 (units): 1\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition 7 (millions):2\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSimilarly, 693,628,450:\n\nPosition 1:0\n\nPosition 2:5\n\nPosition3:4\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:6\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:6\n\nSo when adding the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number:3, second:9 \u2192 3+9=12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThen hundred millions (position9):6+6 +1 (carryover) =13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1 to billions.\n\nSo the total is 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), then the rest.\n\nWait, so the digits would be:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions: (position7:2 +3 (from second number's millions digit) + any carryover?)\n\nWait let's go step by step again properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Units (position1):1+0=1\n\n2. Tens (position2):4+5=9\n\n3. Hundreds (position3):3+4=7\n\n4. Thousands (position4):4+8=12 \u2192 write 2, carryover 1\n\n5. Ten thousands (position5):7+2=9 +1=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1\n\n6. Hundred thousands (posit