nWait, let me confirm the second number: 118,468,573.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 0 (ones): 3\n\nDigit 1 (tens):7\n\nDigit 2 (hundreds):5\n\nDigit 3 (thousands):8\n\nDigit 4 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands):4\n\nDigit 6 (millions):1\n\nDigit 7 (ten millions):8\n\nDigit 8 (hundred millions):1\n\nWait, but 118,468,573 is 1 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 8 (millions), then 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown.\n\nWait, let's parse 118,468,573 properly:\n\nThe number is 118,468,573.\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n118 (million), 468 (thousand), 573.\n\nSo:\n\n- Hundred millions place: 1 (since 118 million is 1*100,000,000 + 1*10,000,000 + 8*1,000,000)\n\nWait, actually:\n\n118,468,573:\n\n- The first three digits from the left: 1 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 8 (millions)\n\nThen next three: 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands)\n\nThen 5 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSo digits from left to right (highest to lowest):\n\nDigit 8 (hundred millions): 1\n\nDigit 7 (ten millions):1\n\nDigit 6 (millions):8\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands):4\n\nDigit 4 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit 3 (thousands):8\n\nDigit 2 (hundreds):5\n\nDigit 1 (tens):7\n\nDigit 0 (ones):3\n\nTherefore, when written from right to left (position 0 to 8):\n\nPosition 0 (ones):3\n\nPosition1 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):8\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):4\n\nPosition6 (millions):8? Wait no, position6 is millions. Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, position6 (millions place) would be the 7th digit from the right. Let me count:\n\nNumber: 1 1 8 , 4 6 8 , 5 7 3\n\nPositions from right (0 to 8):\n\nPosition 0:3 (ones)\n\n1:7 (tens)\n\n2:5 (hundreds)\n\n3:8 (thousands)\n\n4:6 (ten thousands)\n\n5:4 (hundred thousands)\n\n6:8 (millions)\n\n7:1 (ten millions)\n\n8:1 (hundred millions)\n\nAh, so the millions digit is position6: 8.\n\nSo the second number's digits:\n\nPosition0:3\n\n1:7\n\n2:5\n\n3:8\n\n4:6\n\n5:4\n\n6:8\n\n7:1\n\n8:1\n\nNow, the first number's digits:\n\nFirst number: 636,656,080\n\nBreaking down similarly:\n\n6 3 6 , 6 5 6 , 0 8 0\n\nSo:\n\nPosition0 (ones):0\n\n1:8 (tens)\n\n2:0 (hundreds)\n\n3:6 (thousands)\n\n4:5 (ten thousands)\n\n5:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n6:3 (millions)\n\n7:6 (ten millions)\n\n8:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSo digits for first number:\n\nPosition0:0\n\n1:8\n\n2:0\n\n3:6\n\n4:5\n\n5:6\n\n6:3\n\n7:6\n\n8:6\n\nNow, adding each position from 0 to 8, keeping track of carry-over.\n\nStarting with position0 (ones):\n\nFirst number:0, second:3 \u2192 0+3=3. No carry. Write 3.\n\nPosition1 (tens):\n\n8 +7 =15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):\n\n0 +5 +1 (carry) =6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):\n\n6 +8 =14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):\n\n5 +6 +1 (carry) =12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):\n\n6 +4 +1 (carry) =11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\n3 +8 +1 (carry) =12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\n6 +1 +1 (carry) =8. Write 8, no carry.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\n6 +1 =7. Write 7.\n\nPutting all together from position8 to 0:\n\nPositions 8-0 digits:7,8,2,2, (wait let me list all):\n\nWait, let's list each position's result:\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition7:8\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition3:4\n\nPosition2:6\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition0:3\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest (position8 to 0):\n\n7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write them in order:\n\nStarting from position8 (hundred millions):7,\n\nposition7 (ten millions):8,\n\nposition6 (millions):2,\n\nposition5 (hundred thousands):1,\n\nposition4 (ten thousands):2,\n\nposition3 (thousands):4,\n\nposition2 (hundreds):6,\n\nposition1 (tens):5,\n\nposition0 (ones):3.\n\nSo combining these digits: 7 8 2 1 2 4 6 5 3.\n\nWait, that would be 782,124,653?\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\n- Hundred millions:7,\n\n- Ten millions:8 \u2192 780,000,000,\n\n- Millions:2 \u2192 782,000,000,\n\n- Hundred thousands:1 \u2192 782,100,000,\n\n- Ten thousands:2 \u2192 782,120,000,\n\n- Thousands:4 \u2192 782,124,000,\n\n- Hundreds:6 \u2192 782,124,600,\n\n- Tens:5 \u2192 782,124,650,\n\n- Ones:3 \u2192 782,124,653.\n\nYes, so the total is 782,124,653.\n\nWait, but let me verify the addition step by step again to ensure no mistakes.\n\nLet me re-add each digit:\n\nStarting from position0 (ones):\n\n0 +3 =3. Correct.\n\nPosition1:8+7=15 \u2192 5 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition2:0+5 +1=6. Correct.\n\nPosition3:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition4:5+6 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition5:6+4 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition6:3+8 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition7:6 (from first number) +1 (from second number) +1 (carryover) =8. Correct.\n\nPosition8:6 +1 =7. Correct.\n\nSo the digits are indeed 7,8,2,1,2,4,6,5,3 \u2192 782,124,653.\n\nTherefore, the sum should be 782,124,653.\n\nWait, but let me cross-verify using another method, perhaps adding the numbers as whole:\n\n636,656,080\n\n+118,468,573\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the millions:\n\n636,656,080 is 636 million plus 656 thousand and 80.\n\n118,468,573 is 118 million plus 468 thousand and 573.\n\nAdding the millions: 636 + 118 = 754 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 656 + 468 = 1,124 thousand.\n\nAdding the remaining: 80 +573 = 653.\n\nSo total would be 754,000,000 + 1,124,000 + 653 = 755,124,653? Wait, that's conflicting with the previous result.\n\nWait, hold on, this discrepancy means I made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let me recalculate:\n\nWait, 636,656,080 + 118,468,573.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n636,656,080\n\n+118,468,573\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 +3 =3\n\nTens:8+7=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nHundreds:0+5 +1=6\n\nThousands:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:5+6 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:6+4 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:3 (from first number's millions digit) +8 (second number's millions digit) +1 (carryover) =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nTen millions:6 (first number's ten millions) +1 (second's ten millions) +1 (carryover) =8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:6 +1 =7.\n\nSo the digits are 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones). So 782,124,653.\n\nBut when I did the breakdown into millions, thousands, etc., I got a different result. Let me check that again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown method.\n\nFirst number: 636,656,080.\n\nThis is 636 million, 656 thousand, and 80.\n\nSecond number:118,468,573 is 118 million, 468 thousand, 573.\n\nAdding the millions: 636 + 118 = 754 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 656 + 468 = 1,124 thousand. Wait, 656,000 + 468,000 = 1,124,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 80 +573 = 653.\n\nSo total is 754,000,000 + 1,124,000 = 755,124,000, plus 653 gives 755,124,653.\n\nWait, but according to digit-wise addition, it's 782,124,653. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, this is concerning. Let me check with another approach.\n\nLet me write both numbers numerically:\n\nFirst number: 636,656,080\n\nSecond number: 118,468,573\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n636,656,080\n\n+118,468,573\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add column by column from right:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n0 +3 =3\n\nNext (tens):8+7=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nHundreds:0+5 +1=6\n\nThousands:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:5+6 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:6+4 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:3 (from first number's millions digit) +8 (second's millions digit) +1 (carryover) =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nTen millions:6 (first's ten millions digit) +1 (second's ten millions digit) +1 (carryover) =8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:6 (first's hundred millions) +1 (second's hundred millions) =7.\n\nSo the result is 782,124,653.\n\nWait, but when I broke it down into millions, I got 755 million something. That must be an error in the breakdown.\n\nWait, let me check the breakdown again:\n\nFirst number: 636,656,080 is 636 million plus 656 thousand plus 80.\n\nSecond number:118,468,573 is 118 million plus 468 thousand plus 573.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 636 + 118 = 754 million.\n\nAdding the thousands parts: 656,000 + 468,000 = 1,124,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 80 +573 = 653.\n\nTotal: 754,000,000 + 1,124,000 = 755,124,000 + 653 = 755,124,653.\n\nWait, but according to digit-wise addition, it's 782,124,653. There's a difference of 27 million. Where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second, I think I messed up the breakdown. Let me check the first number again.\n\nWait, the first number is 636,656,080.\n\nBreaking into millions:\n\nThe number is 636,656,080.\n\nThe first three digits from the left are 636, which is the millions part? Wait no, actually, in the standard breakdown:\n\nThe number is 636,656,080.\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n- 636 (million), then 656 (thousand), then 080.\n\nWait, but 636,656,080 is actually 636 million, 656 thousand, and 80. So that's correct.\n\nSecond number:118,468,573 is 118 million, 468 thousand, 573. Correct.\n\nAdding the millions: 636 +118 = 754 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 656 +468 = 1,124 thousand. So that's 1,124,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 80 +573 = 653.\n\nTotal: 754,000,000 + 1,124,000 = 755,124,000 + 653 = 755,124,653.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, it's 782,124,653. There's a discrepancy of 27 million. So one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, let me do the digit-wise addition again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 636,656,080\n\nDigits from right (position0 to 8):\n\nPosition0:0\n\n1:8\n\n2:0\n\n3:6\n\n4:5\n\n5:6\n\n6:3\n\n7:6\n\n8:6\n\nSecond number:118,468,573\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition0:3\n\n1:7\n\n2:5\n\n3:8\n\n4:6\n\n5:4\n\n6:8\n\n7:1\n\n8:1\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition0:0+3=3\n\nPosition1:8+7=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nPosition2:0+5 +1=6\n\nPosition3:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nPosition4:5+6 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition5:6+4 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition6:3 (first's millions digit) +8 (second's millions digit) +1 (carryover) =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition7:6 (first's ten millions digit) +1 (second's ten millions digit) +1 (carryover) =8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition8:6 (first's hundred millions) +1 (second's hundred millions) =7.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition8:7,\n\nPosition7:8,\n\nPosition6:2,\n\nPosition5:1,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition2:6,\n\nPosition1:5,\n\nPosition0:3.\n\nSo the number is 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nWhich is 782,124,653.\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown method, it's 755,124,653. So which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown method's millions addition.\n\nWait, first number is 636,656,080. The \"636\" is in the millions place? Wait no, actually, the first three digits (636) are in the hundred millions, ten millions, and millions places. Wait, let's parse the first number again:\n\n636,656,080:\n\n- The first digit (from left) is 6, which is the hundred millions place (since it's a 9-digit number).\n\n- Second digit:3 (ten millions)\n\n- Third digit:6 (millions)\n\n- Fourth digit:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n- Fifth digit:5 (ten thousands)\n\n- Sixth digit:6 (thousands)\n\n- Seventh digit:0 (hundreds)\n\n- Eighth digit:8 (tens)\n\n- Ninth digit:0 (ones)\n\nWait, so the millions part is the third digit (6) representing 6 million, plus the following digits. Wait, perhaps the breakdown into millions is not straightforward.\n\nWait, the number 636,656,080 can be written as:\n\n600,000,000 (hundred millions) +\n\n30,000,000 (ten millions) +\n\n6,000,000 (millions) +\n\n600,000 (hundred thousands) +\n\n50,000 (ten thousands) +\n\n6,000 (thousands) +\n\n0 (hundreds) +\n\n80 (tens and ones).\n\nSo total millions part is 600,000,000 +30,000,000 +6,000,000 = 636,000,000.\n\nThen the rest is 656,080.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 118,468,573 is:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions) +\n\n10,000,000 (ten millions) +\n\n8,000,000 (millions) +\n\n400,000 (hundred thousands) +\n\n60,000 (ten thousands) +\n\n8,000 (thousands) +\n\n500 (hundreds) +\n\n70 (tens) +\n\n3 (ones).\n\nSo the millions part is 100,000,000 +10,000,000 +8,000,000 = 118,000,000.\n\nThe rest is 468,573.\n\nTherefore, adding the millions parts:\n\n636,000,000 +118,000,000 = 754,000,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n656,080 +468,573 = ?\n\nLet's compute that:\n\n656,080 +468,573:\n\nAdding units:0+3=3\n\nTens:8+7=15 \u21925 carryover1\n\nHundreds:0+5 +1=6\n\nThousands:6+8=14 \u21924 carryover1\n\nTen thousands:5+6 +1=12 \u21922 carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:6+4 +1=11 \u21921 carryover1\n\nMillions:0 (since 656,080 has no million part beyond the initial 636 million) plus 0 (from 468,573) plus carryover1 \u21921.\n\nWait, but 656,080 is part of the first number's total, which is already accounted for in the millions. Wait, perhaps I need to add 656,080 and 468,573 directly:\n\n656,080\n\n+468,573\n\n= (600,000 +56,080) + (400,000 +68,573)\n\n= 1,000,000 + (56,080 +68,573)\n\n56,080 +68,573 = 124,653\n\nSo total is 1,000,000 +124,653 =1,124,653.\n\nTherefore, total sum is 754,000,000 +1,124,653 =755,124,653.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the digit-wise addition which gave 782,124,653. There's a problem here.\n\nWait, now I'm confused. Let me do the digit-wise addition again step by step, carefully.\n\nFirst number:636,656,080\n\nSecond number:118,468,573\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n        636,656,080\n      + 118,468,573\n      -------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place:0 +3 =3\n\nTens:8 +7 =15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nHundreds:0 +5 +1 =6\n\nThousands:6 +8 =14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:5 +6 +1 =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:6 +4 +1 =11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:3 (from first number's millions digit) +8 (second's millions digit) +1 (carryover) =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nTen millions:6 (first's ten millions digit) +1 (second's ten millions digit) +1 (carryover) =8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:6 (first's hundred millions) +1 (second's hundred millions) =7.\n\nSo the result is 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nWhich is 782,124,653.\n\nBut according to the breakdown method, it's 755,124,653. There's a discrepancy of 27 million. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown method's millions addition.\n\nWait, first number is 636,656,080. The \"636\" in the millions part?\n\nWait, no. Let me parse the first number again:\n\n636,656,080 is:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions)\n\n- 3 (ten millions)\n\n- 6 (millions)\n\n- 6 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n\n- 6 (thousands)\n\n- 0 (hundreds)\n\n- 8 (tens)\n\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSo the millions part is 6 (millions) plus the higher digits. Wait, the total millions part is 636 million (from the first three digits: 6,3,6 \u2192 636 million). So that's correct.\n\nSecond number is 118,468,573:\n\n- 1 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 (ten millions)\n\n- 8 (millions)\n\n- 4 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 6 (ten thousands)\n\n- 8 (thousands)\n\n- 5 (hundreds)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 3 (ones)\n\nSo the millions part is 118 million (from first three digits:1,1,8 \u2192 118 million). Correct.\n\nAdding the millions:636 +118 =754 million. Correct.\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\nFirst number's remaining:656