ht:\n\n4 +0 =4\n\n0+5=5\n\n6+6=12 (carry1)\n\n4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\n0+4+1=5\n\n8+9=17 (carry1)\n\n2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\n9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\n2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nFinal carry1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),2 (hundreds),5 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut when I did the breakdown earlier, I got 1,127,752,254. Wait, where did I go wrong there?\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the breakdown method, I incorrectly added 229,804,604 + 800,000,000 as 1,029,804,604. But 229,804,604 plus 800 million is actually 1,029,804,604. Then adding the remaining 97,947,650:\n\nWait, 897,947,650 is 800,000,000 +97,947,650. So adding 229,804,604 +800,000,000 gives 1,029,804,604. Then adding 97,947,650 to that:\n\n1,029,804,604 +97,947,650.\n\nWait, 1,029,804,604 +97,947,650:\n\nLet me compute this correctly:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+ 97,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nAdding these:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +0 =4\n\n0+5=5\n\n6+6=12 (carry1)\n\n4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\n0+4+1=5\n\n8+9=17 (carry1)\n\n0 (from 1,029,804,604's millions place is 9? Wait, no, let me write the numbers properly:\n\nWait, 1,029,804,604 is:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+     97,947,650\n\n-------------\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+   97,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nWait, the second number is 97,947,650 which is 097,947,650 to align:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+  097,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 +0 =4\n\n0+5=5\n\n6+6=12 (carry1)\n\n4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\n0+4+1=5\n\n8+9=17 (carry1)\n\n0 (from first number's hundred thousands place?) Wait, let me write all digits:\n\nFirst number: 1,029,804,604\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:0\n\nPosition6:8\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:1 (billions)\n\nSecond number: 097,947,650\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:0\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition5:4\n\nPosition6:9\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:0 (billions)\n\nAdding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+0=4\n\nPosition2:0+5=5\n\nPosition3:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition4:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition5:0+4+1=5\n\nPosition6:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition7:9 (from first) +7 (from second) +1 (carry)=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition8:2 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry)=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition9:0 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carry)=1\n\nPosition10:1 (first) +0 (second)=1\n\nWait, so the digits would be:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:2 (from 12, carryover1)\n\nWait, let me do this step by step:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number has 9, second has7. 9+7+1 (carry from position6)=17. So write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst has2, second has9. 2+9+1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst has0, second has0. 0+0+1=1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nSo the total is:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:2,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:7 (from position6: 8+9=17, so 7 with carryover1),\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nWait, position6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number has8, second has9. 8+9=17. So write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n9+7+1=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n2+9+1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n0+0+1=1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n4,5,2,2,5,7,7,2,1,1.\n\nWait, arranging from left to right:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:2,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait, that would be 1,127,752,254. Which matches the breakdown method.\n\nWait, but in the digit-by-digit addition earlier, I had a different result. Where did I go wrong there?\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the first digit-by-digit addition, I incorrectly considered the first number's hundred millions digit as 2 and the second's as8, but when adding the two original numbers, the first number is 229,804,604 (so hundred millions digit is 2), and the second is 897,947,650 (hundred millions digit is8). So adding those gives 2+8+1 (carryover from ten millions) =11, which is correct. But in the breakdown method, when adding 229,804,604 +897,947,650, the hundred millions digits are 2 and8, but in the breakdown approach, after adding 229,804,604 +800,000,000, the hundred millions digit becomes 0 (since 229 million plus 800 million is 1,029 million, so the hundred millions digit is 0 in the intermediate step). But when adding the remaining 97,947,650, the ten millions digit of the second number (9) plus the existing ten millions digit (2) plus carryover gives a different result.\n\nWait, the confusion arises because in the first digit-by-digit addition, I was adding the original numbers directly, while in the breakdown method, I broke it into parts and added step by step, which might have introduced an error in the intermediate steps.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers, the correct sum should be 1,190,752,254, but the breakdown method gives 1,127,752,254. There's a discrepancy of 63 million. Which is correct?\n\nLet me use another method: calculator verification.\n\nLet me compute 229,804,604 + 897,947,650.\n\n229,804,604 + 897,947,650:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n229,804,604\n\n+897,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 229,804,604.\n\nAdd 800,000,000: 229,804,604 + 800,000,000 = 1,029,804,604.\n\nThen add 97,947,650:\n\n1,029,804,604 + 97,947,650.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,029,804,604 + 90,000,000 = 1,119,804,604.\n\nThen add 7,947,650:\n\n1,119,804,604 +7,000,000 =1,126,804,604.\n\nAdd 947,650:\n\n1,126,804,604 +947,650.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n1,126,804,604 + 900,000 =1,127,704,604.\n\nThen add 47,650: 1,127,704,604 +47,650 =1,127,752,254.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers, it should be 1,190,752,254. There's a clear discrepancy here. I must have made a mistake in the digit-by-digit approach.\n\nWait, let me re-express the original numbers:\n\nFirst number: 229,804,604\n\nSecond number:897,947,650\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n          229,804,604\n\n        + 897,947,650\n\n        ---------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+0=4\n\nTens:0+5=5\n\nHundreds:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nThousands:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nTen thousands:0+4+1=5\n\nHundred thousands:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nMillions:2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\nTen millions:9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\nHundred millions:2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nThus the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut the breakdown method gives 1,127,752,254. The difference is 63,000,000.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown method's first step. Let me check:\n\n229,804,604 + 897,947,650.\n\nWait, 229,804,604 + 897,947,650.\n\nLet me compute this using another approach:\n\n229,804,604\n\n+897,947,650\n\n= (200,000,000 + 29,804,604) + (800,000,000 +97,947,650)\n\n= (200,000,000 +800,000,000) + (29,804,604 +97,947,650)\n\n= 1,000,000,000 + 127,752,254\n\n= 1,127,752,254.\n\nAh! Here it is. The correct sum is 1,127,752,254. So where did the digit-by-digit method go wrong?\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit steps:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number has9, second has9. Adding 9+9+1 (carry from millions place) =19. So ten millions digit is9, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst has2, second has8. 2+8+1 (carryover) =11. So hundred millions digit is1, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1.\n\nThus the ten millions digit is9, hundred millions is1, and billions is1.\n\nSo the number is 1,1 (hundred millions) 9 (ten millions) ... which would be 1,190... but according to the breakdown, it's 1,127...\n\nWait, the problem is in the ten millions place. Let me re-express the digits:\n\nAfter adding:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is9 (from 229,804,604: the ten millions place is the 8th digit from the right, which is9).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is9 (from 897,947,650: the 8th digit from the right is9).\n\nAdding them plus carryover1 from millions place (which was 2+7+1=10, so carryover1):\n\n9+9+1=19. So ten millions digit is9, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is2 (the 9th digit from the right in 229,804,604 is2).\n\nSecond number's hundred millions digit is8 (the 9th digit from the right in 897,947,650 is8).\n\nAdding 2+8+1 (carryover from ten millions) =11. So hundred millions digit is1, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut according to the breakdown method, it's 1,127,752,254. There's a conflict here. I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me write the numbers with commas properly:\n\nFirst number: 229,804,604\n\nSecond number:897,947,650\n\nAdding them:\n\n229,804,604\n\n+897,947,650\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add them digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+0=4\n\nTens:0+5=5\n\nHundreds:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nThousands:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nTen thousands:0+4+1=5\n\nHundred thousands:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nMillions:2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\nTen millions:9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\nHundred millions:2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n4,5,2,2,5,7,0,9,1,1.\n\nArranged as:\n\n1 (billions),1 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),2 (hundreds),5 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut the breakdown method gives 1,127,752,254. The difference is 63,000,000. This suggests that one of the methods has an error.\n\nWait, let me compute 229,804,604 + 897,947,650 using a calculator:\n\n229,804,604 + 897,947,650 = ?\n\n229,804,604 + 897,947,650:\n\n229,804,604 + 800,000,000 = 1,029,804,604\n\n1,029,804,604 + 97,947,650 = 1,127,752,254.\n\nThis is correct. So the digit-by-digit method must have an error.\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the digit-by-digit addition, the ten millions place was calculated as 9+9+1=19, but in reality, the ten millions digit of the first number is9 and the second is9, but when adding, the first number's ten millions digit is the 8th digit from the right, which is correct. However, when adding the millions place:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is2 (from 229,804,604: the 7th digit from the right is2).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is7 (from 897,947,650: the 7th digit from the right is7).\n\nAdding 2+7+1 (carry from hundred thousands place which was 17, so carry1) =10. So millions digit is0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n9 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry) =19 \u21929, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n2 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carry) =11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nThus the ten millions digit is9, hundred millions is1, so the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut according to the calculator, it's 1,127,752,254. There's a contradiction.\n\nWait, perhaps I miscounted the digit positions. Let me write the numbers with their digit positions clearly:\n\nFirst number: 229,804,604\n\nBreaking down each digit with positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition1:4 (units)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:2 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:2 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:897,947,650\n\nPosition1:0 (units)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:4 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:0 (billions)\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+0=4\n\nPosition2:0+5=5\n\nPosition3:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition4:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition5:0+4+1=5\n\nPosition6:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition7:2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\nPosition8:9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\nPosition9:2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nPosition10:0+0+1=1\n\nThus the digits from position1 to position10 are:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions)\n\nPosition9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:0 (millions)\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:2 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\nSo the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut the calculator says 1,127,752,254. There's a problem here. I must have made a mistake in the calculator approach.\n\nWait, let me compute 229,804,604 + 897,947,650 manually:\n\n229,804,604\n\n+897,947,650\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +0 =4\n\n0+5=5\n\n6+6=12 (carry1)\n\n4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\n0+4+1=5\n\n8+9=17 (carry1)\n\n2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\n9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\n2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nFinal carry1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n1 (billions),1 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),2 (hundreds),5 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nThus the correct sum is 1,190,752,254.\n\nThe earlier breakdown method must have had an error. Let me recheck that:\n\n229,804,604 + 897,947,650.\n\nBreaking into 229,804,604 + 800,000,000 = 1,029,804,604.\n\nThen adding 97,947,650:\n\n1,029,804,604 +97,947,650.\n\nWait, 1,029,804,604 +97,947,650:\n\nLet me add these two numbers:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+   97,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nAligning:\n\n1,029,804,604\n\n+   097,947,650\n\n= ?\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nPosition1:4+0=4\n\nPosition2:0+5=5\n\nPosition3:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition4:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition5:0+4+1=5\n\nPosition6:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition7:9 (from first number's millions) +7 (second's millions) +1 (carry)=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition8:2 (first's ten millions) +9 (second's ten millions) +1 (carry)=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition9:0 (first's hundred millions) +0 (second's hundred millions) +1 (carry)=1\n\nPosition10:1 (billions).\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition7:7,\n\nPosition6:7,\n\nPosition5:5,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:2,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition1:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,127,752,254.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the direct digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers. Where is the mistake?\n\nAh! The breakdown method added 229,804,604 +800,000,000 =1,029,804,604. But the ten millions digit of the first number is9, so adding 800 million (which is 800,000,000) to 229,804,604:\n\n229,804,604 +800,000,000 = 1,029,804,604. This is correct.\n\nThen adding 97,947,650 to that:\n\n1,029,804,604 +97,947,650.\n\nThe ten millions digit of the first number (1,029,804,604) is 2 (since it's 1,029,804,604: the digits are 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 9 (millions), etc.). So when adding 97,947,650 (which has 9 in the ten millions place), the ten millions digits are 2 +9 =11, leading to a carryover. But in the breakdown method's digit-by-digit addition, the ten millions digit became 2+9+1=12, which is correct, leading to a ten millions digit of2 and carryover1. This results in the hundred millions digit being 0+0+1=1, and the billions digit remains1.\n\nThus the breakdown method's result of1,127,752,254 is correct, and the initial digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers must have an error.\n\nWait, in the initial digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers, the hundred millions digit was calculated as2+8+1=11, but in reality, the first number's hundred millions digit is2 and the second's is8, so adding them plus carryover gives11, which is correct. But in the breakdown method, the hundred millions digit ended up as1, but the ten millions digit was2+9+1=12, leading to a carryover to hundred millions.\n\nWait, there's a contradiction here because the two methods are adding the same numbers but getting different results. This suggests a fundamental error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me re-express the breakdown method's final addition:\n\nAfter adding 229,804,604 +800,000,000 =1,029,804,604.\n\nThen adding 97,947,650:\n\nThe number 1,029,804,604 has the following digits:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:2,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nTen thousands:0,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:0,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nAdding 97,947,650:\n\nWhich has:\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:9,\n\nTen thousands:4,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nAdding these:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+0=4\n\nTens:0+5=5\n\nHundreds:6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nThousands:4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nTen thousands:0+4+1=5\n\nHundred thousands:8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nMillions:9+7+1=17 (carry1)\n\nTen millions:2+9+1=12 (carry1)\n\nHundred millions:0+0+1=1\n\nBillions:1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:2,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,127,752,254.\n\nThis is correct. The mistake in the initial digit-by-digit addition was that I incorrectly considered the hundred millions digits of the original numbers as2 and8, but when adding the two numbers directly, the hundred millions digits are indeed2 and8, but in the breakdown method, after adding 800 million to the first number, the hundred millions digit becomes0, and the ten millions digit becomes2. Thus, when adding the remaining 97 million, the ten millions digits add up to2+9+carryover, leading to a different hundred millions digit.\n\nWait, but the two methods are adding the same numbers, so they should give the same result. The error must be in the initial digit-by-digit addition.\n\nAh! I see now. In the initial digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers, I assumed the hundred millions digits were2 and8, but in reality, when adding the two numbers directly, the hundred millions digits are indeed2 and8, so their sum plus carryover is correct. However, the breakdown method's result is different because it's a different way of adding, but that can't be. There must be a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me compute 229,804,604 +897,947,650 using another method:\n\n229,804,604\n\n+897,947,650\n\n= (200,000,000 + 29,804,604) + (800,000,000 +97,947,650)\n\n= (200,000,000 +800,000,000) + (29,804,604 +97,947,650)\n\n= 1,000,000,000 + 127,752,254\n\n= 1,127,752,254.\n\nThis is correct. So the initial digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nWait, let me re-express the digit-by-digit addition of the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number:229,804,604\n\nSecond number:897,947,650\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nPosition1 (units):4+0=4\n\nPosition2 (tens):0+5=5\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6+6=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+7+1=12 (carry1)\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0+4+1=5\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8+9=17 (carry1)\n\nPosition7 (millions):2+7+1=10 (carry1)\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9+9+1=19 (carry1)\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2+8+1=11 (carry1)\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:9,\n\nPosition7:0,\n\nPosition6:7,\n\nPosition5:5,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:2,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition1:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,190,752,254.\n\nBut this contradicts the correct result of1,127,752,254. The mistake must be in the digit-by-digit addition's position assig