hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones). \n\nThe second number is 744,089,601, which is also 9 digits: 7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). \n\nSo when adding, the billions place would actually be the first digit. Wait, 591 million is less than a billion, so the first digit is hundred millions. So when adding, the highest place here is hundred millions. Let me recheck each step carefully.\n\nStarting over step by step:\n\nOnes place: 6 + 1 = 7. Correct.\n\nTens: 7 + 0 =7.\n\nHundreds: 3 +6=9.\n\nThousands: 1 (from first number) +9 (second number's thousands place?) Wait, let me write down each digit's place properly.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down each number digit by digit would help. Let's list each digit from right to left (starting at ones):\n\nFirst number: 591,931,376\n\nDigits (right to left):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6\n\n2 (tens):7\n\n3 (hundreds):3\n\n4 (thousands):1\n\n5 (ten thousands):3\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9\n\n7 (millions):1\n\n8 (ten millions):9\n\n9 (hundred millions):5\n\nSecond number:744,089,601\n\nDigits (right to left):\n\n1:1\n\n2:0\n\n3:6\n\n4:9\n\n5:8\n\n6:0\n\n7:4\n\n8:4\n\n9:7\n\nWait, that might be confusing. Alternatively, writing each number's digits in order from left to right:\n\nFirst number: 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSecond number:7 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),4 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),6 (hundreds),0 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nHundred millions: 5 +7 =12\n\nTen millions:9 +4=13\n\nMillions:1 +4=5\n\nHundred thousands:9 +0=9\n\nTen thousands:3 +8=11\n\nThousands:1 +9=10\n\nHundreds:3 +6=9\n\nTens:7 +0=7\n\nOnes:6 +1=7\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to process from the right (ones) to the left, carrying over as needed. Let me do that again properly.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nOnes place: 6 +1 =7. Write down 7. No carryover.\n\nTens place:7 (from first number) +0 (second)=7. Write 7. No carry.\n\nHundreds:3 +6=9. Write 9. No carry.\n\nThousands:1 (first) +9 (second)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:3 (first) +8 (second)=11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:9 (first) +0 (second)=9 +1 (carry)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions:1 (first) +4 (second)=5 +1 (carry)=6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nTen millions:9 (first) +4 (second)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions:5 (first) +7 (second)=12 +1 (carry)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest place (hundred millions), but there's a carryover of 1. That means we need to add a new digit to the left, which is the billions place. So the total becomes 1 (billion) 3 (hundred millions)... etc.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum is 1,336,020,977?\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the digits step by step with the carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7\n\nTens:7\n\nHundreds:9\n\nThousands:0 (with carry 1)\n\nTen thousands:2 (with carry 1)\n\nHundred thousands:0 (with carry 1)\n\nMillions:6\n\nTen millions:3 (with carry 1)\n\nHundred millions:3 (from 12 +1 carryover? Wait, let me track again.\n\nWait, let's go step by step with carries:\n\n1. Ones: 6+1=7 \u2192 7, carry 0\n\n2. Tens:7+0=7 \u21927, carry 0\n\n3. Hundreds:3+6=9 \u21929, carry 0\n\n4. Thousands:1+9=10 \u21920, carry 1\n\n5. Ten thousands:3+8=11 +1=12 \u21922, carry 1\n\n6. Hundred thousands:9+0=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry 1\n\n7. Millions:1+4=5 +1=6 \u21926, carry 0\n\n8. Ten millions:9+4=13 \u21923, carry 1\n\n9. Hundred millions:5+7=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry 1\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we have a carryover of 1. Since there are no more digits, this becomes a new digit to the left (billions place). 