(hundred millions place? Wait no, position9 is the hundred millions digit. So the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:9 (from 6+3=9)\n\nTen millions:7 (from first number's 7) +1 (second's 1) =8? Wait, position8 is ten millions. Let me re-express the final digits properly.\n\nWait, the digits in each position:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 6 +3 =9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7 +1=8\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 +9=9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +1=2\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0 +1 +1 (carryover?) Wait no, position5 was ten thousands, which was 0+1 + the carryover from position4? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait in position4 (thousands place):\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is9, second's is4. 9+4=13, plus the carryover from position3 (which was 1). Wait, no, the carryover from position3 (hundreds) was 1, so 9+4 +1? Wait no, the carryover from position3 is added to position4.\n\nWait let me go step by step again carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n7 +4 =11 \u2192 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n5 +9 =14 +1 (carryover) =15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n8 +8 =16 +1 (carryover) =17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 (first) +4 (second) =13 +1 (carryover) =14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n0 (first) +1 (second) =1 +1 (carryover) =2 \u21922, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n1 (first) +1 (second) =2 \u21922, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n0 (first) +9 (second) =9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n7 (first) +1 (second) =8 \u21928, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6 (first) +3 (second) =9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position9 (right to left) are:\n\n1 (ones),5 (tens),7 (hundreds),4 (thousands),2 (ten thousands),2 (hundred thousands),9 (millions),8 (ten millions),9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo writing the number from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n\n9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),9 