sition3):9\n\nTens (position2):7\n\nOnes (position1):8\n\nWait, that would be 1,013,341,978? Wait, let me see:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nThen hundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:1 (from position8 which was 11, so the 1 in that position is ten millions place?)\n\nWait, maybe I need to structure the digits properly. Let me think of the total as:\n\nStarting from the first digit (billions):\n\n1 (from the carryover) followed by 0 (hundred millions), then the ten millions place was 1 (from 7+3+1=11?), then millions place 3, hundred thousands 3, ten thousands 4, thousands 1, hundreds 9, tens 7, ones 8.\n\nSo the number is 1,022,341,978? Wait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me try adding them numerically without commas:\n\nFirst number: 878962317\n\nSecond number:143379661\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 +1 =8\n\n1+6=7\n\n3+6=9\n\n2+9=11 (carryover 1)\n\n6+7 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\n9+3 +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\n8+4 +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\n7+3 +1=11 (1, carryover1)\n\n8+1 +1=10 (0, carryover1)\n\nFinally, the carryover1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n8,7,9,1 (from thousands place), then 4,3,3,1 (from ten millions?), then 0, and the leading 1.\n\nSo writing the number as:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions?), 3 (millions?), 3 (hundred thousands?), 4 (ten thousands?), 1 (thousands?), 9 (hundreds?), 7 (tens?), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,013,341,978? Hmm, but let me check with actual addition:\n\nLet me compute 878,962,317 + 143,379,661.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n878,962,317\n\n+143,379,661\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 878,962,317 + 100,000,000 = 978,962,317\n\nThen add 43,379,661:\n\n978,962,317 + 40,000,000 = 1,018,962,317\n\nThen add 3,379,661:\n\n1,018,962,317 + 3,000,000 = 1,021,962,317\n\nThen add 379,661:\n\n1,021,962,317 + 300,000 = 1,022,262,317\n\nThen 79,661:\n\n1,022,262,317 + 70,000 = 1,022,332,317\n\nThen 9,661:\n\n1,022,332,317 + 9,0