317 is:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 7 (ones). So the digits are:\n\nPosition1 (units):7\n\nPosition2 (tens):1\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):8\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nPosition10 (billions):0? Wait, no, the first digit is 8 in the hundred millions place, so the number is 878,962,317 which is 8 hundred million plus 78 million, etc. So the billions digit is 0, but the hundred millions is 8.\n\nThe second number is 143,379,661:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (ten millions?), wait:\n\n143,379,661 is 1 (hundred millions?), 4 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, commas are placed as 143,379,661:\n\nSo 1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). So digits:\n\nPosition1 (units):1\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1\n\nPosition10 (billions):0\n\nSo when adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from position1 (units):\n\nFirst number:7, second:1 \u2192 total 8.\n\nPosition2 (tens):1 +6=7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3 +6=9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2 +9=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 +7 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9 +3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8 +3 +1=12 \u21922? Wait wait, 8 (from first number's millions place) plus 3 (second's millions) plus carryover1 \u2192 8+3=11 +1=12? Wait, 8+3 is 11, plus 1 is 12. So 2 here, carryover1.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake here ea