r method. Maybe adding the numbers using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, adding 996,931,287 + 379,598,925.\n\nLet me try adding them in parts:\n\nFirst, add the millions:\n\n996,931,287 is 996,931 thousand 287.\n\n379,598,925 is 379,598 thousand 925.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking into larger chunks:\n\nAlternatively, 996,931,287 + 379,598,925.\n\nLet me add the two numbers step by step:\n\nStart with 996,931,287\n\nAdd 300,000,000 \u2192 1,296,931,287\n\nThen add 79,598,925:\n\nAdding 70,000,000 \u2192 1,366,931,287\n\nThen 9,598,925:\n\nAdding 9,000,000 \u2192 1,375,931,287\n\nThen 598,925:\n\nAdding 500,000 \u2192 1,376,431,287\n\nThen 98,925:\n\nAdding 90,000 \u2192 1,376,521,287\n\nThen 8,925:\n\nAdding 8,000 \u2192 1,376,529,287\n\nThen 925 \u2192 1,376,530,212?\n\nHmm, this seems inconsistent with the previous result. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using calculator-like addition:\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n996,931,287\n\n+379,598,925\n\n-------------\n\nAdding digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7+5=12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nTens:8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThousands:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (ones),1 (tens),2 (hundreds),0 (thousands),9 (ten thousands),8 (hundred thousands),4 (millions),3 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions), and the final 1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,334,890,212.\n\nWait, but when I tried adding in parts earlier, I ended up with 1,376,530,212 which is different. Hmm, so I must have messed up that method. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps the first method is correct. Let me confirm with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 996,931,287 + 379,598,925.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n7 +5 =12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nTens:8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThousands:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the total is 1,334,890,212.\n\nWait, so that's the correct answer. The previous method where I added in parts must have had an error. Let me see where I went wrong there.\n\nOriginal first number: 996,931,287\n\nAdding 300,000,000 gives 1,296,931,287. Correct.\n\nThen adding 79,598,925:\n\n79,598,925 is 70,000,000 +9,598,925.\n\nAdding 70,000,000 to 1,296,931,287 gives 1,366,931,287. Correct.\n\nThen adding 9,598,925:\n\nAdding 9,000,000 \u2192 1,375,931,287. Then adding 598,925:\n\n598,925 is 500,000 +98,925.\n\nAdding 500,000 \u2192 1,376,431,287.\n\nAdding 98,925: 98,925 is 90,000 +8,925.\n\nAdding 90,000 \u2192 1,376,521,287.\n\nAdding 8,925: 8,000 +925.\n\nAdding 8,000 \u2192 1,376,529,287.\n\nAdding 925 \u2192 1,376,530,212.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, the correct total is 1,334,890,212. There's a discrepancy here. So I must have made a mistake in the part addition.\n\nAh! Wait a second, the second number is 379,598,925. When I broke it down into 300,000,000 +79,598,925, that's correct. But when I added 300 million to the first number, which is 996,931,287, that gives 1,296,931,287. Then adding 79,598,925:\n\nWait, 79,598,925 is 70,000,000 +9,598,925. So adding 70 million to 1,296,931,287 gives 1,366,931,287. Then adding 9,598,925:\n\nWait, 9,598,925 is 9,000,000 +598,925. Adding 9 million gives 1,375,931,287. Then adding 598,925:\n\nWait, 598,925 is 500,000 +98,925. Adding 500,000 gives 1,376,431,287. Then adding 98,925:\n\n98,925 is 90,000 +8,925. Adding 90,000 gives 1,376,521,287. Then adding 8,925:\n\n8,925 is 8,000 +925. Adding 8,000 gives 1,376,529,287. Then adding 925 gives 1,376,530,212.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the correct total is 1,334,890,212. There's a difference of 41,640,000 between the two results. That's a big discrepancy. So I must have messed up the part addition approach.\n\nWait, let me check the first step again. The first number is 996,931,287. Adding 300,000,000 gives 1,296,931,287. That's correct.\n\nThen adding 79,598,925 to that. Wait, 79,598,925 is the remaining part of the second number (since 379,598,925 = 300,000,000 +79,598,925). So adding 79,598,925 to 1,296,931,287:\n\nLet me compute 1,296,931,287 +79,598,925.\n\nAdding 70,000,000 first: 1,296,931,287 +70,000,000 = 1,366,931,287. Correct.\n\nThen adding 9,598,925:\n\n1,366,931,287 +9,598,925.\n\nLet me compute 1,366,931,287 +9,000,000 =1,375,931,287.\n\nThen add 598,925: 1,375,931,287 +598,925.\n\nAdding 500,000: 1,376,431,287.\n\nThen 98,925: 1,376,431,287 +98,925 = 1,376,530,212.\n\nWait, but that's the same as before. But according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,334,890,212. So there's a mistake here. Wait, perhaps I added the wrong numbers?\n\nWait, the second number is 379,598,925. Let me confirm that 300,000,000 +79,598,925 equals 379,598,925. Yes, that's correct.\n\nWait, but the first number is 996,931,287. Adding 379,598,925 to it should give the same as adding 300 million plus 79 million etc. But according to digit-by-digit, the total is 1,334,890,212, but the part addition gives 1,376,530,212. The difference is 1,376,530,212 -1,334,890,212 =41,640,000. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition. Let me check that again.\n\nLet me re-add the numbers digit by digit once more carefully:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAligning them:\n\n        9 9 6 9 3 1 2 8 7\n\n      + 3 7 9 5 9 8 9 2 5\n\n      --------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes:7 +5 =12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nTens:8 +2 =10 +1 =11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundreds:2 +9 =11 +1 =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThousands:1 +8 =9 +1 =10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3 +5 =8 +1 =9 \u21929, carryover0\n\nHundred thousands:9 +9 =18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nMillions:6 +7 =13 +1 =14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen millions:9 +3 =12 +1 =13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:9 +3 =12 +1 =13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (ones),1 (tens),2 (hundreds),0 (thousands),9 (ten thousands),8 (hundred thousands),4 (millions),3 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions),1 (billions).\n\nSo writing from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 1,334,890,212.\n\nHmm, so why does the part addition give a different result? Let me check the part addition again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nAdding 300,000,000:\n\n996,931,287 +300,000,000 = 1,296,931,287. Correct.\n\nNow adding the remaining 79,598,925 to this:\n\n1,296,931,287 +79,598,925.\n\nLet me compute this as:\n\n1,296,931,287 +70,000,000 =1,366,931,287.\n\nThen add 9,598,925:\n\n1,366,931,287 +9,598,925.\n\nBreaking down 9,598,925 into 9,000,000 +598,925.\n\nAdding 9,000,000: 1,366,931,287 +9,000,000 =1,375,931,287.\n\nAdding 598,925:\n\n1,375,931,287 +598,925.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n1,375,931,287 +500,000 =1,376,431,287.\n\nThen add 98,925:\n\n1,376,431,287 +98,925 =1,376,530,212.\n\nWait, but according to digit-by-digit, it should be 1,334,890,212. The difference is 41,640,000. That's a big number. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait a second, perhaps I misread the second number. Let me confirm the second number: 379,598,925.\n\nYes, that's 3 hundred millions, 79 million, etc. Wait, but when adding 300 million to the first number, which is 996,931,287, that gives 1,296,931,287. Then adding the remaining 79,598,925 should give the total. But according to digit-by-digit, the total is 1,334,890,212. Let me subtract 1,296,931,287 from 1,334,890,212 to see what the remaining should be:\n\n1,334,890,212 -1,296,931,287 = 37,958,925. Which is exactly the remaining part (79,598,925? Wait no, 379,598,925 minus 300,000,000 is 79,598,925. So the difference between the two totals is 1,376,530,212 -1,334,890,212 =41,640,000. So there's an error of 41,640,000. That suggests that in the part addition, I added an extra 41 million somewhere.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me try adding 996,931,287 +379,598,925 directly with another method.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n996,931,287\n\n+379,598,925\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n7 +5 =12 \u21922, carryover1\n\n8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\n2+9=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\n1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\n3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0\n\n9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\n6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\n9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\n9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nFinally the carryover1 \u21921.\n\nSo the result is 1,334,890,212. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the part addition must have an error. Let me check again:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nAdding 300,000,000 gives 1,296,931,287. Correct.\n\nAdding 79,598,925 to that:\n\nWait, 79,598,925 is 79,598,925. Let me add this to 1,296,931,287 directly:\n\n1,296,931,287\n\n+ 79,598,925\n\n-------------\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 +5 =12 \u21922, carryover1