s3 \u21929+3=12, plus 1 \u219213. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's digit is9, second's is3 \u21929+3=12, plus carryover1 \u219213. So here, we write 3 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, we need to add that 1 as a new digit to the left.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from last to first (since we started from the right):\n\nStarting from the first digit we wrote (position1:2), then position2:1, position3:2, position4:0, position5:3, position6:5, position7:4, position8:3, position9:3, and then the final carryover1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (position1), 1 (position2), 2 (position3), 0 (position4), 3 (position5),5 (position6),4 (position7),3 (position8),3 (position9), and then the 1 carried over.\n\nSo when written in order from left to right (billions place first), it would be:\n\n1 (the carried over 1), followed by the digits from position9 to position1:\n\nWait, let me think. The positions are numbered from right (position1) to left (position9 for the first number). The final digits after each position are:\n\nAfter position9 (hundred millions place), we had 13, so we wrote 3 and carried over 1. The next digit (the carryover) is the next higher place, which is billions. Since both original numbers have up to hundred millions (9 digits), adding them could result in a 10-digit number. So the final digit is the 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from left to right (highest to lowest place) would be:\n\n1 (billions), then the result from position9 (3), position8 (3), position7 (4), position6 (5), position5 (3), position4 (0), position3 (2), position2 (1), position1 (2).\n\nWait, let me list them step by step:\n\nThe final digits after each position:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1):\n\nPosition1: 2\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:5\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:3\n\nPosition9:3\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 at the billions place.\n\nSo arranging them from highest to lowest place (left to right):\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions place:3 (from position9)\n\nTen millions place:3 (position8)\n\nMillions place:4 (position7)\n\nHundred thousands place:5 (position6)\n\nTen thousands place:3 (position5)\n\nThousands place:0 (position4)\n\nHundreds place:2 (position3)\n\nTens place:1 (position2)\n\nOnes place:2 (position1)\n\nPutting all together: 1 3 3 4 5 3 0 2 1 2\n\nWait, let me write that as a number:\n\n1,334,530,212?\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:3 \u2192 133 (billions, hundred millions, ten millions?)\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nThe digits from left to right are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n2 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n2 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,334,530,212.\n\nWait, let me verify this with another method. Maybe adding the numbers using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, adding 996,931,287 + 379,598,925.\n\nLet me try adding them in parts:\n\nFirst, add the millions:\n\n996,931,287 is 996,931 thousand and 287.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking into larger chunks:\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n996,931,287\n\n+379,598,925\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the millions parts:\n\n996,931,287 can be considered as 996,931 thousand plus 287.\n\nWait, maybe better to split into:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287 = 900,000,000 + 96,000,000 + 931,000 + 287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925 = 300,000,000 + 79,000,000 + 598,000 + 925\n\nAdding the large chunks:\n\n900,000,000 + 300,000,000 = 1,200,000,000\n\n96,000,000 +79,000,000 = 175,000,000\n\n931,000 +598,000 = 1,529,000\n\n287 +925 = 1,212\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n1,200,000,000 + 175,000,000 = 1,375,000,000\n\n1,375,000,000 +1,529,000 = 1,376,529,000\n\n1,376,529,000 +1,212 = 1,376,530,212\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result of 1,334,530,212. Hmm, so there must be a mistake in my digit-by-digit addition earlier.\n\nWait, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the addition step by step again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n       996,931,287\n\n     + 379,598,925\n\n     ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place:7 +5=12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nTens:8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundreds:2+9=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThousands:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 remains.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (ones),1 (tens),2 (hundreds),0 (thousands),3 (ten thousands),5 (hundred thousands),4 (millions),3 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions), and then the 1 in billions.\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),2 (hundreds),1 (tens),2 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,334,530,212.\n\nBut when I did the chunk addition, I got 1,376,530,212. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me check the chunk addition again:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAdding the millions parts:\n\nWait, perhaps I split the numbers incorrectly.\n\nWait, 996,931,287:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n900,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n90,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n6,000,000 (millions)\n\n900,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n30,000 (ten thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n200 (hundreds)\n\n80 (tens)\n\n7 (ones)\n\nWait, maybe my previous breakdown was wrong. Let me try again:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nBreakdown:\n\n900,000,000 (9*100,000,000)\n\n90,000,000 (9*10,000,000)\n\n6,000,000 (6*1,000,000)\n\n900,000 (9*100,000)\n\n30,000 (3*10,000)\n\n1,000 (1*1,000)\n\n200 (2*100)\n\n80 (8*10)\n\n7 (7*1)\n\nTotal: 900M +90M=990M; +6M=996M; +900k=996,900k; +30k=996,930k; +1k=996,931k; +200=996,931,200; +80=996,931,280; +7=996,931,287. Correct.\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nBreakdown:\n\n300,000,000 (3*100M)\n\n70,000,000 (7*10M)\n\n9,000,000 (9*1M)\n\n500,000 (5*100k)\n\n90,000 (9*10k)\n\n8,000 (8*1k)\n\n900 (9*100)\n\n20 (2*10)\n\n5 (5*1)\n\nAdding these:\n\n300M +70M=370M; +9M=379M; +500k=379,500k; +90k=379,590k; +8k=379,598k; +900=379,598,900; +20=379,598,920; +5=379,598,925. Correct.\n\nNow adding the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAdding the hundred millions:\n\n900M + 300M = 1,200M\n\nTen millions: 90M +70M =160M \u2192 total so far: 1,200M +160M =1,360M\n\nMillions:6M +9M=15M \u2192 total 1,375M\n\nHundred thousands:900k +500k=1,400k \u2192 1,375M +1.4M =1,376.4M\n\nTen thousands:30k +90k=120k \u2192 1,376,400k +120k =1,376,520k\n\nThousands:1k +8k=9k \u2192 1,376,520k +9k =1,376,529k\n\nHundreds:200 +900=1,100 \u2192 1,376,529,000 +1,100 =1,376,530,100\n\nTens:80 +20=100 \u2192 1,376,530,100 +100 =1,376,530,200\n\nOnes:7 +5=12 \u2192 1,376,530,200 +12 =1,376,530,212.\n\nAh, so the chunk method gives 1,376,530,212, but the digit-by-digit addition gave 1,334,530,212. There's a discrepancy here. So I must have made a mistake in the digit-by-digit approach.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 996,931,287\n\nSecond number: 379,598,925\n\nAligning them:\n\n          9 9 6 9 3 1 2 8 7\n\n        + 3 7 9 5 9 8 9 2 5\n\n        -------------------------\n\nWait, actually, when written vertically, the digits should align properly. Let me write each digit in columns:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number (996,931,287):\n\nBillions: 9 (since it's a 9-digit number, the first digit is hundred millions, so actually, the first digit is 9 in the hundred millions place. Wait, confusion arises because the number is 9 digits, so the highest place is hundred millions. So when adding to a 9-digit number, the sum can be up to 10 digits (billions place).\n\nLet me write both numbers with leading zeros to make them 9 digits each, but actually, both are 9 digits. Wait, let me write them digit by digit from right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nFirst number (996,931,287):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):7\n\n2 (tens):8\n\n3 (hundreds):2\n\n4 (thousands):1\n\n5 (ten thousands):3\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9\n\n7 (millions):6\n\n8 (ten millions):9\n\n9 (hundred millions):9\n\nSecond number (379,598,925):\n\nPosition1:5\n\n2:2\n\n3:9\n\n4:8\n\n5:9\n\n6:5\n\n7:7\n\n8:3\n\n9:3\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:7+5=12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nPosition2:8+2=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition3:2+9=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition4:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nPosition5:3+9=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition6:9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nPosition7:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nPosition8:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition9:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nNow, the final carryover1 is added as a new digit to the left (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (positions1-9 and the carryover):\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:2\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:5\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:3\n\nPosition9:3\n\nPlus the carryover1 at the beginning.\n\nSo arranging from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\n1 (billions), then the result of position9 (3), position8 (3), position7 (4), position6 (5), position5 (3), position4 (0), position3 (2), position2 (1), position1 (2).\n\nThus the number is 1,334,530,212.\n\nWait, but the chunk method gave 1,376,530,212. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me do a calculator check. Since I can't use a calculator, perhaps I can add the numbers in another way.\n\nLet me add 996,931,287 + 379,598,925.\n\nLet me add 996,931,287 + 300,000,000 = 1,296,931,287\n\nThen add 79,598,925:\n\n1,296,931,287 + 70,000,000 = 1,366,931,287\n\nThen +9,598,925:\n\n1,366,931,287 +9,000,000 =1,375,931,287\n\n+598,925:\n\n1,375,931,287 +500,000 =1,376,431,287\n\n+98,925:\n\n1,376,431,287 +98,925 =1,376,530,212.\n\nAh, so the correct answer is 1,376,530,212. Therefore, my digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit steps:\n\nPosition7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's digit at position7 (millions place) is 6.\n\nSecond number's digit at position7 (millions place) is7 (since in the breakdown above, position7 for the second number is7).\n\nSo 6+7=13, plus carryover1 (from position6) gives 14. So write 4, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place):\n\nFirst number's digit is9, second's is3. 9+3=12, plus carryover1 \u219213. So write3, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place):\n\nFirst number's digit is9, second's is3. 9+3=12, plus carryover1 \u219213. So write3, carryover1. Correct.\n\nSo the digits up to position9 give 3 (hundred millions), but the total should be 1,376,530,212. Wait, but according to the digit-by-digit steps, the digits from position9 to position1 are:\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions place)\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:0 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:1 (tens)\n\nPosition1:2 (ones)\n\nPlus the carryover1 in billions.\n\nSo the number is 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),4 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),2 (hundreds),1 (tens),2 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,334,530,212. But the correct answer is 1,376,530,212. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The positions for the digits might have been misassigned. Let me re-express the digit positions properly.\n\nWait, when I broke down the first number (996,931,28