(ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:4 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSo both numbers are 9-digit numbers. Therefore, when adding, the maximum digits would be 10 (if there's a carryover in the hundred millions place).\n\nTherefore, in the digit-by-digit addition earlier, I incorrectly considered them as 10-digit numbers, but they are actually 9-digit numbers. That's where the mistake was.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition correctly with 9 digits each.\n\nFirst number (769,466,698):\n\nPositions 1-9 (right to left):\n\n1:8\n\n2:9\n\n3:6\n\n4:6\n\n5:4\n\n6:6\n\n7:9\n\n8:6\n\n9:7\n\nSecond number (944,438,224):\n\nPositions1-9:\n\n1:4\n\n2:2\n\n3:2\n\n4:8\n\n5:3\n\n6:4\n\n7:4\n\n8:4\n\n9:9\n\nNow adding each position with carry:\n\nStart with position1 (ones):\n\n8 +4 =12 \u21922, carry1\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n9 +2 =11 +1=12 \u21922, carry1\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n6 +2 =8 +1=9 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n6 +8 =14 \u21924, carry1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n4 +3 =7 +1=8 \u21928, carry0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n6 +4 =10 \u21920, carry1\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n9 +4 =13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n6 +4 =10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n7 +9 =16 +1=17 \u21927, carry1\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (position9), we have a carryover of1, so we add a new digit at position10 (billions place):1.\n\nNow, the digits from position1 to position10 are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:7\n\nPosition8:1\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition1:2\n\nArranged from left to right (billions to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 7 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, let me list them properly:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition2 (tens):2\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1,714,084,922.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,000,000,000 (billions)\n\n+700,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n+10,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n+4,000,000 (millions)\n\n+0 (hundred thousands)\n\n+80,000 (ten thousands)\n\n+4,000 (thousands)\n\n+900 (hundreds)\n\n+20 (tens)\n\n+2 (ones)\n\nTotal: 1,714,084,922.\n\nWait, but earlier when I did the breakdown adding 769M +944M, I got 1,713,904,922. There's a discrepancy here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's compute 769,466,698 + 944,438,224:\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n769,466,698\n\n+944,438,224\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n700,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,600,000,000\n\n69,466,698 +44,438,224 = ?\n\n69,466,698 +44,438,224:\n\n69,466,698 +40,000,000 =109,466,698\n\n+4,438,224 \u2192109,466,698 +4,438,224 =113,904,922\n\nTotal sum:1,600,000,000 +113,904,922 =1,713,904,922.\n\nBut according to the corrected digit-by-digit addition, it's 1,714,084,922. There's a difference of 180,000.\n\nHmm, so where is the mistake now?\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nPosition7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's position7 is 9 (millions place), second's position7 is4 (millions place). So 9+4=13 plus carryover1 from position6 (which was 10, so carryover1). So 13+1=14. So write4, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's position8 is6 (ten millions), second's position8 is4 (ten millions). So 6+4=10 plus carryover1 \u219211. So write1, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's position9 is7 (hundred millions), second's position9 is9 (hundred millions). 7+9=16 plus carryover1 \u219217. So write7, carryover1.\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1 (carryover)\n\nPosition9:7\n\nPosit