      "text": "\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 758,930,577 and 819,849,018. Hmm, adding such large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by their place values. Let me visualize that:\n\n      758,930,577\n    + 819,849,018\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's go through each column one by one.\n\n**Ones place:** 7 + 8. That's 15. I'll write down 5 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\n**Tens place:** 7 (from the first number) + 1 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 1. Wait, let me check again. The tens digit of the first number is 7 (since the number is 758,930,577, the digits from right are ones:7, tens:7, hundreds:5, etc.), and the second number's tens digit is 1 (from 819,849,018). So adding those: 7 + 1 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. No carryover here.\n\n**Hundreds place:** Next, the hundreds digits. First number has 5, second has 0. 5 + 0 = 5. No carryover.\n\n**Thousands place:** The first number's thousands digit is 0, and the second's is 9. 0 + 9 = 9. Still no carryover.\n\n**Ten-thousands place:** First number has 3, second has 4. 3 + 4 = 7. Easy, no carryover.\n\n**Hundred-thousands place:** First number's digit here is 9, and the second's is 8. 9 + 8 = 17. So write down 7 and carry over 1.\n\n**Millions place:** First number has 8, second has 1. 8 + 1 = 9, plus the carried 1 makes 10. So write 0 and carry over 1 again.\n\n**Ten-millions place:** First number's digit is 5, second is 9. 5 + 9 = 14, plus the carried 1 is 15. So write 5 and carry over 1.\n\n**Hundred-millions place:** First number has 7, second has 8. 7 + 8 = 15, plus the carried 1 makes 16. Write 6 and carry over 1.\n\n**Billions place:** The first number has 7 in the billions place, and the second has 8. Wait, actually, let me check the digit positions again because I might have miscounted. Let me recount the digits to ensure accuracy.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number: 758,930,577\nBreaking it down:\n- Billions: 7\n- Hundred millions: 5\n- Ten millions: 8\n- Millions: 9\n- Hundred thousands: 3\n- Ten thousands: 0\n- Thousands: 5\n- Hundreds: 7\n- Tens: 7\n- Ones: 7\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. So 758,930,577 is 7 (hundred millions?), no. Wait, let's parse it correctly:\n\nThe number 758,930,577 is:\n\n- 7 (hundred millions place?), let's see:\n\nActually, in the standard format, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n758,930,577:\n\n- The first comma from the right separates the thousands, so:\n\n758,930,577 is 758 (million) 930 (thousand) 577.\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nPosition from right (starting at 0):\n\nDigit 0 (ones): 7\n\nDigit 1 (tens):7\n\nDigit 2 (hundreds):5\n\nDigit 3 (thousands):0\n\nDigit 4 (ten thousands):3\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nDigit 6 (millions):8\n\nDigit 7 (ten millions):5\n\nDigit 8 (hundred millions):7\n\nWait, that might be a better way. Let me list each digit with its place:\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\nPosition 0: 7 (ones)\n\n1:7 (tens)\n\n2:5 (hundreds)\n\n3:0 (thousands)\n\n4:3 (ten thousands)\n\n5:9 (hundred thousands)\n\n6:8 (millions)\n\n7:5 (ten millions)\n\n8:7 (hundred millions)\n\nSimilarly for the second number: 819,849,018\n\nBreaking down:\n\nPosition 0:8 (ones)\n\n1:1 (tens)\n\n2:0 (hundreds)\n\n3:9 (thousands)\n\n4:4 (ten thousands)\n\n5:8 (hundred thousands)\n\n6:1 (millions)\n\n7:9 (ten millions)\n\n8:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo now, let's realign each digit properly for addition:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits in each place:\n\nNumber1 (758,930,577):\n\nPositions (from 0 to 9):\n\n0:7\n\n1:7\n\n2:5\n\n3:0\n\n4:3\n\n5:9\n\n6:8\n\n7:5\n\n8:7\n\n9:0 (since it's a 9-digit number, the 9th position is 0)\n\nWait, actually, 758,930,577 has 9 digits, so the highest position is 8 (hundred millions). The second number, 819,849,018 is also 9 digits. So both have up to the hundred millions place.\n\nSo when adding, let's go digit by digit from position 0 to 8.\n\nLet me create a table:\n\nPosition | Number1 | Number2 | Sum (with carry)\n---|---|---|---\n0 (ones) |7 |8 |7+8=15 \u2192 5, carry 1\n1 (tens)|7 |1 |7+1 +1(carry)=9 \u2192 9, carry 0\n2 (hundreds)|5 |0 |5+0=5 \u21925, carry 0\n3 (thousands)|0 |9 |0+9=9 \u21929, carry 0\n4 (ten thousands)|3 |4 |3+4=7 \u21927, carry 0\n5 (hundred thousands)|9 |8 |9+8=17 \u21927, carry 1\n6 (millions)|8 |1 |8+1 +1=10 \u21920, carry 1\n7 (ten millions)|5 |9 |5+9 +1=15 \u21925, carry 1\n8 (hundred millions)|7 |8 |7+8 +1=16 \u21926, carry 1\n9 (billions)|0 |0 (since both numbers have 9 digits) but carryover 1 \u21921\n\nWait, hold on. After adding the hundred millions place (position 8), we had a carryover of 1. Since both numbers don't have a digit beyond hundred millions (they are 9-digit numbers), the carryover becomes a new digit in the billions place (position 9). So the total sum will have a 10th digit.\n\nSo compiling the results from each position:\n\nStarting from position 0 (ones) to 9 (billions):\n\nPosition 0:5\n\n1:9\n\n2:5\n\n3:9\n\n4:7\n\n5:7\n\n6:0\n\n7:5\n\n8:6\n\n9:1 (from the final carryover)\n\nSo writing the digits from position 9 down to 0:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions?), wait let me list them properly.\n\nWait, the positions are numbered from 0 (ones) to 9 (billions). So the digits in order from highest to lowest (billions down to ones):\n\nPosition 9:1\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:9\n\nPosition0:5\n\nWait, let me list them in order:\n\nBillions place (position9):1\n\nHundred millions (position8):6\n\nTen millions (position7):5\n\nMillions (position6):0\n\nHundred thousands (position5):7\n\nTen thousands (position4):7\n\nThousands (position3):9\n\nHundreds (position2):5\n\nTens (position1):9\n\nOnes (position0):5\n\nSo putting all together:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,650,779,595?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let's construct the number step by step:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nPosition9:1 \u2192 1,000,000,000\n\nPosition8:6 \u2192 600,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,600,000,000\n\nPosition7:5 \u2192 50,000,000 \u2192 total 1,650,000,000\n\nPosition6:0 \u2192 0 \u2192 remains 1,650,000,000\n\nPosition5:7 \u2192 700,000 \u2192 total 1,650,700,000\n\nPosition4:7 \u2192 70,000 \u2192 1,650,770,000\n\nPosition3:9 \u2192 9,000 \u2192 1,650,779,000\n\nPosition2:5 \u2192 500 \u2192 1,650,779,500\n\nPosition1:9 \u2192 90 \u2192 1,650,779,590\n\nPosition0:5 \u2192 5 \u2192 1,650,779,595\n\nYes, so the total is 1,650,779,595.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers in parts or using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's add 758,930,577 + 819,849,018.\n\nBreaking them into millions:\n\n758,930,577 = 758,000,000 + 930,577\n\n819,849,018 = 819,000,000 + 849,018\n\nAdding the millions parts: 758,000,000 + 819,000,000 = 1,577,000,000\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 930,577 + 849,018.\n\nCalculating 930,577 + 849,018:\n\n930,577 + 800,000 = 1,730,577\n\nThen add 49,018: 1,730,577 +49,018 = 1,779,595\n\nSo total sum is 1,577,000,000 + 1,779,595 = 1,578,779,595? Wait, that's conflicting with the previous result.\n\nWait, hold on, that can't be. Wait, 758,000,000 +819,000,000 is indeed 1,577,000,000.\n\nThen 930,577 +849,018:\n\nLet me compute that again:\n\n930,577\n\n+849,018\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nOnes:7+8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9\n\nHundreds:5+0=5\n\nThousands:0+9=9\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 \u21927 carry1\n\nMillions: (the first number has 9 in hundred thousands, so the millions part here is 9 (from 930,577's hundred thousands place?), wait maybe I need to do this properly.\n\nWait, 930,577 is 0.930577 million? No, wait 930,577 is 930 thousand 577. Wait, actually, 930,577 is 9 (hundred thousands) 3 (ten thousands) 0 (thousands) 5 (hundreds) 7 (tens) 7 (ones). So adding to 849,018 which is 8 (hundred thousands) 4 (ten thousands) 9 (thousands) 0 (hundreds) 1 (tens) 8 (ones).\n\nAdding them:\n\nHundred thousands place:9 +8=17 \u21927 carry1\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7 +1=8\n\nThousands:0+9=9\n\nHundreds:5+0=5\n\nTens:7+1=8\n\nOnes:7+8=15 \u21925 carry1\n\nWait, but the total would be:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5, carry1\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:9\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nPlus the carryover from hundred thousands:1 (since 9+8=17, so carryover 1 to the millions place).\n\nWait, so the total of 930,577 +849,018 is 1,779,595 (since 930k +849k =1,779k, plus 577+18=595). So yes, 1,779,595.\n\nTherefore, adding to the millions part:\n\n1,577,000,000 +1,779,595 = 1,578,779,595.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous result of 1,650,779,595. Hmm, so there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me check the first method again. In the first approach, when adding digit by digit, I ended up with 1,650,779,595, but the second method gives 1,578,779,595. These are different. So I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nLet me retrace the first method's steps.\n\nIn the first method, when adding the hundred millions place (position8):\n\nNumber1's hundred millions digit is 7, Number2's is 8. Adding them plus the carryover from the previous (ten millions place):\n\nWait, let's go step by step again for the first method.\n\nStarting over with digit-by-digit addition:\n\nPositions 0 to 8 (since both are 9-digit numbers):\n\nPosition0 (ones):7+8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition1 (tens):7+1 +1=9 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5+0=5 \u21925, carry0\n\nPosition3 (thousands):0+9=9 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):3+4=7 \u21927, carry0\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9+8=17 \u21927, carry1\n\nPosition6 (millions):8+1 +1=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5+9 +1=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7+8 +1=16 \u21926, carry1\n\nThen, since there are no more digits, the final carryover 1 becomes the billions place (position9).\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:9\n\nPosition0:5\n\nSo the number is 1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo written out: 1,650,779,595.\n\nBut the second method gave 1,578,779,595. The discrepancy is in the millions and hundred millions places.\n\nWait, let me check the second method again. The first number is 758,930,577 and the second is 819,849,018.\n\nAdding them directly:\n\n758,930,577\n\n+819,849,018\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  758,930,577\n\n+819,849,018\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7+8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9\n\nHundreds:5+0=5\n\nThousands:0+9=9\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 \u21927, carry1\n\nMillions:8+1=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTen millions:5+9=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nHundred millions:7+8=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1\n\nBillions:0+0 +1=1\n\nSo the result is 1,650,779,595. Wait, so the second method must have had an error.\n\nWait in the second method, I broke it into 758,000,000 + 819,000,000 = 1,577,000,000. Then added the remaining parts 930,577 +849,018 =1,779,595. Then total is 1,577,000,000 +1,779,595 =1,578,779,595. But that's wrong because 758,930,577 is 758,000,000 + 930,577, and 819,849,018 is 819,000,000 +849,018. So adding the millions parts: 758,000,000 +819,000,000 =1,577,000,000. Then adding the smaller parts: 930,577 +849,018 =1,779,595. So total is 1,577,000,000 +1,779,595 =1,578,779,595. But this contradicts the digit-by-digit result.\n\nWait, where is the mistake here?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The first number is 758,930,577. Breaking into 758,000,000 and 930,577 is correct. The second number is 819,849,018, which is 819,000,000 +849,018. So adding those two parts:\n\n758,000,000 +819,000,000 =1,577,000,000.\n\nThen 930,577 +849,018:\n\nLet me compute that again carefully:\n\n930,577\n\n+849,018\n\nAdding:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:7+8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9\n\nHundreds:5+0=5\n\nThousands:0+9=9\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 \u21927, carry1\n\nMillions place: The first number has 9 in the hundred thousands (so the millions digit is 9?), wait no. Wait, 930,577 is 0.930577 million? Wait, no. Wait, 930,577 is 930 thousand 577. So in terms of millions, it's 0.930577 million? Wait, no, actually, 930,577 is less than a million. So when adding 930,577 +849,018, the total is indeed 1,779,595. So adding that to 1,577,000,000 gives 1,578,779,595. But this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,650,779,595.\n\nWait, this is a problem. There must be an error in one of the methods. Let me check with another approach.\n\nLet me use another method: adding the numbers step by step.\n\nFirst number: 758,930,577\n\nSecond number:819,849,018\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with the first number:\n\n758,930,577\n\nAdd 800,000,000 \u2192 1,558,930,577\n\nThen add 19,849,018:\n\n1,558,930,577 +19,849,018.\n\nBreaking down 19,849,018 into 10,000,000 +9,849,018.\n\nAdding 10,000,000: 1,568,930,577\n\nThen add 9,849,018:\n\n1,568,930,577 +9,849,018.\n\nAdding 9,000,000: 1,577,930,577\n\nThen 849,018:\n\n1,577,930,577 +849,018 = ?\n\nAdding 800,000: 1,578,730,577\n\nThen 49,018: 1,578,730,577 +49,018 = 1,578,779,595.\n\nAh! So this gives the same result as the second method: 1,578,779,595. But the first method gave 1,650,779,595. So there's a mistake in the first method's digit-by-digit addition.\n\nLet me re-examine the first method's steps.\n\nIn the first method, when adding the hundred millions place (position8):\n\nNumber1's hundred millions digit is 7, Number2's is 8. Adding them plus the carryover from the previous (ten millions place):\n\nWait, let's go step by step again for the first method's digit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from position0 (ones):\n\nPosition0:7+8=15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition1 (tens):7 (from first number) +1 (from second) +1 (carry) =9 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5 +0=5 \u21925, carry0\n\nPosition3 (thousands):0 +9=9 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):3 +4=7 \u21927, carry0\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9 +8=17 \u21927, carry1\n\nPosition6 (millions):8 (from first) +1 (from second) +1 (carry) =10 \u21920, carry1\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry) =15 \u21925, carry1\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carry) =16 \u21926, carry1\n\nThen the carryover 1 becomes the billions place (position9).\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:9\n\nPosition0:5\n\nSo the number is 1,650,779,595.\n\nWait, but according to the other methods, it should be 1,578,779,595. The difference is in the hundred millions and ten millions places.\n\nWait, let me check the ten millions place again. In the first method, position7 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 5 (since 758,930,577: the digits are 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions)... Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the digit positions in the first method.\n\nWait, let's re-express the first number's digits properly.\n\nFirst number: 758,930,577.\n\nBreaking down each digit from the left (billions place):\n\nDigit positions (from left to right):\n\n1 (billions): 7 (but since it's a 9-digit number, the first digit is hundred millions place?)\n\nWait, confusion arises from how we count digit positions. Let me clarify:\n\nThe number 758,930,577 has digits as follows:\n\nDigit 1: 7 (hundred millions place)\n\nDigit 2:5 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:8 (millions)\n\nDigit4:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:0 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:5 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:7 (tens)\n\nDigit9:7 (ones)\n\nWait, no, actually, when written as 758,930,577:\n\n- The first comma separates the millions, so:\n\n758 (million), 930 (thousand), 577.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (positions 0 to 8):\n\nPosition0 (ones):7\n\nPosition1 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):0\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition6 (millions):8\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7\n\nAh! Here's the mistake in the first method. In the first method, I considered the ten millions digit as position7, which is correct, but the hundred millions digit is position8 (7). The second number's hundred millions digit is 8 (from 819,849,018):\n\nBreaking down the second number:\n\n819,849,018:\n\nPosition0:8\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition7:9 (ten millions place)\n\nPosition8:8 (hundred millions place)\n\nSo when adding the ten millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 5 (position7), second's is 9 (position7). So 5 +9 + carryover from millions place.\n\nWait, let's retrace the first method's steps with correct digit positions:\n\nPosition6 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 8 (position6), second's is1 (position6). Adding 8+1 + carryover from hundred thousands place (which was 1 from position5's addition):\n\nWait, position5 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is9, second's is8. 9+8=17, so carryover1 to position6.\n\nSo position6 (millions):\n\n8 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nThen position7 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is5 (position7), second's is9 (position7). So 5+9 +1 (carryover from millions) =15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst's 7 + second's8 +1 (carryover) =16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nSo the hundred millions digit is6, and the carryover1 becomes the billions digit (position9:1).\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:6 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition7:5 (ten millions)\n\nPosition6:0 (millions)\n\nPosition5:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition4:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition3:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition2:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition1:9 (tens)\n\nPosition0:5 (ones)\n\nSo the number is 1,650,779,595.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 1,578,779,595. The discrepancy is in the hundred millions and ten millions places. Wait, let me check the second method again.\n\nWait in the second method, when I broke it into 758,000,000 +819,000,000 =1,577,000,000. Then adding the remaining 930,577 +849,018 =1,779,595. So total is 1,577,000,000 +1,779,595 =1,578,779,595.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit, it's 1,650,779,595. The difference is in the hundred millions place: 6 vs 5?\n\nWait, let me compute the two numbers directly with calculator-like steps:\n\n758,930,577\n\n+819,849,018\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 758,930,577\n\nAdd 800,000,000 \u2192 1,558,930,577\n\nThen add 19,849,018:\n\n1,558,930,577 +19,849,018.\n\nAdding 10,000,000 \u21921,568,930,577\n\nThen 9,849,018:\n\nAdding 9,000,000 \u21921,577,930,577\n\nThen 849,018:\n\nAdding 800,000 \u21921,578,730,577\n\nThen 49,018 \u21921,578,779,595.\n\nSo that's correct. So why does the digit-by-digit method give a different result?\n\nAh! I think I see the mistake in the first method's digit positions. Let me re-express the digit positions correctly.\n\nWait, in the first method, when I listed the digits for the first number (758,930,577):\n\nPosition0 (ones):7\n\nPosition1 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):0\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition6 (millions):8\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7\n\nSecond number (819,849,018):\n\nPosition0:8\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition8:8\n\nSo when adding the hundred millions place (position8):\n\n7 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carry from ten millions) =16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nBut the ten millions place (position7):\n\nFirst's ten millions digit is5, second's is9. Adding them plus carry from millions (position6):\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\nFirst's millions digit is8, second's is1. Adding them plus carry from hundred thousands (position5):\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):\n\n9+8=17 \u21927, carry1.\n\nSo position6:8+1+1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nThus, position7 (ten millions):\n\n5 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry) =15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nSo the carryover to hundred millions is1.\n\nThus, the hundred millions digit is 7+8+1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nSo the hundred millions digit is6, and the carryover1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nThus, the total is 1,650,779,595.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 1,578,779,595. There's a conflict here. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method's breakdown.\n\nWait, in the second method, I broke the first number into 758,000,000 +930,577. But 758,000,000 plus 930,577 is indeed 758,930,577. The second number is 819,000,000 +849,018 =819,849,018. So adding the millions parts:758,000,000 +819,000,000 =1,577,000,000. Then adding the remaining parts:930,577 +849,018 =1,779,595. So total is 1,577,000,000 +1,779,595 =1,578,779,595.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,650,779,595. The difference is in the hundred millions place: 6 vs 5?\n\nWait, let me compute the two numbers using another approach:\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 758,930,577\n\nSecond number:819,849,018\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:7+8=15 \u21