ft to right) would be:\n\nCarryover 1 (billions place), then the result of position9 (position9 was the original hundred millions place, so adding those digits plus carryover gives 15, so 5 in that place, plus the carryover 1 to the next higher digit which is the billions. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions.\n\nWait, let me think again. Let's consider each digit's place:\n\nOriginal numbers are 9 digits each. Let's write them as:\n\nFirst number: 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number:5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the ones (position1):\n\n5+5=10 \u2192 0, carryover1\n\nTens (position2):5+3=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0\n\nHundreds (position3):6+0=6 \u21926, carryover0\n\nThousands (position4):4+3=7 \u21927, carryover0\n\nTen thousands (position5):5+9=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands (position6):8+8=16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1\n\nMillions (position7):7+1=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0\n\nTen millions (position8):6+6=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nHundred millions (position9):9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred millions place addition becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the total sum is 1 (billions) 5 (hundred millions) 2 (ten millions) 9 (millions) 7 (hundred thousands) 4 (ten thousands) 7 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 9 (tens) 0 (ones).\n\nPutting that together: 1,529,747,690.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Let's add the numbers as whole:\n\n967,854,655 + 561,893,035.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n967,854,655 is 967 million plus 854,655.\n\n561,893,035 is 561 million plus 893,035.\n\nAdding the millions: 967 + 561 = 1,528 million.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts: 854,655 + 893,035.\n\n854,655 + 893,035:\n\nAdding thousands: 854,000 + 893,000 = 1,747,000.\