ly. The digits from highest to lowest (billions down to ones):\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:3\n\nOnes:5\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me list each digit position with their values:\n\nStarting from the billions place (the leftmost digit):\n\n1 (billions)\n\nThen hundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:3\n\nOnes:5\n\nSo combining these digits:\n\n1,4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,463,131,335?\n\nWait let me write it out:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4 \u2192 1,400,000,000\n\nTen millions:6 \u2192 60,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,460,000,000\n\nMillions:3 \u2192 3,000,000 \u2192 1,463,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:1 \u2192 100,000 \u2192 1,463,100,000\n\nTen thousands:3 \u2192 30,000 \u2192 1,463,130,000\n\nThousands:1 \u2192 1,000 \u2192 1,463,131,000\n\nHundreds:3 \u2192 300 \u2192 1,463,131,300\n\nTens:3 \u2192 30 \u2192 1,463,131,330\n\nOnes:5 \u2192 total 1,463,131,335.\n\nWait, but earlier when I thought I had 1,472,131,335, that was wrong because I must have miscalculated the millions place.\n\nWait let me check the millions place again. Position7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6 (since in the first number, 506,172,121: the millions place is the 7th digit from the right, which is 6). The second number's millions digit is 6 (from 965,959,214, the 7th digit from the right is 6). So 6+6=12 plus the carryover 1 from the hundred thousands place (which was 11, so carryover 1). So 12+1=13. So millions digit is 3, carryover 1. So that's correct. So millions place is 3, carryover to ten millions.\n\nTen millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 0, second is 5. 0+5=5 plus the carryover 1 gives 6. So ten millions is 6. No carryover here.\n\nThen hundred millions (position9):\n\n5 +9 =14, so 4 with carryover 1. So hundred millions is 4, and the carryover becomes the billions digit 1.\n\nSo putting it all together, the total is 1,463,131,335.\n\nWait, but in my first calculation I had 1,472,131,335, which was wrong because I must have miscalculated the millions place. So the correct answer should be 1,463,131,335.\n\nHmm, so I need to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me verify using another method, perhaps adding the numbers as whole:\n\n506,172,121\n\n+965,959,214\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 506,172,121 + 900,000,000 = 1,406,172,121\n\nThen add 65,959,214:\n\n1,406,172,121 + 60,000,000 = 1,466,172,121\n\nThen add 5,959,214:\n\n1,466,172,121 + 5,000,000 = 1,471,172,121\n\nThen add 959,214:\n\n1,471,172,121 + 900,000 = 1,472,072,121\n\nThen add 59,214:\n\n1,472,072,121 + 50,000 = 1,472,122,121\n\nThen add 9,214:\n\n1,472,122,121 + 9,000 = 1,472,131,121\n\nThen add 214:\n\n1,472,131,121 + 214 = 1,472,131,335.\n\nWait, that's conflicting with the previous result. Now I'm confused. Which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown. Let me try adding the two numbers directly:\n\n506,172,121\n\n+965,959,214\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  506,172,121\n\n+965,959,214\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:1+4=5\n\nTens:2+1=3\n\nHundreds:1+2=3\n\nThousands:2+9=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nTen thousands:7+5=12 +1=13 \u2192 3, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+6=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nTen millions:0+5=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover\n\nHundred millions:5+9=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nBillions:0 (since first number has 5 in hundred millions, but the second has 9, but when adding hundred millions, it's 5+9=14, so the carryover 1 becomes the billions digit. Wait, the first number's hundred millions digit is 5 (since it's 506 million...), the second's is 9 (965 million...). So adding those gives 14, so 4 in hundred millions, carryover 1 to billions. Since there are no digits beyond that, the total is 1,463,131,335?\n\nWait but in the step-by-step addition above, when I broke it down as adding 965,959,214 to 506,172,121 by parts, I ended up with 1,472,131,335. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait let me do the addition another way. Let's use calculator-like approach:\n\n506,172,121\n\n+965,959,214\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n1 +4 =5\n\nTens:2+1=3\n\nHundreds:1+2=3\n\nThousands:2+9=11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nTen thousands:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:6+6=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nTen millions:0+5=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover\n\nHundred millions:5+9=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921\n\nSo the total is 1,463,131,335.\n\nBut when I did the breakdown adding 965,959,214 as 900M +65,959,214, then adding step by step, I got 1,472,131,335. That must be an error in that method.\n\nWait let me recalculate that way again carefully:\n\nStarting with 506,172,121 + 965,959,214.\n\nLet me split 965,959,214 into 900,000,000 + 65,959,214.\n\nFirst add 506,172,121 + 900,000,000 = 1,406,172,121.\n\nThen add 65,959,214 to that:\n\n1,406,172,121 + 60,000,000 = 1,466,172,121.\n\nThen add 5,959,214:\n\n1,466,172,121 + 5,000,000 = 1,471,172,121.\n\nThen add 959,214:\n\n1,471,172,121 + 900,000 = 1,472,072,121.\n\nThen add 59,214:\n\n1,472,072,121 + 50,000 = 1,472,122,121.\n\nThen add 9,214:\n\n1,472,122,121 + 9,000 = 1,472,131,121.\n\nThen add 214:\n\n1,472,131,121 + 214 = 1,472,131,335.\n\nHmm, so this method gives a different result. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me check the first digit-by-digit addition again.\n\nWait, in the first method, when adding the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 5 (since 506,172,121 is