ndred millions) ... So the number is 1,463,131,335.\n\nBut in the second method, the result is 1,472,131,335. The difference is in the ten millions and hundred millions places. Let me check the second method's breakdown again.\n\nWait when adding 506,172,121 + 965,959,214:\n\nThe first number is 506,172,121 (which is 506 million), and the second is 965,959,214 (which is 965 million). So adding 506 + 965 = 1,471 million. So the total should be over 1.471 billion, which matches the second method's result. But the first method gave 1.463 billion, which is less. So there must be an error in the first method's digit-by-digit calculation.\n\nWait let me re-express the numbers with their place values properly.\n\nFirst number: 506,172,121\n\nBreaking down each digit from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nDigit 1 (billions): 5\n\nDigit 2 (hundred millions):0\n\nDigit 3 (ten millions):6\n\nDigit 4 (millions):1\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands):7\n\nDigit 6 (ten thousands):2\n\nDigit 7 (thousands):1\n\nDigit 8 (hundreds):2\n\nDigit 9 (tens):1\n\nDigit 10 (ones):1\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. So 506,172,121 is:\n\n5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions?), wait no. Wait the standard is:\n\nIn the number 506,172,121:\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 506,172,121 \u2192 506 (million), 172 (thousand), 121.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\nBillions: 0 (since it's less than a billion? Wait no, 506 million is 0.5 billion. Wait 1 billion is 1,000,000,000. So 506,172,121 is 506 million, so it's 0.506 billion. Wait but when written as 506,172,121, the first digit is 5 in the hundred millions place.\n\nWait let's clarify the place values properly:\n\nFor the number 506,172,121:\n\n- The digits are: 5 0 6 1 7 2 1 2 1\n\nPositions from right (ones):\n\nPosition 1: 1 (ones)\n\nPosition 2: 2 (tens)\n\nPosition 3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4:2 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 7:6 (millions)\n\nPosition 8:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the hundred millions digit is 5, ten millions is 0, millions is 6, etc.\n\nSecond number: 965,959,214:\n\nDigits: 9 6 5 9 5 9 2 1 4\n\nPositions from right:\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\nPosition2:1 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:6 (millions)\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSo adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right (position1 to position9):\n\nPosition1 (ones):1+4=5\n\nPosition2 (tens):2+1=3\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1+2=3\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2+9=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1+9=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nPosition7 (millions):6+6=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0+5=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+9=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the digits from billions down:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:3\n\nOnes:5\n\nSo the number is 1,463,131,335.\n\nBut according to the second method, adding 506,172,121 + 965,959,214 gives 1,472,131,335. There's a discrepancy of 9 million somewhere.\n\nWait let me compute 506,172,121 + 965,959,214 using calculator steps:\n\n506,172,121\n\n+965,959,214\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n506,172,121 is 506,172,121\n\n965,959,214 is 965,959,214\n\nAdding the two:\n\nStart with the millions:\n\n506,000,000 + 965,000,000 = 1,471,000,000\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n172,121 + 959,214 = ?\n\n172,121 + 959,214:\n\n172,121 + 900,000 = 1,072,121\n\nThen +59,214 \u2192 1,072,121 +59,214 = 1,131,335\n\nSo total is 1,471,000,000 +1,131,335 = 1,472,131,335.\n\nAh! So the correct answer is 1,472,131,335. So where did I go wrong in the first digit-by-digit method?\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit steps:\n\nPosition7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6 (position7: millions place is the 7th digit from the right, which is 6 in 506,172,121).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 6 (position7 in 965,959,214 is 6).\n\nSo 6+6=12 plus carryover1 from hundred thousands (which was 1+9+1=11, so carryover1). So 12+1=13. So millions digit is 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 0 (position8 is ten millions, which is the 8th digit from the right in 506,172,121 \u2192 0).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is5 (position8 in 965,959,214 is 5).\n\nSo 0+5=5 plus carryover1 \u21926. So ten millions is 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n5 (first) +9 (second) =14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nSo the digits up to hundred millions are:\n\nBillions:1 (carryover)\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:3\n\nOnes:5\n\nWait, but when we add the millions parts, the total should be 1,471 million plus the rest. But according to digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 4, which would make the hundred millions part 400 million, but the actual total is 1,472 million. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, the hundred millions digit in the first number is 5, second is9. 5+9=14, so hundred millions digit is4, and carryover1 to billions. So the hundred millions contribute 400 million, but the total should be 506 +965 =1,471 million. Wait, but 506 million is 5 hundred millions (500 million) plus 6 ten millions (60 million), totaling 560 million? Wait no, 506 million is 506,000,000. So 5 hundred millions (500M) plus 6 ten millions (60M) equals 560M. Adding 965M (9 hundred millions (900M) +6 ten millions (60M) +5 millions (5M) = 965M). So total is 560M +965M =1,525M? Wait no, that can't be. Wait I'm getting confused.\n\nWait let me think numerically:\n\n506,172,121 i