m:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 1+2=3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 0+5=5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 2+3=5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 7+7=14. Write 4, carry 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 0+3 +1=4 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 7+6=13. Wait, hold on. The hundred-thousands place in the first number is the 7 in 917,077,201. Let me confirm the positions again. Breaking down the first number:\n\nDigits from right: positions are ones (1), tens (0), hundreds (2), thousands (7), ten thousands (7), hundred thousands (0), millions (7), ten millions (1), hundred millions (9), billions (9? Wait, no. Wait, 917,077,201 is 9 (hundred millions?), let's see:\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write each digit's position properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number: 9 1 7 0 7 7 2 0 1\n\nWait, actually, 917,077,201 is 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, that might not be right. Let me count the digits:\n\n917,077,201 has 9 digits. Breaking down:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 7\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 0\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 1\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 9\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I need to parse it correctly. Let's see:\n\nThe number is 917,077,201. Breaking into groups:\n\n917,077,201 \u2192 917 (million), 077 (thousand), 201.\n\nSo:\n\n- Billions place: 9 (since 917 million is 0.917 billion, so the first digit is 9 in the hundred millions place?)\n\nWait, perhaps I should list each digit's place properly:\n\nLet me write the first number as:\n\nDigit 9: 9 (hundred millions place)\n\nDigit 1: 1 (ten millions)\n\nDigit 7: 7 (millions)\n\nDigit 0: 0 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit 7: 7 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit 7: 7 (thousands)\n\nDigit 2: 2 (hundreds)\n\nDigit 0: 0 (tens)\n\nDigit 1: 1 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 582,637,352\n\nBreaking down:\n\n5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nSo aligning both numbers:\n\nHundred millions: 9 vs 5 \u2192 9+5=14? Wait, but when adding, each column is added. Let me write them vertically:\n\n          9 1 7 0 7 7 2 0 1\n        + 5 8 2 6 3 7 3 5 2\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes: 1 + 2 = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 0 +5 =5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 2 +3 =5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands:7 +7 =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:7 (from first number) +3 (second number) +1 (carryover) = 11. Wait, hold on! Wait, in the ten thousands place, first number has 7 (since the breakdown was 917,077,201 \u2192 the ten thousands digit is the fifth from the right, which is the 7 in the 077 part?), so yes, ten thousands place is 7. The second number's ten thousands place is 3 (from 582,637,352 \u2192 the ten thousands digit is the fifth from the right, which is 3). So 7 +3 +1 (carryover) =11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake earlier here. In my first calculation, I thought the hundred thousands place was 0 and 6, but let's go step by step again properly.\n\nLet me list each digit's place for both numbers:\n\nFirst number (917,077,201):\n\nPositions (from right, 1 to 9):\n\n1: 1 (ones)\n\n2: 0 (tens)\n\n3: 2 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:1 (millions)\n\n8:1 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number (582,637,352):\n\nPositions (from right, 1 to 9):\n\n1:2 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:3 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:3 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:2 (millions)\n\n8:8 (ten millions)\n\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding column by column from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1 +2 =3\n\nPosition 2 (tens):0 +5=5\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+3=5\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7+7=14 \u2192 write 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 +3 +1 (carryover) =11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 +6 +1 (carryover) =7 \u2192 write 7, no carryover\n\nPosition7 (millions):1 +2 =3 \u2192 write 3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1 +8 =9 \u2192 write 9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9 +5 =14 \u2192 write 4, carryover 1\n\nBut since there's a carryover after the hundred millions (position9), we need to add that as a new digit to the left (billions place). So the total becomes 1 (carryover) followed by the digits we have.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have are:\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:4\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the total digits from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones). Wait, maybe I need to reconstruct the number properly.\n\nWait, let's list all the digits with their positions after adding:\n\nAfter adding each column, the digits from position1 to position9 plus the carryover:\n\nThe result digits (excluding the final carryover) are:\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:1 (with a carryover 1)\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:4 (with a carryover 1 from the hundred millions addition)\n\nWait, actually, when adding the hundred millions (position9):\n\n9 (from first) +5 (from second) =14, plus any carryover? Wait, the carryover from position8?\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nWait, position9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 9, second is 5. So 9+5=14. So we write 4 in that position and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes the billions digit. So the total is 1 followed by the 4, then the rest.\n\nSo the digits from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:9 (from position8: 1+8=9)\n\nMillions:3 (position7:1+2=3)\n\nHundred thousands:7 (position6:0+6+1(carryover from position5)=7)\n\nTen thousands:1 (position5:7+3+1=11 \u2192 1 with carryover)\n\nThousands:4 (position4:7+7=14 \u21924 with carryover)\n\nHundreds:5 (position3:2+3=5)\n\nTens:5 (position2:0+5=5)\n\nOnes:3 (position1:1+2=3)\n\nPutting all together:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo the number is 1,493,714,553?\n\nWait, let me write that with commas properly:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions digit:1\n\nThen hundred millions:4 \u2192 so 1,4 (hundred millions place is 4, so 1,4...)\n\nThen ten millions:9 \u2192 1,49 (ten millions is 9 \u2192 149...)\n\nMillions:3 \u2192 1,493 (millions place 3 \u2192 1,493...)\n\nHundred thousands:7 \u2192 1,493,7 (hundred thousands is 7 \u2192 1,493,7...)\n\nTen thousands:1 \u2192 1,493,71 (ten thousands is 1 \u2192 1,493,71...)\n\nThousands:4 \u2192 1,493,714 (thousands place 4 \u2192 ...714,)\n\nHundreds:5 \u2192 1,493,714,5\n\nTens:5 \u2192 1,493,714,55\n\nOnes:3 \u2192 1,493,714,553.\n\nWait, so the total is 1,493,714,553?\n\nBut let me check with another method to confirm. Maybe adding the numbers step by step:\n\nFirst number: 917,077,201\n\nSecond number: 582,637,352\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add 917,077,201 + 500,000,000 = 1,417,077,201\n\nThen add the remaining 82,637,352:\n\n1,417,077,201 + 82,637,352\n\nFirst add 80,000,000: 1,417,077,201 +80,000,000 =1,497,077,201\n\nThen add 2,637,352:\n\n1,497,077,201 +2,637,352 = ?\n\nAdding 2,000,000: 1,499,077,201\n\nThen 637,352: 1,499,077,201 +637,352 = 1,500,714,553.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which means I must have made a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition earlier.\n\nWait, so according to this method, the total is 1,500,714,553. But in the digit-by-digit, I got 1,493,714,553. So where did I go wrong?\n\nLet me check the digit-by-digit again carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 917,077,201\n\nSecond number:582,637,352\n\nAligning:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits:\n\nFirst number (917,077,201):\n\nDigits from right (position1 to 9):\n\n1:1, 2:0, 3:2, 4:7, 5:7, 6:0, 7:1, 8:1, 9:9\n\nSecond number (582,637,352):\n\nDigits from right (position1 to9):\n\n1:2, 2:5, 3:3, 4:7, 5:3, 6:6, 7:2, 8:8, 9:5\n\nNow adding each column:\n\nPosition1 (ones):1+2=3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nPosition2 (tens):0+5=5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+3=5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7+7=14 \u2192 write 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 (from first) +3 (second) +1 (carryover) =11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 (first) +6 (second) +1 (carryover) =7 \u2192 write7, no carryover\n\nPosition7 (millions):1 (first) +2 (second) =3 \u2192 write3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1 (first) +8 (second) =9 \u2192 write9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9 (first) +5 (second) =14 \u2192 write4, carryover1\n\nNow, the carryover1 from position9 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the digits from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo the number is 1,493,714,553. But according to the other method, it's 1,500,714,553. There's a difference in the millions place. Wait, let's see:\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit, the millions place (position7) was 1 (from first number) +2 (from second number) =3. But in the other method, when adding 917,077,201 +582,637,352:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n917,077,201\n\n+582,637,352\n\nLet me add