thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSecond number: 993,616,165.\n\nFrom right to left:\n\nPosition1 (ones):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition7 (millions):9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nSo when adding the millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 4, second's is9. So 4+9=13 plus any carryover?\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit steps earlier, when we got to position7 (millions):\n\nAt that step, the previous carryover was from position6 (hundred thousands):\n\nPosition6: 8 (first) +3 (second) =11 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nThen moving to position7 (millions):\n\n4 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carryover) =14 \u21924 with carryover1. So that's correct.\n\nSo the millions digit in the sum is 4, and carryover1 to the next (ten millions place).\n\nThen ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is8, second's is9. 8+9=17 plus carryover1 \u219218 \u21928 with carryover1.\n\nHundred millions (position9):\n\n6+9=15 plus carryover1 \u219216 \u21926 with carryover1.\n\nBillions (position10): carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1,684,185,656.\n\nBut in the alternative method, I had 1,678,485,656. The difference is in the hundred millions place. Let me check the alternative method again.\n\nWait in the alternative method, I added 684 million +993 million =1,677 million. But the first number is 684,869,491 which is 684 million plus 869,491. The second is 993,616,165 which is 993 million plus 616,165. So adding the millions parts: 684 +993 =1,677 million. Then adding the remaining parts: 869,491 +616,165 =1,485,656. So total is 1,677,000,000 +1,485,656 =1,678,485,656.\n\nWait, but according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,684,185,656. The difference is 1,684,185,656 -1,678,485,656 =5,700,000. Hmm, so where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second, perhaps I made a mistake in the alternative method's breakdown.\n\nWait, the first number is 684,869,491. Let me parse it correctly:\n\n684,869,491:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n600,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n80,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n4,000,000 (millions)\n\n800,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n60,000 (ten thousands)\n\n9,000 (thousands)\n\n400 (hundreds)\n\n90 (tens)\n\n1 (ones)\n\nSo total is indeed 684,869,491.\n\nSecond number:993,616,165:\n\n900,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n90,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n3,000,000 (millions)\n\n600,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n10,000 (ten thousands)\n\n6,000 (thousands)\n\n100 (hundreds)\n\n60 (tens)\n\n5 (ones)\n\nSo adding them:\n\nHundred millions:600M +900M =1,500M\n\nTen millions:80M +90M =170M \u2192 total so far 1,670M\n\nMillions:4M +3M =7M \u2192 total 1,677M\n\nHundred thousands:800k +600k =1,400k \u21921.4M \u2192 total 1,678.4M\n\nTen thousands:60k +10k =70k \u2192 total 1,678.47M\n\nThousands:9k +6k =15k \u2192 total 1,678.485M\n\nHundreds:400 +100 =500 \u2192 total 1,678,485,500\n\nTens:90 +60 =150 \u2192 total 1,678,485,650\n\nOnes:1 +5 =6 \u2192 total 1,678,485,656.\n\nWait, so that's correct. But according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,684,185,656. There's a discrepancy of 5.7 million. So I must have messed up the digit-by-digit addition somewhere.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps again carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nPositions from right (1 to 10):\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n1 +5 =6. Carry 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n9 +6 =15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n4 +1 =5 +1 (carry) =6. Carry0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 +6 =15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6 +1 =7 +1 (carry) =8. Carry0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8 +3 =11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n4 +9 =13 +1 (carry) =14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n8 +9 =17 +1 (carry) =18 \u21928, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6 +9 =15 +1 (carry) =16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\nCarry1 \u21921.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is 1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nPutting commas:\n\n1,684,185,656.\n\nWait, but according to the alternative method, it's 1,678,485,656. The difference is in the hundred millions and ten millions places.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 6, but in the alternative method, the hundred millions part was 6 (from first number) +9 (second) =15, plus carryover? Wait in the alternative method, the hundred millions were added as 600M +900M =1,500M, but in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 6, which would be part of 1,684 million?\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is in how the digits are being interpreted. Let me write the digit-by-digit result as a number:\n\nStarting from position10 (billions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6 \u2192 so that's 600,000,000\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8 \u219280,000,000\n\nPosition7 (millions):4 \u21924,000,000\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 \u2192100,000\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 \u219280,000\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5 \u21925,000\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6 \u2192600\n\nPosition2 (tens):5 \u219250\n\nPosition1 (ones):6 \u21926\n\nAdding these up:\n\n1,000,000,000\n\n+600,000,000 \u21921,600,000,000\n\n+80,000,000 \u21921,680,000,000\n\n+4,000,000 \u21921,684,000,000\n\n+100,000 \u21921,684,100,000\n\n+80,000 \u21921,684,180,000\n\n+5,000 \u21921,684,185,000\n\n+600 \u21921,684,185,600\n\n+50 \u21921,684,185,650\n\n+6 \u21921,684,185,656.\n\nAh! So the digit-by-digit result is indeed 1,684,185,656. But in the alternative method, I got 1,678,485,656. The mistake must be in the alternative method's breakdown.\n\nWait, in the alternative method, I added the millions parts as 684 million +993 million =1,677 million. But the first number is 684,869,491, which is 684 million plus 869,491. The second number is 993,616,165, which is 993 million plus 616,165. So adding the millions parts (the 684 and 993) gives 1,677 million. But the total sum is 1,677,000,000 plus the remaining parts (869,491 +616,165 =1,485,656). So total is 1,677,000,000 +1,485,656 =1,678,485,656.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,684,185,656. The difference is 5,700,000. Where is this coming from?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the alternative method's breakdown of the numbers.\n\nWait, let me re-express the first number:\n\n684,869,491:\n\nThe \"684\" is in the millions? Wait no, commas are placed every three digits from the right. So 684,869,491 is:\n\n684 (million) , 869 (thousand), 491.\n\nWait, no, actually, the first comma after three digits from the right:\n\nWait, the number is 684,869,491. Breaking down:\n\nStarting from the right, the first comma separates thousands, the next separates millions, etc.\n\nSo:\n\n684,869,491 \u2192 684 (millions), 869 (thousands), 491.\n\nWait, no, actually, commas are placed as follows:\n\nFor numbers over a million, the first comma is after the thousands, then millions, etc. So 684,869,491 is:\n\n684 (million) , 869 (thousand), 491.\n\nWait, but 684 million is 684,000,000. Adding 869,491 gives 684,869,491. That's correct.\n\nThe second number:993,616,165 is 993 million, 616 thousand, 165.\n\nSo adding the millions parts:684 +993 =1,677 million.\n\nAdding the thousands parts:869,000 +616,000 =1,485,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts:491 +165 =656.\n\nTotal:1,677,000,000 +1,485,000 +656 =1,678,485,656.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,684,185,656. The difference is 5,700,000. So there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me do a small check with smaller numbers to see which method is correct.\n\nLet me take the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 684,869,491\n\nSecond number:993,616,165\n\nLet me add them using another method, perhaps breaking into parts:\n\nFirst number: 600,000,000 + 84,869,491\n\nSecond number:900,000,000 +93,616,165\n\nAdding the large parts:600M +900M =1,500M\n\nAdding the smaller parts:84,869,491 +93,616,165.\n\nCompute 84,869,491 +93,616,165:\n\n84,869,491 +93,616,165:\n\nAdding:\n\n80,000,000 +90,000,000 =170,000,000\n\n4,869,491 +3,616,165 =8,485,656\n\nTotal:170,000,000 +8,485,656 =178,485,656\n\nAdding to the 1,500M gives 1,500,000,000 +178,485,656 =1,678,485,656.\n\nThis matches the alternative method. So the digit-by-digit must have an error.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit?\n\nLet me re-express the digit-by-digit addition step-by-step again, carefully:\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst: 684,869,491 \u2192 digits from right (position1 to 9):\n\n1:1, 2:9, 3:4, 4:9, 5:6, 6:8, 7:4, 8:8, 9:6\n\nSecond:993,616,165 \u2192 digits from right (position1 to 9):\n\n1:5, 2:6, 3:1, 4:6, 5:1, 6:3, 7:9, 8:9, 9:9\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n1 +5 =6. Carry 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n9 +6 =15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n4 +1 =5 +1 (carry) =6. Carry0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 +6 =15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6 +1 =7 +1 (carry) =8. Carry0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8 +3 =11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n4 (first) +9 (second) =13 +1 (carry) =14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n8 (first) +9 (second) =17 +1 (carry) =18 \u21928, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6 (first) +9 (second) =15 +1 (carry) =16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\nCarry1 \u21921.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is 1,684,185,656.\n\nWait, but according to the other methods, it's 1,678,485,656. The difference is in the hundred millions and ten millions places.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 6, but according to the alternative method, the hundred millions part was 6 (from first number) +9 (second) =15, plus any carryover?\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit (position9) is 6, but the actual addition there was 6 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry from ten millions place) =16. So the digit is 6 with a carryover of 1 to the next (billions). But in the alternative method, the hundred millions were added as 600M +900M =1,500M, but in the digit-by-digit, the hundred millions digit is 6, which would be 600M, but with the carryover from ten millions, which was 18 (ten millions place: 8 with carryover1). So the total hundred millions would be 600M +900M + carryover1 from ten millions? Wait no, the carryover from ten millions is added to the hundred millions place.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is in how the carries propagate. Let me think of the total as:\n\nThe digit-by-digit result is 1,684,185,656.\n\nThe alternative method gives 1,678,485,656.\n\nThe difference is exactly 5,700,000. Let me compute 1,684,185,656 minus 1,678,485,656 =5,700,000.\n\nHmm, so where is this 5.7 million coming from?\n\nWait, let me check the millions place in the digit-by-digit result. The millions digit is 4 (position7), which is 4,000,000. In the alternative method, the millions parts were 684M +993M =1,677M. But in the digit-by-digit, the millions digit is 4, so that would be 4 million, but the total is 1,684 million. Wait, that suggests that the digit-by-digit is counting the millions place as part of the total, but perhaps I'm misinterpreting the digit positions.\n\nWait, the digit-by-digit's position7 is millions place, so the digit there is 4, so that's 4,000,000. The total up to that point would be:\n\nBillions:1,000,000,000\n\nHundred millions:600,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,600,000,000\n\nTen millions:80,000,000 \u21921,680,000,000\n\nMillions:4,000,