 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, no:\n\nWait 993,616,165:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nStarting from the left: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 5 (ones). So the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition 2:6 (tens)\n\nPosition 3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition 4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition 5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition 6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition 7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition 8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition 9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, but the first number's digits:\n\n684,869,491:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition 7 (millions):4\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSo when adding, we need to add each corresponding digit from both numbers, starting from position 1 (ones) up to position 9 (hundred millions), and possibly a tenth digit if there's a carryover beyond the hundred millions.\n\nLet me create a table for each position:\n\nPosition | Number1 | Number2 | Sum (with carry)\n---|---|---|---\n1 (ones) | 1 | 5 | 1+5=6 \u2192 6 (no carry)\n2 (tens) |9 |6 |9+6=15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n3 (hundreds)|4 |1 |4+1 +1(carry)=6 \u21926, no carry\n4 (thousands)|9 |6 |9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1\n5 (ten thousands)|6 |1 |6+1 +1=8 \u21928, no carry\n6 (hundred thousands)|8 |3 |8+3=11 \u21921, carryover1\n7 (millions)|4 |9 |4+9 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n8 (ten millions)|8 |9 |8+9 +1=18 \u21928, carryover1\n9 (hundred millions)|6 |9 |6+9 +1=16 \u21926, carryover1\n10 (billions)|0 |0 |1 (carryover) \u21921\n\nWait, so the total sum would be:\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):6\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition10:1\n\nSo writing the digits from position10 down to position1:\n\n1 (billions place), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands?), wait, let me arrange them properly.\n\nWait, the digits from highest to lowest place:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions place)\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:5 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition1:6 (ones)\n\nSo putting them together: 1,684,815,656?\n\nWait, let me check again:\n\nWait, the digits in each position:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,684,185,656?\n\nWait, let me write it step by step:\n\nStarting from the highest digit (position10):\n\n1 followed by the digits from position9 to position1:\n\n1 (billions), then 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,684,185,656?\n\nWait, but let me check the addition again step by step to ensure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nLet me re-add each column carefully:\n\nStarting from the right (position1):\n\n1. Ones: 1 +5 =6. Correct.\n\n2. Tens:9+6=15. Write 5, carryover1. Correct.\n\n3. Hundreds:4+1 +1=6. Correct.\n\n4. Thousands:9+6=15. Write5, carryover1. Correct.\n\n5. Ten thousands:6+1 +1=8. Wait, 6 (from first number) +1 (second number) plus the carryover 1? Wait, the carryover from thousands place was 1, so yes. 6+1+1=8. Correct.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:8 (first) +3 (second) =11. So 1, carryover1. Correct.\n\n7. Millions:4 +9 +1 (carryover) =14. So 4, carryover1. Correct.\n\n8. Ten millions:8 +9 +1 (carryover) =18. So 8, carryover1. Correct.\n\n9. Hundred millions:6 +9 +1 (carryover) =16. So 6, carryover1. Correct.\n\n10. Billions: The carryover1. So that's the new digit.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo combining these:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo written as 1,684,185,656?\n\nWait, let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:\n\n684,869,491 + 993,616,165.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 684,869,491 + 900,000,000 = 1,684,869,491\n\nThen add the remaining 93,616,165:\n\n1,684,869,491 + 93,616,165.\n\nAdding 93,616,165 to 1,684,869,491:\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1,684,869,491 + 90,000,000 = 1,774,869,491\n\nThen add 3,616,165:\n\n1,774,869,491 + 3,616,165 = ?\n\nAdding 3,000,000: 1,777,869,491\n\nThen 616,165:\n\n1,777,869,491 + 616,165 = 1,778,485,656?\n\nWait, that's conflicting with the previous result. Hmm, so there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me do the addition directly:\n\n684,869,491\n\n+993,616,165\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:1+5=6\n\nTens:9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nHundreds:4+1=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carry?\n\nWait, wait, the hundreds place in first number is 4 (from 684,869,491: the hundreds digit is the third from the right, which is 4 in 491). The second number's hundreds digit is 1 (from 616,165: the hundreds digit is 1 in 165). So 4 +1 =5 plus carryover1 makes 6. So that's correct.\n\nThousands place: first number's thousands digit is 9 (from 69491?), wait let's parse the first number again:\n\n684,869,491:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n684,869,491:\n\nThe digits are:\n\nBillions:6\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:1? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused with the commas.\n\nWait, the number is 684,869,491. Let's separate the digits:\n\n6 8 4 , 8 6 9 , 4 9 1\n\nSo:\n\nFirst part (hundred millions): 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions)\n\nSecond part (hundred thousands): 8 (hundred thousands?), wait no:\n\nWait, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n684,869,491 is:\n\n684 (million), 869 (thousand), 491.\n\nSo:\n\n684,869,491 = 6*100,000,000 + 8*10,000,000 +4*1,000,000 +8*100,000 +6*10,000 +9*1,000 +4*100 +9*10 +1*1.\n\nSo the thousands digit (position4) is 9 (from the 869, so the thousands place is the third digit from the comma in 869, which is 9). Wait, perhaps I need to list all digits:\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):9\n\nPosition1 (ones):1\n\nWait, that's conflicting with earlier breakdown. Hmm, perhaps my initial digit positions were wrong. Let me clarify:\n\nThe number 684,869,491:\n\nBreaking down each digit with positions from right (position1):\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nPosition1:1 (ones)\n\nPosition2:9 (tens)\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 993,616,165:\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:6 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\nFirst number:9, second:6 \u21929+6=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst:6, second:1 \u21926+1 +1 (carry) =8.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst:8, second:3 \u21928+3=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:4, second:9 \u21924+9 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst:8, second:9 \u21928+9 +1=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:6, second:9 \u21926+9 +1=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:6 (from hundreds place:4+1+1=6?)\n\nWait, let me recheck the hundreds place (position3):\n\nFirst number's hundreds digit is4, second's is1. Plus any carryover?\n\nThe hundreds place addition:\n\nPosition3:4 (first) +1 (second) plus any carryover from the previous (position4's carryover was 1, but that was added in position4. Wait, the carryover from position4 (thousands) is added to position5 (ten thousands). So the hundreds place (position3) doesn't have a carryover from position4. Wait, no, the carryover from position4 (thousands) is added to the next higher position (ten thousands). So the hundreds place addition is independent.\n\nWait, let me go step by step again for each position:\n\nStarting from position1 (ones):\n\n1+5=6, no carry.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n4 (first) +1 (second) +0 (no carry from previous except the carry from position2 is added here? Wait no, the carryover from position2 (tens) is added to the hundreds place?\n\nWait, no. The carryover from position2 (tens) is added to the next higher digit, which is position3 (hundreds). Wait, actually, when adding, the carryover from each digit is added to the next higher place. So:\n\nAfter adding position2 (tens), which gives 15, we write 5 and carryover1 to the hundreds place (position3).\n\nSo for position3 (hundreds):\n\n4 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover) =6. So no further carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 (first) +6 (second) =15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover) =8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8 (first) +3 (second) =11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n4 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carryover) =14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n8 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carryover) =18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n6 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carryover) =16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\n0 +0 +1 (carryover) =1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:6\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo written as 1,684,185,656.\n\nWait, but when I did the alternative addition earlier, adding 684,869,491 + 993,616,165:\n\nLet me compute this using another method:\n\n684,869,491\n\n+993,616,165\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nStart with 684,869,491 + 993,616,165.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n684,869,491 is 684 million plus 869,491.\n\n993,616,165 is 993 million plus 616,165.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 684 + 993 = 1,677 million.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 869,491 + 616,165.\n\n869,491 + 616,165:\n\n800,000 + 600,000 = 1,400,000\n\n69,491 + 16,165 = 85,656\n\nSo total of the smaller parts: 1,400,000 +85,656 = 1,485,656.\n\nWait, but 869,491 +616,165:\n\nLet me compute:\n\n869,491\n\n+616,165\n\n= (800,000 + 69,491) + (600,000 +16,165)\n\n= 1,400,000 + (69,491 +16,165)\n\n69,491 +16,165:\n\n69,491 +10,000 =79,491\n\n79,491 +6,165 =85,656. So yes, total 1,485,656.\n\nSo total sum is 1,677,000,000 +1,485,656 = 1,678,485,656?\n\nWait, but that contradicts the previous result of 1,684,185,656.\n\nHmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me do the addition step by step numerically:\n\n684,869,491\n\n+993,616,165\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  684,869,491\n\n+ 993,616,165\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:1+5=6\n\nTens:9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nHundreds:4+1=5 +1=6 \u21926, no carry\n\nThousands:9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:6+1=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry\n\nHundred thousands:8+3=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nMillions:4+9=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nTen millions:8+9=17 +1=18 \u21928, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:6+9=15 +1=16 \u21926, carryover1\n\nBillions:0+0 +1=1\n\nSo the result is 1,684,185,656.\n\nWait, but when I did the alternative method, I got 1,678,485,656. That must be an error in that method.\n\nWait, let me recalculate the alternative approach:\n\n684,869,491 + 993,616,165.\n\nBreaking into millions:\n\n684,869,491 = 684,000,000 + 869,491\n\n993,616,165 = 993,000,000 + 616,165\n\nAdding the millions parts: 684,000,000 + 993,000,000 = 1,677,000,000\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 869,491 + 616,165.\n\nWait, 869,491 + 616,165:\n\nLet me compute this again:\n\n869,491\n\n+616,165\n\nAdding units:\n\nUnits place:1+5=6\n\nTens:9+6=15 \u21925 carry1\n\nHundreds:4+1=5 +1=6\n\nThousands:9+6=15 \u21925 carry1\n\nTen thousands:6+1=7 +1=8\n\nHundred thousands:8+6=14 \u21924 carry1\n\nMillions:0+0 +1=1 (since both numbers have 0 in the millions beyond their own parts?)\n\nWait, no, actually, the numbers are 869,491 and 616,165. So:\n\n869,491 is 0.869491 million? Wait no, they are separate numbers. Wait, 869,491 is 869 thousand 491, and 616,165 is 616 thousand 165.\n\nAdding them:\n\n869,491 + 616,165:\n\n800,000 + 600,000 = 1,400,000\n\n69,491 + 16,165 = 85,656\n\nTotal: 1,400,000 +85,656 =1,485,656.\n\nSo total sum is 1,677,000,000 +1,485,656 =1,678,485,656.\n\nBut this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,684,185,656.\n\nHmm, so there must be a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check with calculator-like approach:\n\nLet me compute 684,869,491 + 993,616,165.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 684,869,491\n\nAdd 993,616,165:\n\nFirst, add 684,869,491 + 900,000,000 = 1,584,869,491\n\nThen add 93,616,165:\n\n1,584,869,491 + 93,616,165.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,584,869,491 + 90,000,000 = 1,674,869,491\n\nThen add 3,616,165:\n\n1,674,869,491 + 3,000,000 =1,677,869,491\n\nThen add 616,165:\n\n1,677,869,491 + 616,165 = ?\n\n1,677,869,491 + 600,000 =1,678,469,491\n\nThen +16,165: 1,678,469,491 +16,165 =1,678,485,656.\n\nAh! So this gives 1,678,485,656, which matches the alternative method. But the digit-by-digit addition gave 1,684,185,656. So there's a mistake in the digit-by-digit approach.\n\nWait, where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit?\n\nLet me re-examine the digit positions:\n\nFirst number: 684,869,491\n\nDigits from right (position1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:1 (ones)\n\nPosition2:9 (tens)\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:993,616,165\n\nDigits from right (position1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:6 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition1:1+5=6 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition2:9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition3:4+1 +1 (carry) =6 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition4:9+6=15 \u21925, carryover1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition5:6+1 +1 (carry) =8 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition6:8+3=11 \u21921, carryover1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition7:4+9 +1 (carry) =14 \u21924, carryover1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition8:8+9 +1 (carry) =18 \u21928, carryover1 \u2192 correct.\n\nPositi