mber is 963,134,105. Breaking it down:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n- 6 (ten millions)\n- 3 (millions)\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n- 3 (ten thousands)\n- 4 (thousands)\n- 1 (hundreds)\n- 0 (tens)\n- 5 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 821,767,453.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n- 2 (ten millions)\n- 1 (millions)\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n- 6 (ten thousands)\n- 7 (thousands)\n- 4 (hundreds)\n- 5 (tens)\n- 3 (ones)\n\nWait, so when adding the hundred millions place: 9 (from first) + 8 (from second) = 17. So that's 7 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place. Since there are no higher digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the billions place. So the total sum would be 1,784,901,558?\n\nWait, let me do this step by step again carefully to avoid mistakes.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 5 + 3 = 8\n\nTens: 0 + 5 = 5\n\nHundreds: 1 + 4 = 5\n\nThousands: 4 + 7 = 11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nTen thousands: 3 + 6 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10 \u2192 write 0, carryover 1\n\nHundred thousands: 1 + 7 = 8 + 1 = 9 \u2192 write 9, no carryover\n\nMillions: 6 + 1 = 7 \u2192 write 7\n\nTen millions: 9 (from first number's ten millions?) Wait, hold on. Wait, let me list each digit position properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the place values. Let me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number: 9 6 3 1 3 4 1 0 5\n\nSecond number:8 2 1 7 6 7 4 5 3\n\nWait, actually, let's write each digit in order from left to right, but aligned properly. Let me count the digits:\n\nFirst number: 963,134,105 has 9 digits: 9 (1st), 6 (2nd), 3 (3rd), 1 (4th), 3 (5th), 4 (6th), 1 (7th), 0 (8th), 5 (9th).\n\nSecond number: 821,767,453 also has 9 digits: 8 (1st), 2 (2nd), 1 (3rd), 7 (4th), 6 (5th), 7 (6th), 4 (7th), 5 (8th), 3 (9th).\n\nAdding digit by digit from right (the 9th digit):\n\nPosition 9 (ones): 5 + 3 = 8\n\nPosition 8 (tens): 0 +5 =5\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):1 +4=5\n\nPosition6 (thousands):4 +7=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +6=9 +1=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):1 +7=8 +1=9 \u2192 write9, no carryover\n\nPosition3 (millions):3 +1=4 \u2192 wait, hold on. Wait, the third digit from the left in first number is 3 (millions place?), but let me check:\n\nWait, actually, the positions might be better thought as:\n\nStarting from the right, each digit's place:\n\nPosition 1 (billions): first digit from the left in a 9-digit number is the hundred millions place. Wait, maybe I need to clarify the place values properly.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think of each digit's place as follows for a 9-digit number:\n\nDigit 1 (leftmost): Hundred millions place (10^8)\n\nDigit 2: Ten millions (10^7)\n\nDigit3: Millions (10^6)\n\nDigit4: Hundred thousands (10^5)\n\nDigit5: Ten thousands (10^4)\n\nDigit6: Thousands (10^3)\n\nDigit7: Hundreds (10^2)\n\nDigit8: Tens (10^1)\n\nDigit9: Ones (10^0)\n\nSo for the first number, 963,134,105:\n\nDigit1:9 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:6 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:3 (millions)\n\nDigit4:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:4 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:1 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:0 (tens)\n\nDigit9:5 (ones)\n\nSecond number, 821,767,453:\n\nDigit1:8 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:2 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:1 (millions)\n\nDigit4:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:7 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:4 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:5 (tens)\n\nDigit9:3 (ones)\n\nNow adding each digit from right to left (starting at digit9):\n\nDigit9 (ones):5+3=8\n\nDigit8 (tens):0+5=5\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):1+4=5\n\nDigit6 (thousands):4+7=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):3+6=9 +1=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):1+7=8 +1=9 \u2192 write9, no carryover\n\nDigit3 (millions):3+1=4 \u2192 write4\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):6+2=8 \u2192 write8\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):9+8=17 \u2192 write7, carryover1\n\nNow, since we have a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we need to add a new digit to the left (billions place), so the total becomes 1 followed by the digits we have so far.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions place):\n\n1 (carryover) then the digits from Digit1 to Digit9:\n\nWait, let me structure the result step by step.\n\nAfter adding all digits:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have are:\n\nDigit9:8\n\nDigit8:5\n\nDigit7:5\n\nDigit6:1 (with a carryover 1)\n\nDigit5:0 (with carryover1)\n\nDigit4:9\n\nDigit3:4\n\nDigit2:8\n\nDigit1:7 (from 9+8=17, but with a carryover 1 to the next higher place)\n\nWait, actually, when adding the hundred millions place (Digit1):\n\n9 (from first number) +8 (second number) =17. So we write down 7 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. Since there's no higher digit, the billions place is 1. So the total digits are:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:8\n\nSo putting all together from left to right:\n\n1 (billions), 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, let me check each digit position again:\n\nAfter the addition steps:\n\n- The carryover from the hundred millions addition (Digit1) is 1, so that's the billions digit.\n\nThen the digits from each position:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:7 (from 9+8=17, so 7 here)\n\nTen millions:6 (from first number) +2 (second) =8 \u2192 no carryover here, so 8.\n\nMillions:3 (first) +1 (second)=4 \u2192 4.\n\nHundred thousands:1 (first) +7 (second)=8, plus carryover 1 from thousands place? Wait, no, the carryover from the ten thousands place was handled earlier.\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nWait, the addition steps:\n\nStarting from the right (Digit9 to Digit1):\n\nDigit9:8\n\nDigit8:5\n\nDigit7:5\n\nDigit6:1 (with carryover 1 to next digit)\n\n